var DOCS = {"cil-deck-1": {"course": "CIL", "ref": "Slide 1.1", "title": "Introduction", "kicker": "Lecture deck · Dr Ihuoma K. Ilobinso · 14 slides", "words": 1207, "parts": [{"label": "Law, courts and the sources of Nigerian contract law", "note": "", "src": "1.1 introduction.pptx.pdf", "html": "<h3 id=\"cil-deck-1-0\">Introduction<\/h3><p id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-1\"><b>What is Law?<\/b> ● Law is a body of rules established to regulate the actions of people. <b>What is Contract Law?<\/b> The body of rules which governs/regulate enforceable agreements. It enforces promises between parties in the sell/exchange of goods or services. <b>Function of Contract Law<\/b><\/p><ol><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-2\">Law and Order- to ensure orderliness in the dealings between parties<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-3\">Justice- to promote justice in contractual relationships<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-4\">Remedies- provide adequate remedies where parties have suffered loss<\/li><\/ol><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-5\">Legal protection- to protect certain classes of parties. Consumers,<\/li><\/ul><ol start=\"4\"><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-6\">unsound mind etc.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-7\">To promote trade and commerce-<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"cil-deck-1-1\">Classification of Law<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-8\">Generally, there are different areas of law. E.g. criminal law, electoral law, Tort, etc.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-9\">Law can be broadly classified into Public Law and Private Law <b>Public Law<\/b> - it governs relationships between government and<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-10\">the public. E.g. Constitutional Law, electoral law, criminal law, Administrative Law, Law of Evidence <b>Private Law<\/b> - Regulates interactions among private citizens<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-11\">E.g. Contract Law, Banking Law, Land Law, etc.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-1-2\">It can also be classified in Criminal Law and Civil Law<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-12\"><b>Criminal Law<\/b> - deals with prohibition and punishment of<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-13\">criminal conducts. Crime /offence must be expressly provided for in a statute and punishment prescribed. Punishment- Imprisonment or Fine<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-14\"><b>Civil Law<\/b> - aspect of law that is not criminal Punishment - monetary compensation, fines, community service, etc.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-1-3\">Court Systems<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-15\">Supreme Court<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-16\">Court of Appeal<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-17\">Federal High Court<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-18\">State High Courts Magistrate Court<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-19\">Customary/Area Courts<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-20\">Sharia Court<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-21\">Courts of special jurisdiction<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-22\">Industrial Court<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-23\">Coroners court<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-24\">Juvenile court<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-1-4\">Sources of Contract Law<\/h3><ol><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-25\">The Received English Law- colonial link to<\/b> <b>Britain.<\/b> The received English law can be divided into 3 parts: - <b>Common law<\/b> <b>Doctrines of equity<\/b> <b>Statutes of general application<\/b> - Laws passed by the british Parliament before Ist January 1900<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-26\">Case Law<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-27\">Nigerian Legislations/Statutory Law<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-28\">Customary law<\/li><\/ol><p id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-29\"><b>• Sources of Contract Law-<\/b> where can we find these laws <b>Nigerian Statutory Law-<\/b> 1999 Constitution, Sale of Goods Law of Lagos<\/p><ol><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-30\"><\/b> State, Childs Right Act, Illiterate Protection Act, Criminal Code, Electoral Act, <b>Customary law<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-31\"><\/b> <b>The Received English Law-<\/b> When the British colonised us, they came with<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-32\"><\/b> their business practices and laws. Some of these laws are still applicable. Therefore reference will be made at intervals to English authorities. SoG Act 1893- The received English law can be divided into 3 parts: <b>Common law<\/b> (judge-made law, e.g. Carlill v Carbolic Smoke Ball) - <b>Case Law<\/b> (Superior a. Courts in Nigeria and Common Law) <b>Doctrines of equity (<\/b>was developed to mitigate the hardship caused by the<\/li><\/ol><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-33\">b. strict application of common law. Based on justice and fairness) and <b>Statutes of general application<\/b> (law passed by the British Parliament c. before Jan 1, 1900 and applies to Nigeria directly as a received English statute of general application. e.g. the Sale of Goods Act 1893. However some States now have their own SOGA which is quite similar to the English counterpart (Western region- Osun, Ogun, Oyo, Ondo, Ekiti), Lagos, Bende (Edo and Delta).<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-34\"><b>What is a contract?<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-35\">A contract is an <b>agreement<\/b> made between two or more parties, which creates <b>legally binding rights and obligations<\/b>.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-36\"><b>• Agreement-<\/b> Contract must be <b>consensual<\/b> and <b>voluntary<\/b>, in other words the parties must agree, else no contract exists. Agreement can either express or implied.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-37\">The law of contract is not interested in regulating bargains by parties. <b>Parties are free to make good or bad bargains<\/b>- Freedom of contract.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-38\">Rather, it is interested in enforcing promises/bargains that have been made.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-39\">This suggests that not all agreements are <b>legally<\/b> <b>binding<\/b> or <b>enforceable-<\/b> by the court.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-40\">E.g. Gratuitous agreements between friends &amp; family, illegal contracts, defective consent – duress, mistake, misrepresentation, etc.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-41\">We enter into contracts almost everyday.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-1-5\">Freedom of contract<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-42\">The notion postulates that parties are free negotiate, bargain and<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-43\">agree on their rights and duties in a contract <b>without<\/b> <b>interference<\/b> by<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-44\">the state/law- this is a characteristic of a modern democratic society.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-45\">The law of contract is not interested in regulating bargains by parties.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-46\">Parties are free to make good or bad bargains.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-47\">Rather, it is interested in enforcing promises/bargains made – <i><b>Caveat<\/b><\/i><\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-48\"><i><b>Emptor<\/b><\/i> (let the buyer beware)<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-49\">However, this position of the law began to change in the 20th and 21st<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-50\">century, with the increase/emergence of big companies that<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-51\">produce goods in mass and sell to buyers on a <b>take-it-or-leave<\/b> it<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-52\">basis, without much negotiation- Imbalance of bargaining power.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-53\">The law now intervenes in certain situations where there is no real<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-54\">bargain between the parties, it forces certain conditions to apply to<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-55\">certain transactions regardless of what the parties intend e.g.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-56\">Contracts by consumer, infants/minors, illiterates, intoxicated persons,<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-57\">persons of unsound mind.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-1-6\">Classification of Contracts<\/h3><p id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-58\">There are different ways of classifying contracts <b>Simple Contracts &amp; Contracts Under Seal<\/b><\/p><ol><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-59\"><\/b> <b>Express &amp; Implied Contracts<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-60\"><\/b> <b>Bilateral &amp; Unilateral Contracts<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-61\"><\/b> <b>Executed &amp; Executory Contracts<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-62\"><\/b><\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"cil-deck-1-7\">Simple Contracts &amp; Contracts Under Seal<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-63\"><b>• Contracts under Seal<\/b> are the very formal contracts, they are written and have the seal of the signer attached to the document.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-64\"><b>• A seal<\/b> is an impression or mark/adhesive placed on a contract document. It <b>symbolises authenticity<\/b>.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-65\">The seal is of great significance in law. In Nigeria (common law) contracts which are sealed were treated differently from contracts without seal.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-66\">Contracts under seal are generally referred to as <b>deeds<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-67\">The law requires that certain contracts must be by deed. E.g. contracts relating to land.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-68\"><b>Simple Contracts<\/b> are contracts made <b>orally or in writing<\/b>, but not under seal. Simple contracts require consideration (something given in return for a promise) and other elements to be valid.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-1-8\">Express and Implied Contracts<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-69\">There is a misconception that contracts must be in a<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-70\">specific form, maybe written, signed and sealed.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-71\">However, most contracts do not need to be in<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-72\">writing.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-73\"><b>Express Contracts<\/b>: A contract is said to be express when it is negotiated by the parties and the intentions of the parties are <b>clearly stated in words<\/b> <b>-<\/b> orally or in writing.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-74\"><b>Implied<\/b> <b>Contracts<\/b> are contracts which the intentions/obligations are not stated in words (oral or written). They are <b>inferred from the conduct<\/b> of the parties.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-1-9\">Bilateral and Unilateral Contracts<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-75\">In <b>bilateral<\/b> <b>contracts<\/b>, there is an exchange of promise by the parties. The parties reach an agreement and each party undertakes an obligation. E.g. you promise to manufacture or repair my car, manage my estate or survey my land and I promise to pay you 500k. All parties are involved from the start.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-76\"><b>Unilateral<\/b> <b>Contracts- e.g. reward contracts<\/b> is the type of contract where, at the beginning, only a party promises to do something, while the other party is free to choose to contract or not. Unlike bilateral contracts, there is no agreement between the parties at the initial stage, only one party is bound to do anything. E.g. adverts, buy 1 Pizza and get another one free. Toyota’s advert to buy 2 tyres and get ₦5,000 fuel. <b>Carlill<\/b> <b>v Carbolic Smoke Ball<\/b> [1895] 1 QB 256.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-1-10\">Executed and Executory Contracts<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-77\">In some cases performance of the contract happens at the same time it is formed. E.g. when buying a pen. However, this is not always the case.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-78\"><b>Executed Contract-<\/b> contract where both parties have performed their promises/obligations<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-79\">E.g. purchase from a store<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-1-p0-80\"><b>Executory Contracts-<\/b> is one which is wholly or partly unperformed. Performance to be done in the future.<\/li><\/ul>", "toc": [{"id": "cil-deck-1-0", "t": "Introduction", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-1-1", "t": "Classification of Law", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-1-2", "t": "It can also be classified in Criminal Law and Civil Law", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-1-3", "t": "Court Systems", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-1-4", "t": "Sources of Contract Law", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-1-5", "t": "Freedom of contract", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-1-6", "t": "Classification of Contracts", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-1-7", "t": "Simple Contracts & Contracts Under Seal", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-1-8", "t": "Express and Implied Contracts", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-1-9", "t": "Bilateral and Unilateral Contracts", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-1-10", "t": "Executed and Executory Contracts", "lv": 3}]}]}, "cil-deck-2": {"course": "CIL", "ref": "Slide 1.2", "title": "Formation of Contract", "kicker": "Lecture deck · Dr Ihuoma Ilobinso · 25 slides", "words": 2255, "parts": [{"label": "Offer, acceptance, consideration and intention", "note": "", "src": "1.2 Engineering Contract _FORMATION OF CONTRACT.pptx.pdf", "html": "<h3 id=\"cil-deck-2-0\">Elements of a Valid Contract<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-1\">INTENTIONS TO<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-2\">OFFER ACCEPTANCE CONSIDERATION CONTRACT<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-2-1\"><i>Consensus<\/i> <i>ad idem.<\/i><\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-3\">The <b>process of negotiation<\/b> to reach an agreement is expressed in the form of offer and, acceptance.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-4\">Therefore, to establish that there is an agreement between the parties, the facts and circumstances surrounding the agreement have to be examined. It must be shown that an offer was made by one party (called the <b>offeror<\/b>) and accepted by another party (called the <b>offeree<\/b>).<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-2-2\">OFFER<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-5\">An offer is a <b>proposal<\/b> indicating one's willingness to enter into a contract with the person to whom it is addressed.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-6\">An offer can be made to a <b>specific person<\/b> or to the <b>general<\/b> <b>public<\/b>.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-7\">When it is made to a specific person or group of persons, it is only capable of acceptance by the specific person or a member of the group.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-8\">When it is made to the public, the offeror indicates willing to contract with any member of the public who accepts it. E.g. a reward offer- See <i>Carlill v Carbolic Smoke Ball<\/i>.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-9\">An offer can be made <b>orally<\/b>, in <b>writing<\/b> or by <b>conduct<\/b><\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-2-3\">Offer/Invitation to Treat<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-10\">For a proposition/indication of interest to be considered an offer, it must be<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-11\"><b>clear<\/b>, <b>complete<\/b> and <b>definite<\/b>.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-12\">The offeror must intend to be bound without further negotiation.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-13\">Once the court can detect or imply a <b>lack<\/b> <b>of intention<\/b> to be bound without further negotiation, it will hold that the indication of interest is not an offer but an invitation to treat (invitation to make an offer).<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-14\">The use of the word ‘offer’ does not automatically mean that the proposition was an offer. What is important is the intentions of the party which must be outwardly shown.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-15\"><b>Example<\/b>: An estate agent writes a prospective client saying ‘I offer my services to you, to manage your property in VGC for 15% of rent accrued’, the client replies, ‘I accept your offer, but for 10% of rent accrued. When was the offer made and accepted?<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-16\">Due to the difficulty in determining whether a proposition is an offer or an invitation to treat, the following cases will be used to aid the distinction.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-2-4\">Display of goods in shops<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-17\">The display of goods on the shelf or in shop windows with the price indicated, <b>is a<\/b> <b>mere invitation to treat<\/b>, and not an offer.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-18\">The buyer makes an offer when he picks up the goods and takes it to the seller.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-2-5\">Advertisements in Catalogues and Newspapers<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-19\"><b>Advertisement<\/b> of goods for sale in catalogues, Newspapers, etc. is regarded as <b>a mere invitation to treat<\/b>. Except it is otherwise indicated.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-20\">However, there are times/circumstances when advertisements are framed in such specific terms that they can be construed as an offer.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-21\">Where the advert makes promises in return for an act. E.g. reward for buying a product or for finding a lost property. <i>Carlill v Carbolic Smoke Ball<\/i>.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-22\">In these circumstances, no further act of acceptance is contemplated<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-23\"><b>Auction/bids<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-24\">An <b>advertisement<\/b> stating that an auction will hold is not an offer, but <b>an invitation to treat.<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-25\">The auctioneers <b>invitation<\/b> for bids is also not an offer.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-26\">Each bid constitutes an offer which can be accepted or rejected by the auctioneer. Acceptance occurs only when the auctioneer's hammer falls. Before then, the bidder can withdraw his bid/offer.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-2-6\">Public transportation<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-27\">On the question of when a contract is formed in public transportation, whether at the time of issuing a travel timetable or at the time of boarding. Standing at the bus stop?<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-28\">A transport timetable or itinerary does not constitute an offer, but an invitation to treat the<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-2-7\">Sale of Shares<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-29\">An advertisement inviting the public to purchase shares in a company is not an offer but an invitation to treat, although it is usually called an offer of shares.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-30\">A member of the public makes the offer when he applies for the shares and the company may or may not accept the offer.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-2-8\">Tender<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-31\">A request for tender (where a tenderer is invited to state the lowest he can use to execute a contract) is not an offer, but an invitation to treat.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-32\">However, if the invitation explicitly states that the lowest or highest bid will be accepted, then the invitation constitutes an offer.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-33\"><b>SUPPLY OF INFORMATION<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-34\">A mere supply of information, e.g. price, does not constitute an offer.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-35\">See <i><b>Harvey v Facey<\/b><\/i> (1893) AC 552- the plaintiff requested for<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-36\">the price of some goods ‘bumper hall pen’, the defendant<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-37\">replied 900 pounds, the plaintiff sent another telegraph<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-38\">accepting to buy the it. They sued when the defendant refused<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-39\">to sell it.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-2-9\">Acceptance<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-40\">Acceptance is the <b>final<\/b> and <b>unqualified assent<\/b> to the terms of an <b>offer<\/b>.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-41\">In other words, the offeree <b>must agree with all the terms<\/b> of the offer. He is not allowed to change the terms of the contract or to incorporate new ones.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-42\">Any modification or addition to the terms of the offer will <b>amount<\/b> to a counter offer and not an acceptance.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-43\">A <b>counter offer<\/b> constitutes an <b>invalid acceptance<\/b> but is a <b>valid offer<\/b> which may be accepted by the other party. E.g...<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-44\">It is important to ascertain when an offer was made and when it was accepted because, once the offer is validly accepted, the contract becomes binding on both parties.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-45\">They become obligated to perform their promises.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-46\">However, silence or inactivity does not amount to acceptance.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-47\">A statement which merely acknowledges receipt of the offer or which states the intentions of the offeree to accept the offer does not amount to acceptance.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-48\">Request for further information does not invalidate an offer or acceptance.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-49\">Conditional acceptance- An acceptance that is subject to the performance of a condition, does not constitute a valid acceptance until the condition is fulfilled.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-2-10\">Mode of acceptance<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-50\">Where the offeror prescribes a particular mode by which acceptance may be communicated,<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-51\">(e.g. must be in writing, via email) then the prescribed mode<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-52\">must be followed else the acceptance is invalid. E.g. <b>Afolabi v.<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-53\"><b>Polymera Industries Ltd<\/b>. The offeror specified that the offer<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-54\">should be accepted by signing the attached agreement and<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-55\">returning at the earliest possible time.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-56\">Where no particular method of acceptance is prescribed, the general rule is that acceptance may be given by the same mode of communication or by a method equally as fast as the method used in making the offer.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-2-11\">Communication of acceptance<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-57\">The <b>general<\/b> <b>rule<\/b> is that, acceptance becomes effective only when it has been <b>communicated/received<\/b> by the offeror.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-58\">Until an acceptance is received by the offeror, the offer may be revoked.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-59\">The main rationale for this rule is that it could cause hardship to the offeror if he is to be bound without <b>knowing<\/b> that his offer has been accepted.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-60\"><b>Exceptions to this general rule-<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-61\"><b>• Acceptance by Post-<\/b> where acceptance is to be communicated to the offeror via the postal system or telegram, then acceptance becomes effective when posted, rather than when it is received the offeror.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-62\"><i><b>Adams v Lindsay<\/b><\/i> <i>–<\/i> offer for the sale of wool was received on sept. 5,<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-63\">acceptance posted same day but received on sept. 9, defendant sold the<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-64\">wool to someone else on the sept. 8. it was held that the contract came<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-65\">into existence on sept. 5.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-2-12\">Termination of Offer<\/h3><p id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-66\"><b>An offer may be terminated<\/b> before acceptance by any of these following ways: <b>Revocation/withdrawal-<\/b> The offeror can revoke or withdraw his offer before acceptance. Acceptance makes the offer irrevocable. The postal rule does not apply to communication of revocation. <b>Rejection-<\/b> Where the offeree rejects the offer e.g. counter-offer <b>Lapse of time-<\/b> where the offeror imposes a time limit and the offeree did not accept the offer within the time specified. <b>Failure of a condition-<\/b> where the offer is conditional and the condition was not met <b>Death<\/b> of the <b>offeree or offeror-<\/b> the person to whom the offer is made is the only one capable of accepting the offer. <b>Death<\/b> of the <b>offeror-<\/b> where the offeree has notice of the offerors death before acceptance, the offer is automatically revoked. However, if the offer was accepted before knowledge of the offerors death, termination of the offer will be dependent on the nature of the contract. If the contract is one that can still be performed<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-deck-2-13\">Consideration<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-67\">For a <b>simple<\/b> contract to be enforceable, there must be consideration. In other words, each party must do or promise to do something in exchange for what is gained from the other party<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-68\">Consideration is a promise by the promissor to give something of value to the promisee in exchange for a thing of value.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-69\"><i><b>Currie v Misa-<\/b><\/i> Consideration – right, interest, profit, benefit, or forbearance, detriment, loss, responsibility’<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-70\">A promise not supported by consideration is a gratuitous promise. Not enforceable. Awoof is not a contract, unless it is under seal.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-71\">So if you seek to enforce someone else’s promise to you, you must prove that you gave something in return for that promise.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-72\">Each party must <b>receive a benefit<\/b> and <b>suffer a<\/b> <b>detriment/loss<\/b>. This benefit and detriment is referred to as consideration.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-73\">Consideration can be a thing or a service. It does not have to be <b>money<\/b>.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-74\">Most contracts involves 2 promises, each is both a promissor and a promisee.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-75\"><b>E.g<\/b>. For instance, where A simply promises to sweep the class- it is not enforceable unless it is under seal Where <b>A<\/b> promises to sweep the class in exchange for a loaf of bread- contract<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-2-14\">Rules Governing Consideration<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-76\"><b>Consideration must have some value but need not<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-77\"><b>1.<\/b> <b>be adequate.<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-78\">The consideration must have <b>a real or economic value<\/b>, but need not be adequate.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-79\">It should not just be an emotional or sentimental value. E.g. A promise by a son to his father to be more responsible in exchange for a new car.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-80\">The court will not usually interfere with the bargains of the contracting parties. Parties are allowed to make whatever bargains that they want. The court is not concerned with whether the considerations are of fair or unfair value, it should just be something that a value can be placed on.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-81\">e.g. a plate of food in exchange for an iPhone.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-2-15\">2. Consideration must move from the promisee<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-82\">Most contracts involves 2 promises, each is both a promissor and a promisee.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-83\">Any one who does not furnish consideration cannot bring an action to enforce the contract. E.g. in a contract made by A and B for the benefit of C, C cannot bring an action to enforce the contract because he did not furnish consideration.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-2-16\">3. Consideration may be Executory or executed, but not past.<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-84\">Consideration is <b>Executory<\/b> where both parties <b>exchange promises<\/b> to perform an act in the future e.g. a promise to supply a bag of rice in exchange for payment.- bilateral contracts.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-85\">Consideration is <b>executed<\/b> where a party <b>promises<\/b> to do something <b>in<\/b> <b>return<\/b> for the <b>performance of an act,<\/b> rather than for the mere promise of the future performance of an act.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-86\"><b>Consideration to perform an existing obligation is past<\/b> consideration, therefore not valid. where a party performs the act before the other party makes a promise.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-87\"><b>Exceptions<\/b> to the rule that past consideration is no consideration is where<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-88\">a service is rendered at the request of the promissor with the understanding<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-89\">that the act will be paid for. Example, where you ask a taxi driver to take<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-90\">you to your hostel. Request for an Uber.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-2-17\">4. Consideration must not be illegal<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-91\">The courts will not allow Illegal or immoral considerations to succeed.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-92\"><b>5. A promise to fulfil an existing obligation is not consideration<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-93\">Where a person performs or promises to perform an act which he is already<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-94\">obliged to perform, he is not contributing anything to the bargain.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-95\">But if he does more than he is bound to do, that is consideration.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-96\">For instance, consideration is not sufficient, where there is a:<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-97\"><b>A public duty –<\/b> hiring a LASMA officer to direct traffic<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-98\"><b>A duty imposed by contract with the promisor-<\/b> where the promisee is bound to perform the duty by an earlier contract. <i>Stilk v Myrick<\/i>- ship/sailors<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-99\"><b>A duty imposed by contract with third party-<\/b> where the promisee performs an act which he is already bound to perform by another contract.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-2-18\">Intention to Create legal relations<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-100\">In addition to offer, acceptance and consideration, there is a 4th element- intention to contract.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-101\">For the contract to be valid, the parties must have the intention to create legal relations.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-102\">However, to determine whether the parties intended to create legal relations or not, the court will not usually ask the parties whether or not they intended to contract.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-103\">Rather, it will employ an objective test. It will consider whether an onlooker/officious bystander /reasonable man will assume, from the facts of the case, whether or not the parties intended to contract.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-104\">In order to determine whether intention to contract exists or not, the courts classify agreements into <b>social or domestic<\/b> <b>agreements<\/b> and <b>Commercial agreements.<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-105\">where the agreement is of a <b>Social and Domestic nature,<\/b> the court generally presumes that the parties did not have the intention to be bound e.g. agreement between husbands/wives, parents/child, friends. However, the courts presumption can be rebutted/changed if there is evidence that the parties intended to be bound.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-106\"><b>Commercial Agreements-<\/b> it is generally presumed that parties in commercial agreements intend to enter into a legally binding contract. It is therefore not necessary to prove further intentions.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-2-p0-107\">This presumption can be rebutted/changed if there is evidence that the parties did not intend to be bound.<\/li><\/ul>", "toc": [{"id": "cil-deck-2-0", "t": "Elements of a Valid Contract", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-2-1", "t": "Consensus ad idem.", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-2-2", "t": "OFFER", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-2-3", "t": "Offer/Invitation to Treat", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-2-4", "t": "Display of goods in shops", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-2-5", "t": "Advertisements in Catalogues and Newspapers", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-2-6", "t": "Public transportation", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-2-7", "t": "Sale of Shares", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-2-8", "t": "Tender", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-2-9", "t": "Acceptance", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-2-10", "t": "Mode of acceptance", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-2-11", "t": "Communication of acceptance", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-2-12", "t": "Termination of Offer", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-2-13", "t": "Consideration", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-2-14", "t": "Rules Governing Consideration", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-2-15", "t": "2. Consideration must move from the promisee", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-2-16", "t": "3. Consideration may be Executory or executed, but not past.", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-2-17", "t": "4. Consideration must not be illegal", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-2-18", "t": "Intention to Create legal relations", "lv": 3}]}]}, "cil-deck-3": {"course": "CIL", "ref": "Slide 1.3", "title": "Terms of Contract", "kicker": "Lecture deck · Dr Ihuoma Ilobinso · 17 slides", "words": 956, "parts": [{"label": "Conditions, warranties, innominate terms and exclusion clauses", "note": "", "src": "1.3 Engineering Contract Terms .pptx.pdf", "html": "<h3 id=\"cil-deck-3-0\">Introduction<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-1\"><b>Terms of contract<\/b> refer to the <b>legally binding<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-2\"><b>statements/promises<\/b> made by parties to the contract which give rise to <b>rights and obligations.<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-3\">when a contract is being negotiated, various statements are made.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-4\">Disputes may arise with respect to whether the parties intended that some of the statements made during the negotiation are to be legally binding or not.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-5\">Not all statements are legally binding. Some of these statements become part of a legally binding contract as <b>terms<\/b>. Some are not considered to be part of the contract and are merely <b>representations<\/b>.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-3-1\">Terms and Representation<\/h3><p id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-6\">The courts have developed tests for determining whether a statement is a term or<\/p><p id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-7\">representation. <b>Stage of contract:<\/b> at what stage of the contract was the statement made?<\/p><ol><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-8\"><\/b> early stage = representation Later stage = term. <b>Agreements reduced to writing<\/b>: was the oral statement reduced to writing, any<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-9\"><\/b> statement that is not included in the final contractual document is likely to be a representation. <b>Special knowledge and Skill<\/b>:<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-10\"><\/b> statement by a party who has expert knowledge and skill = term statement is made by a layman =representation. <b>Importance of the statement<\/b>: if the party making the statement indicates to the<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-11\"><\/b> knowledge of the other party that the statement is of great importance, that they won't have entered into the contract if the statement had not been made.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"cil-deck-3-2\">Categories of contractual terms<\/h3><p id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-12\">Terms (whether implied or express) are not all of<\/p><p id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-13\">equal importance. Conditions<\/p><ol><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-14\">Warranties<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-15\">Intermediate or innominate terms<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-16\"><\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"cil-deck-3-3\">Conditions<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-17\">These are terms of <b>great importance<\/b>. They constitute the main purpose of the contract, and failure to comply with the terms is equivalent to not performing the contract.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-18\">breach of a condition will entitle the injured party to <b>repudiate/terminate<\/b> the contract and seek damages.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-19\">Warranties<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-20\"><b>warranty<\/b> is a relatively <b>minor<\/b> <b>term<\/b> in a contract.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-21\">breached may give the injured party the <b>right to claim damages<\/b> but <b>not to<\/b> <b>reject the goods and terminate the<\/b> <b>contract<\/b>.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-3-4\">Innominate or intermediate terms<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-22\">There are cases where it might not be so easy to classify the term as either a condition or a warranty.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-23\">The courts developed this category as a <b>hybrid<\/b> between conditions and warranty.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-24\">a breach of intermediate terms <b>could either lead repudiation<\/b> or <b>damages<\/b> depending on the seriousness of the breach on the contract.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-25\">If the breach is so serious to deprive the party of <b>substantially<\/b> the whole benefits of the contract, the contract may be repudiated.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-26\">to determine when a party has a right repudiate the contract or receive damages, the <b>'substantial<\/b> <b>benefit<\/b>' test will be applied.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-3-5\">Types of Terms<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-27\">There are two main types of terms:<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-28\"><b>express<\/b> and <b>implied<\/b> terms <b>Express terms<\/b> Express terms are those terms that have<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-29\">been precisely agreed upon by the parties. They may be <b>written or oral.<\/b><\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-3-6\">Implied Terms<\/h3><p id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-30\">These are terms which are deemed to form part of the contract even though they may not have been specifically stated or agreed by the parties.<\/p><p id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-31\">Implied terms can be classified into:<\/p><ol><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-32\">terms implied by courts<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-33\">terms implied by custom or usage<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-34\">terms implied by statute<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"cil-deck-3-7\">Terms Implied by Courts<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-35\">these are terms which are not expressly set out in the contract but which are read into the contract by the court on the grounds that the parties' would have expressly included them into their contract if they had thought about it.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-36\">The court is usually very reluctant to imply terms into contracts. However, when considering whether to imply a term or not, the court adopts 2 tests: - Officious Bystander Test - Business Efficacy Test<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-3-8\">Terms implied by custom or usage<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-37\">terms may be implied by custom or trade usage.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-38\">Custom will not be implied where it has been<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-39\"><b>excluded by the parties (<\/b>expressly or impliedly).<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-3-9\">Terms implied by statute/law<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-40\">Terms read into the contract by the statute/law For instance, the Federal Competition and Consumer Protection Act and the Sale of Goods Act <b>Sale of Goods Act<\/b> - Sec 12-15 -Implied condition as to title -Implied warranty as to title -Sale by Sample (condition) -Sale by Description (condition) -Implied Condition as to fitness and quality<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-41\">1.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-deck-3-10\">Implied Terms<\/h3><p id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-42\">Section 12 implies that the seller has the legal right<\/p><p id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-43\">to sell the goods. In other words, the seller must<\/p><p id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-44\">either own the goods or have authority to transfer<\/p><p id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-45\">ownership. It also implies that:<\/p><ol><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-46\">The buyer will enjoy <b>quiet possession<\/b> of the goods.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-47\">The goods are free from undisclosed charges or encumbrances (e.g., liens, mortgages, pledges)<\/li><\/ol><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-48\"><b>Section 13 - Sale by Description<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-49\">Where goods are sold <b>by description<\/b>, there is<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-50\">an implied condition that the goods will<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-51\">correspond exactly with that description.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-52\">A sale may still be &quot;by description&quot; even if the<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-53\">buyer has seen the goods.<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-54\"><b>Section 14 - Implied Terms as to Quality and Fitness for Purpose<\/b><\/p><p id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-55\">The general rule is <b>caveat emptor<\/b> (&quot;let the buyer beware&quot;). However, where the seller<\/p><p id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-56\">sells goods in the course of business, the law implies two important conditions.<\/p><p id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-57\"><b>(a) Merchantable/Satisfactory Quality<\/b><\/p><p id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-58\">The goods must be of satisfactory quality, meaning a reasonable person would regard<\/p><p id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-59\">them as acceptable considering their description, price, and other relevant<\/p><p id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-60\">circumstances.<\/p><p id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-61\"><b>(b) Fitness for Purpose<\/b><\/p><p id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-62\">Where:<\/p><ol><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-63\">The buyer makes known the purpose for which the goods are required; and<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-64\">Relies on the seller's skill or judgment,<\/li><\/ol><ul><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-65\">there is an implied condition that the goods will be reasonably fit for that purpose.<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-66\"><b>Section 15 - Sale by Sample<\/b><\/p><p id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-67\">Where goods are sold by reference to a sample, three<\/p><p id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-68\">conditions are implied:<\/p><ol><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-69\">The bulk of the goods must correspond with the sample in quality.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-70\">The buyer must have a reasonable opportunity to compare the bulk with the sample.<\/li><li id=\"cil-deck-3-p0-71\">The goods must be free from latent defects that would not be apparent upon reasonable examination of the sample.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"cil-deck-3-11\">1.<\/h3>", "toc": [{"id": "cil-deck-3-0", "t": "Introduction", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-3-1", "t": "Terms and Representation", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-3-2", "t": "Categories of contractual terms", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-3-3", "t": "Conditions", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-3-4", "t": "Innominate or intermediate terms", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-3-5", "t": "Types of Terms", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-3-6", "t": "Implied Terms", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-3-7", "t": "Terms Implied by Courts", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-3-8", "t": "Terms implied by custom or usage", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-3-9", "t": "Terms implied by statute/law", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-3-10", "t": "Implied Terms", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-deck-3-11", "t": "1.", "lv": 3}]}]}, "cil-class-1": {"course": "CIL", "ref": "Class 1", "title": "Introduction to Nigerian Law & Contract Formation", "kicker": "Class notes · 14 May 2025", "words": 4021, "parts": [{"label": "", "note": "", "src": "Class 1 - CIL_Law_of_Engineering_Contract_Notes.pdf", "html": "<h2 id=\"cil-class-1-0\">1. Civil Law — An Overview<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-1\">Civil law are the laws that are not criminal in nature — for instance, contract law. It is not criminal. So if you breach your contract, you should not be arrested for it, except for the criminal aspect of it. Maybe you have defrauded somebody. Except where it can be proven that there is fraud — then the police can get involved — but usually, if fraud cannot be proven, obtaining by false pretences, you should actually not go to the police. You should go to contact the court to resolve it.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-1-1\">Court System in Nigeria<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-2\">We respect the court systems in Nigeria. There are different courts:<\/p><ul><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-3\">Supreme Court — the highest court in Nigeria<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-4\">Court of Appeal — comes immediately after the Supreme Court<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-5\">Federal High Courts<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-6\">State High Courts<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-7\">Magistrate Courts<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-8\">Customary Courts<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-9\">Sharia Courts<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-10\">There are also specialised courts like the National Industrial Court, which deals with specific areas of human interactions or specific areas of the law — for instance, employment matters. Like the Coroner's Court, which has to do with inquiring into how somebody died, especially when a child is involved. And there are so many other special courts.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-11\">In law there is a hierarchy where we respect persons and the courts. The highest court in Nigeria is the Supreme Court, followed by the Court of Appeal, then the Federal High Courts, then the State High Courts, then Magistrate Courts, then Customary Courts and Sharia Courts.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-1-2\">2. Sources of Law in Nigeria<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-12\">When we talk about sources of law, what does that mean? We are talking about: where does the law come from? There are different sources.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-1-3\">A. Received English Law<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-13\">The law that we received from our Colonial Masters — 'Received English Law.' When the British came to colonise us and all the other people they colonised, they came with a lot of things — they came with schools, education, and they also came with their law. And so we still apply contract law. These laws still apply to us in Nigeria even up till today, simply because we were colonised by the British.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-14\">Some of those laws still apply. Now in Nigeria, probably not — most of those laws have been changed. But we still apply some from the 1800s. The received English law is divided into three:<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-1-4\">i. Common Law<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-15\">These are laws that are not specifically in any book, in any statutes, or in any written form. You can find them in law reports where the court is making pronouncements or making decisions — so they are referred to as judgment law. They are the primary source of law in contract law.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-16\">These are laws that have been made by judges over time. Their decisions have been made by those judges over time and have become the law. For instance, it has been found that superior courts have always held that an agreement is not a contract if there is no money that has been exchanged.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-17\"><b>Rule of Precedent / Judicial Precedence:<\/b> When a superior court makes a decision, it must be followed by the lower ones, as long as the facts are the same or very similar. The lower courts are not allowed to go outside what the superior court has said. Common law simply means those decisions that have been made over time. They have been repeated by lower courts and are now considered the law. Contract law is one of the areas of law where we have a lot of common law.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-1-5\">ii. Doctrines of Equity<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-18\">They are like principles. 'He that comes to equity must come with clean hands' — has everyone heard that? There are different principles like that. They were established to be able to alleviate the hardship of common law. Common law is black and white.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-19\">Before equity was developed, it was only common law. [An example from the Merchant of Venice:] somebody said he was owed money and the agreement was: if you don't pay me, I will cut your hand. It went to court and the judge was ready to cut off his hand because he didn't pay. That is what common law does — common law says: 'This is the law and you must follow the law.' There is no question of equity. Is it going to be just? Are you going to be doing justice if you make this decision or not? So eventually, the court of equity was developed and established to be able to alleviate the hardship of common law.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-20\">They developed certain doctrines that should apply to us, which allow the courts to do justice — not just to apply the rules. In that case, the lawyer argued: the contract says that you can cut off his hand if he does not pay, but the contract does not say anything about spilling blood. So if you can cut off his hand without spilling his blood, then you can go ahead.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-21\">That is exactly what equity tries to do. It looks at: is it going to be just if we cut off his hand? How is he going to even pay eventually? We are probably going to be depriving him — or some other people — of a means of earning because of his hand. There is going to be blood everywhere. So that is what equity does — it tries to reduce the harshness caused by common law. And those principles still apply in Nigeria.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-1-6\">iii. Statutes of General Application<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-22\">Statutes are books and laws that are in writing — for instance, in Nigeria we have constitutional law. It is passed by the National Assembly or the State House of Assembly. Statutes of General Application are those laws that were passed by the parliament in the United Kingdom before 1900. So any law that was passed in written form before 1900 is considered a Statute of General Application.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-23\">One of those Statutes of General Application is the <b>Sale of Goods Act of 1893<\/b>. It still applies in Nigeria up to date. The only exception to the application of the received English law is where there is an existing law in Nigeria with respect to the same subject matter. For instance, some states in Nigeria do not apply the Sale of Goods Act of 1893 because they have their own Sale of Goods Law — like Lagos State and Edo State and<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-24\">some other states. They do not use the 1893 Act. But there are certain states in Nigeria that do not have their own Sale of Goods law, so the 1893 Act still applies there.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-25\">When we teach, we use the Sale of Goods Act of 1893 because we cannot start teaching what is in every state. Also, most of those Sale of Goods laws in those states were copied and pasted, so you find they have the same provisions. Just to make sure it is uniform, we use the Sale of Goods Act of 1893.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-1-7\">B. Other Sources of Law in Nigeria<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-26\">Aside from Received English Law, other sources of law in Nigeria are:<\/p><ul><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-27\"><b>Case Law (Judgment Law) in Nigeria<\/b> — decisions made by Nigerian courts over time.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-28\"><b>Nigerian Legislation<\/b> — those laws that have been enacted by our own National Assembly or our own legislature, not by the British parliament.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-29\"><b>Customary Law<\/b> — customs are ways of doing things, over and over, that have become accepted as law. When we talk about customary law in contract, it is mostly <i>trade customs<\/i> — the way things have been done within a particular industry. For instance, even if it is not in your contract, it is widely spread and widely known that when you rent a house you might need to pay agency fees. So there is already a custom in that real estate industry. There are certain tests that the court would apply to be able to determine whether that custom should be applied or not.<\/li><\/ul><h2 id=\"cil-class-1-8\">3. What is a Contract?<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-30\">A contract is an agreement between two or more people which is legally binding. Not all agreements are legally binding — so if it is an agreement and it is not legally binding, it is not a contract.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-31\">For an agreement to be considered a contract, it has to be <b>consensual<\/b> — which means there should not be any form of force, undue pressure, or duress. It has to be consensual. It has to be voluntary — people have to enter knowing what they are entering into and agreeing to enter, knowing that if they do not do what they have promised to do, they can be sued.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-32\">Contracts are agreements made between two or more parties or people which create binding rights and obligations.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-33\">All agreements are not contracts, because there are certain agreements that are unenforceable. There are such agreements where the parties did not intend that they should be sued if they did not perform what they promised. There are agreements that are not contracts — they are not enforceable for one reason or another. So all agreements are not contracts, but <b>all contracts must be agreements<\/b>.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-1-9\">Freedom of Contract<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-34\">The aim of the courts — the aim of law — is not to ask whether you made a good bargain or not. That is not the aim of the courts. That is not the aim of contract law, because the purpose of contract law is to ensure that whatever agreement you enter into, you must honour it. This is what is referred to as <b>freedom of<\/b> <b>contract<\/b>. Parties are free to enter into whatever bargain they want to. Parties have the freedom to enter into whatever bargain — and whatever bargain you enter into, you must honour it.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-35\">For instance, if you decide to sell your own house — or your most valuable thing, your iPhone, your laptop — for 450 naira, and the other party agrees, it is consensual. That is the bargain you made. The court is not going to ask where you were sleeping or whether that was a good deal. That is not the business of the court. The business of the court is: did you make that agreement? Did you make it freely? If you made it freely, the<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-36\">business of the court is to enforce it. So you must be careful, or you will be held to have breached your contract. The business of the court is to enforce whatever bargain the parties made.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-1-10\">4. Classifications of Contracts<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-37\">Just as there are different ways of describing human beings, there are different ways of classifying contracts:<\/p><ul><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-38\">Simple Contracts vs. Contracts Under Seal (Deeds)<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-39\">Express vs. Implied Contracts<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-40\">Bilateral vs. Unilateral Contracts<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-41\">Executed vs. Executory Contracts<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-class-1-11\">A. Simple Contracts vs. Contracts Under Seal (Deeds)<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-42\"><b>Contracts Under Seal / Deeds:<\/b> These are very formal contracts. Those contracts that have been signed, sealed, and delivered. You usually find a seal on them. They are very formal — people will come and bring witnesses; this person signed, that person signed in front of the witnesses. The witnesses also signed. There will be seals from the different parties. These types of very formal contracts are referred to as <b>Deeds<\/b>. That is why you often hear 'deed of assignment' and different types of deeds.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-43\"><b>Simple Contracts:<\/b> Aside from those very formal ones that are signed, sealed, and delivered, there are other types of contracts. They could be in writing, by words, or by action — but they are generally referred to as simple contracts because they are not formal. It could just be a document that contains the terms of your agreement, but the fact that it is not signed before witnesses and delivered means it is not a deed.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-44\">The main reason for the differentiation between simple contracts and contracts under seal is that in a contract under seal / deed, you do not need to provide <b>consideration<\/b>. You do not need to show that you gave something in exchange for that contract. So if somebody promises to give you their car and they sign and deliver a deed to that effect, you can enforce it whether you gave anything in exchange or not.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-45\">In a simple contract, however, you need to show that you are giving something in exchange for that person's promise — we will get to that when we talk about consideration. Also, there are certain types of agreements that the law says must be in the form of a deed. For instance, the buying and transfer of land — there are provisions that say it must be in the form of a deed, not just a simple contract.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-1-12\">B. Express vs. Implied Contracts<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-46\"><b>Express Contracts:<\/b> Those contracts where the parties agree on the terms by their words — where the parties clearly state the terms of their contract, whether spoken or written. Express contracts are the ones where the parties have negotiated and agreed — even if they did not negotiate, they agreed by their own words, whether spoken or written, that they would do this, they would do that. 'I will give you this, you give me that.'<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-47\"><b>Implied Contracts:<\/b> Those types of contracts that are implied from the conduct of the parties. They may not use words — they are inferred from the conduct of the parties. For example, you walk into a restaurant where they sell food. You sit down. They seat you and you start eating. You stand up and want to walk out — the court is going to infer that there was a contract, even though you did not exchange words. The fact that you went into a shop where you know they sell food for money, and you ate — it can be inferred from your conduct that you intended to enter into a contract. Contracts can form not just by your words but also from your conduct.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-1-13\">C. Bilateral vs. Unilateral Contracts<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-48\"><b>Bilateral Contracts:<\/b> Those contracts where the parties exchange promises. 'I will do this and you will do that.' Both of them are bound by the exchange of promises.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-49\"><b>Unilateral Contracts:<\/b> Where only one party promises to do something, and the other party has the option to do it or not. For example, you see an advert: 'buy one and get one free.' Only one party is making the promise. In most cases, such promises can be enforceable.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-50\"><b>Case Example — Carlill v. Carbolic Smoke Ball Co.:<\/b> There was a pandemic of influenza — a respiratory disease. Somebody published an advert in a newspaper: 'Take this smoke ball; use it as directed; you will not get influenza. But if you still contract influenza, we will pay you a sum of money — and that money has been deposited in the bank.' A woman used the smoke ball correctly and still got influenza. She sued, and the company was held liable because they were the only ones that made the promise. There was no exchange of promises — that is an example of a <b>unilateral contract<\/b> — a contract where only one person makes a promise to the whole world. Anybody can accept that promise. And if they accepted and did what they needed to do, you cannot go back on your promise. The court can infer there is a contract simply because one person has shown they are serious about keeping their promise. The Carbolic Smoke Ball Company was made to pay the amount of money they promised.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-1-14\">D. Executed vs. Executory Contracts<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-51\"><b>Executed Contract:<\/b> This is where both parties have already performed their obligations. For example, you bought your food, gave them 100 naira, they gave you the food — that contract is executed.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-52\"><b>Executory Contract:<\/b> Where both parties exchange promises to perform in the future. For instance, a contract for you to supply goods next month or next year — performance is to be done in the future.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-1-15\">5. Formation of Contracts<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-53\">Remember that all agreements are not contracts, but all contracts are agreements — which means that for an agreement to be legally binding, for it to be enforceable, there are certain things that you must show. There are certain elements that must be found in valid contracts.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-1-16\">Essential Elements of a Contract<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-54\"><b>Offer<\/b> — A proposal indicating willingness to enter into a contract.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-55\"><b>Acceptance<\/b> — The other party's agreement to the terms of the offer.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-56\"><b>Consideration<\/b> — Something of value exchanged between the parties.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-57\"><b>Intention to Contract<\/b> — Both parties must intend to be legally bound.<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-58\">There are also other elements such as capacity and things like that, but these four are the basic ones. There must be <i>consensus ad idem<\/i> — the parties must agree; they must consent.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-59\">But you will find that when cases end up in court, people deny everything. Even if they agreed with all their might, when an issue arises and it ends up in court, they will deny it. So what happens is that the court is not going to come and ask you: 'Did you agree or did you not agree?' The court is going to look at the circumstances of that case — especially the circumstances of your relationship and your transaction — in order to determine whether there was an offer, whether that offer was accepted, whether consideration was provided, and whether the intention to contract is present. So that is why these are the main elements of a contract — the court is not going to ask; it is just going to find out whether these elements can be found.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-1-17\">The Offer<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-60\">An offer is a proposal indicating willingness to enter into a contract with the person to whom it is addressed. So there will be someone who has made a proposal: 'I want to sell this'; 'you will need to buy'; 'I want to do this'; 'I want us to do this.' The other party agrees that they are willing and ready to enter into that contract.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-61\">An offer can be made to a specific person — whether a corporate person or an individual — and it is that specific person that must accept that offer; it cannot be accepted by someone else. However, if an offer is made to the whole world, anybody can accept it.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-62\">An offer can be oral, it can be in writing, or it can be made by conduct — the court can infer from your conduct that you are the one making an offer.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-63\">What happens when the person to whom the offer was made cannot accept it — perhaps because of ill health? Generally there are certain cases where there are representatives or agents of the person who can accept on their behalf. We will get to that when we talk about capacity.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-1-18\">Offer vs. Invitation to Treat<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-64\">For a proposal to be considered an offer, it must be <b>clear, complete, and definite<\/b>. If a proposal is not clear, not complete, and not definite, it will not be considered an offer — it will be considered an <b>invitation to treat<\/b>, and an invitation to treat cannot be accepted.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-65\">An invitation to treat simply means that you are still inviting the other party to come and negotiate. We are still not sure whether we really want the terms. That proposal must be so clear that the other party should just be able to say yes.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-66\"><b>Written contracts and the Parol Evidence Rule:<\/b> Where a contract is in writing but not clear and complete, that is a very dangerous situation. What you did sign will be held against you. What you did not sign — that is where your lawyer can argue that those terms were not expressly incorporated into the contract. There is something called the <i>parol evidence rule<\/i> — where there is a contract in writing, you cannot bring in extraneous things to add into it, except in very limited cases where the contract itself states that other things may be incorporated. Most record label contracts are the shadiest contracts you will find because they exploit desperate young talents who sign without thinking — very optimistic, looking at the money and not looking at the problems that could arise from that relationship.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-1-19\">Rules for Distinguishing Offer from Invitation to Treat<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-67\">The courts have developed certain rules over time to determine whether a proposal should be considered an offer or an invitation to treat.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-1-20\">Example 1: Goods Displayed in a Shop (e.g., ShopRite)<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-68\">What the courts have generally held:<\/p><ul><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-69\">Goods displayed on a shelf — that is an <b>invitation to treat<\/b>, not an offer.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-70\">When you pick up the goods and put them in your basket — that is <b>still not an offer<\/b>, because you can still change your mind and put them back.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-71\">When you take them to the counter and the cashier accepts your payment — <b>that is when the offer is<\/b> <b>made and accepted<\/b>. You are the one making the offer by taking the goods to the counter and being ready to pay. The cashier's duty is to accept the offer on behalf of the company by taking your payment and giving you a receipt. Every other step before that is an invitation to treat.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-class-1-21\">Example 2: ATM Machine<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-72\">You stand in front of the ATM thinking — <b>not an offer<\/b> (not clear, not complete, not definite).<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-73\">You insert your ATM card — <b>still not clear<\/b>; you might just want to check your balance.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-74\">You are given options — withdraw, transfer, etc. — <b>still not definite<\/b> until you choose.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-75\">You choose an action and select a specific amount — <b>at this point it becomes clear and definite<\/b>. The offer is made when you reach the point of no return — when you confirm. The contract is formed at the point of acceptance.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-class-1-22\">Example 3: Website Display of Goods (UK Case — TV Pricing Error)<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-76\">A company mistakenly displayed a TV on a website at £10 when it was supposed to be £100. People discovered this and crashed the website — thousands of orders were placed at £10. When the company realised the error, they shut it down, but many orders had already been made. The question was: was the display of goods on that digital platform an offer? If the display was an offer and putting the item in your cart was acceptance, the company would be liable for thousands of orders.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-77\">The court also has to look at <i>business efficacy<\/i> — if we decide it that way, businesses will go out of business. So it was held that a display of goods on a website is an <b>invitation to treat<\/b>. The party who put it in their cart was the one making the offer. The company accepted when they received payment and sent a confirmation. So those who had successfully completed the order were entitled to receive the goods. Those who had not yet received a confirmation — no contract had been formed.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-78\">That is why it is really important to determine at what point the offer was made and at what point it was accepted — because <b>that is exactly when the contract is formed<\/b>. Once the contract is formed, the party who does not do what they promised becomes liable — they have breached the contract. But before acceptance, if I withdraw, I cancel, I walk away — you cannot force me to enter into that contract because the contract has not yet been formed.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-1-23\">6. Remedies for Breach of Contract<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-79\">If it goes to court and the court determines that a contract was formed, there will be remedies. The remedy could be:<\/p><ul><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-80\"><b>Refund<\/b> — the return of money paid.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-81\"><b>Specific Performance<\/b> — you must do that thing you promised to do.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-1-p0-82\"><b>Damages<\/b> — monetary compensation for the loss suffered.<\/li><\/ul><h2 id=\"cil-class-1-24\">7. Coming Up in the Next Class<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-83\">From the next class, we will complete the elements of contract formation — acceptance, consideration, and intention to contract.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-1-p0-84\"><i>These notes were compiled from a class transcript (May 14, 2025). They reflect the lecturer's words, corrected for<\/i> <i>transcription/grammar errors and organised by topic for clarity.<\/i><\/p>", "toc": [{"id": "cil-class-1-0", "t": "1. Civil Law — An Overview", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-1-1", "t": "Court System in Nigeria", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-1-2", "t": "2. Sources of Law in Nigeria", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-1-3", "t": "A. Received English Law", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-1-4", "t": "i. Common Law", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-1-5", "t": "ii. Doctrines of Equity", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-1-6", "t": "iii. Statutes of General Application", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-1-7", "t": "B. Other Sources of Law in Nigeria", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-1-8", "t": "3. What is a Contract?", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-1-9", "t": "Freedom of Contract", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-1-10", "t": "4. Classifications of Contracts", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-1-11", "t": "A. Simple Contracts vs. Contracts Under Seal (Deeds)", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-1-12", "t": "B. Express vs. Implied Contracts", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-1-13", "t": "C. Bilateral vs. Unilateral Contracts", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-1-14", "t": "D. Executed vs. Executory Contracts", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-1-15", "t": "5. Formation of Contracts", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-1-16", "t": "Essential Elements of a Contract", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-1-17", "t": "The Offer", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-1-18", "t": "Offer vs. Invitation to Treat", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-1-19", "t": "Rules for Distinguishing Offer from Invitation to Treat", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-1-20", "t": "Example 1: Goods Displayed in a Shop (e.g., ShopRite)", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-1-21", "t": "Example 2: ATM Machine", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-1-22", "t": "Example 3: Website Display of Goods (UK Case — TV Pricing Error)", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-1-23", "t": "6. Remedies for Breach of Contract", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-1-24", "t": "7. Coming Up in the Next Class", "lv": 2}]}]}, "cil-class-2": {"course": "CIL", "ref": "Class 2", "title": "Acceptance, Consideration & Intention to Contract", "kicker": "Class notes · 21 May 2025", "words": 2820, "parts": [{"label": "", "note": "", "src": "Class 2- CIL_Law_of_Engineering_Contract_Class2_Notes.pdf", "html": "<h2 id=\"cil-class-2-0\">Recap from Class 1<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-1\">In the previous class, we discussed the sources of law in Nigeria — the received English law (common law, doctrines of equity, and statutes of general application), Nigerian legislation, case law, and customary law. We also covered what a contract is, classifications of contracts, and the first element of contract formation: the <b>offer<\/b>. We talked about how a proposal must be clear, complete, and definite to qualify as an offer; otherwise it is merely an invitation to treat. We also discussed how the courts have used certain rules and examples to determine at what point an offer is made. We also discussed the rule of precedent — that a decision made by a superior court must be followed by lower courts as long as the facts are the same or very similar.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-2-1\">1. Acceptance<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-2\">Acceptance is where the offeree accepts the proposal or the offer. It is the second element of a valid contract.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-2-2\">A. Conditional Acceptance Is Not Valid Acceptance<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-3\">You cannot change the terms of the offer and still call it acceptance. If you change the terms from the original and attach conditions, that is not acceptance — that is a <b>counter-offer<\/b>.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-4\">For example, if I say 'I want to sell this for 500 naira,' and you say 'Can you sell it to me tomorrow?' or 'I will buy it, but only if you deliver it' — when you give a condition like that, it is not acceptance. It becomes valid only when you perform that condition — only at that point does it become binding.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-5\">So if you take the time frame and you perform the condition before the time prescribed lapses, the contract becomes automatically binding. But if there is a time frame and you did not perform within that time, the offer could be said to have lapsed, and someone else may step in with a better offer.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-6\">That is why conditional acceptance is not valid acceptance. Conditional acceptance is not acceptance.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-2-3\">B. Silence Does Not Constitute Acceptance<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-7\">Silence or inactivity does not constitute valid acceptance. If I make an offer and I get silence — you do not respond, you do not say anything, that is not acceptance. Even if you ask for more information and the other party provides it and then there is still nothing — silence is not acceptance.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-2-4\">C. Communication of Acceptance<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-8\">There are certain rules regarding how acceptance should be communicated.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-2-5\">i. The Mirror Image Rule / Mode of Communication<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-9\">The mode used by the offeror in communicating the offer should also determine the mode in which the acceptance should be communicated. So if the offer was communicated by sending a WhatsApp message, it is expected that you should accept through a WhatsApp message or an even faster mode of communication.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-10\">The general rule is that the mode of communicating acceptance should be the same as the mode used in making the offer. If the offeror says 'respond within the next five days' or 'write to me' or 'send me an email' or 'fill out this form' — then that is exactly how they want you to accept. If you are told exactly how they want to receive the acceptance, that is exactly how you should accept.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-2-6\">ii. The Postal Rule<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-11\">There is an exception to the general rule — the <b>postal rule<\/b>. This rule was developed many years ago when the most common means of communication was the post.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-12\">If you send your acceptance by post, once it is delivered to the post office, it is considered as valid acceptance. This is very different from the general rule. The general rule says that acceptance is effective only when it is received by the offeror. But the postal rule is an exception — acceptance is effective at the point of posting, not at the point of receipt.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-13\">The reason for this is that the law will not punish you for delays that are beyond your control. If you post your acceptance but the postal service delays it, the law will not hold you responsible for that. So once you have posted it, it is considered accepted.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-2-7\">2. Termination of an Offer<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-14\">An offer does not last forever. There are several ways in which an offer can come to an end.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-2-8\">A. Withdrawal / Revocation by the Offeror<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-15\">The offeror can withdraw or revoke the offer before it is accepted. For example, if I say 'I want to sell my phone for 400,000 naira,' and before you get back to me, I communicate to you that I have changed my mind — I do not want to sell it anymore. Perhaps the price of phones has gone up, so I value what I have more, or someone else has made a better offer. You cannot then force me into a contract. That is self-explanatory.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-2-9\">B. Rejection by the Offeree<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-16\">If I make an offer to you and you reject it, the offer dies. Once you have rejected an offer, you cannot go back and say you now want to accept it. The rejection terminates the offer.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-2-10\">C. Lapse of Time<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-17\">If the offeror gives you a set time frame to accept the offer, and that time lapses without acceptance, the offer dies. You cannot accept after the time prescribed has elapsed.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-2-11\">D. Counter-Offer<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-18\">As discussed under conditional acceptance, if the offeree responds with different terms instead of accepting the original offer as it is, that constitutes a counter-offer. The counter-offer kills the original offer. The offeree cannot then go back and accept the original offer.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-2-12\">E. Death of the Offeror<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-19\">If the person who made the offer dies, the offer may be terminated — especially with respect to personal contracts or contracts of service. For instance, if one of your favourite musicians makes an offer to come and perform at your show, and then the musician dies, you cannot accept that offer knowing that the offeror has died.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-20\">However, this is different when it comes to contracts involving property. For instance, a contract between parties regarding property or land — where other people or successors can step in and perform that contract — in such a case, it is not expected that the death of one party should necessarily bring the offer to an end.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-2-13\">3. Consideration<\/h2><h3 id=\"cil-class-2-14\">A. Definition<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-21\">Consideration in law means something of value given in exchange for the other party's promise. It is the price that you pay in exchange for the other party's promise. Somebody must give something in exchange for what the other person has promised.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-22\">This is the legal way of saying that you must give something to get something. You do not leave it as a situation where it is purely gratuitous — where it is up to that person to do it or not. If that person decides not to do it, you cannot sue, because there was nothing exchanged. It is only a person who has given something in exchange that can hold the other party to their promise.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-23\">What we teach law students in two semesters, we are trying to condense for you. You are engineering students, so we should not overload you with too much information.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-2-15\">B. Examples of Consideration<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-24\">If I promise you 500 naira and you promise to sweep this classroom — if I give you this, you give me that — that is consideration. It does not have to be money.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-25\">For instance, say you have five chairs and you decide to have a party and you need my chairs. You come to me and we negotiate — you give me a bag of rice in exchange for the use of my chairs. That is an exchange. That is consideration.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-26\">If I promise to lend you my car, and you say you will bring it back the next day — and at the end of the day you do not return my car and I sue you — you can see that there was an exchange: I gave you the use of my car, and your promise was to return it. That happens all the time.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-2-16\">C. The Importance of Putting Things in Writing<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-27\">If you do not take anything away from this class, the one thing is that you should try to make sure that you do things in writing.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-28\">There was a case where a law student was helping their landlord. The student found a tenant for the landlord, collected the rent money, and transferred it to the landlord. The landlord then decided that the accommodation he was offering was no longer available. And the people had already paid. When it was said that contracts can be oral, they can be formed by word of mouth — but you must be able to prove it by bringing witnesses.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-29\">The person who was there when the promise was made — that person was there when this happened. Even if the contract was made by words, you should always have evidence. Send a text message, send a WhatsApp message: 'Thank you, I hope you got the money, just checking in, thank you for accepting.' That is<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-30\">how you can prove it.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-31\">What is the relevance of evidence? This is because that is the only way you can prove what happened. You do not always have to go to court, but you have to have a contract before you can even talk about breach.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-2-17\">D. Consideration Can Be Anything of Value<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-32\">Consideration could be anything that you give in exchange for the other party's promise — money, goods, services. You give me money, I give you a service. You give me 500,000 naira for me to build a website for you — the building of the platform is the consideration from your side, and the money is the consideration from the other side.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-2-18\">E. Consideration Can Be a Right Given Up<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-33\">Consideration can also be a right that you give up. You have a right to peaceful enjoyment of your property. If you decide to accept some nuisance — for instance, noise or disturbance — in exchange for payment, that is valid consideration.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-34\">For example, say I buy a property and I have the right to peaceful enjoyment. My neighbour promises to pay me 100,000 naira every month for me to endure the noise from their operations.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-35\">There was a case where a man had a boiler factory, and someone came and promised to pay a sum of money if the property owner would allow the noise and disturbance from the boiler. The argument was: yes, we agreed — you are going to let us operate the boiler. But what did you give us in exchange? The answer was: the property belongs to him. He has the right to peace and quiet. And so, giving up that right was a form of consideration.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-2-19\">F. Rules of Consideration<\/h3><h3 id=\"cil-class-2-20\">i. Consideration Must Be Sufficient but Need Not Be Adequate<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-36\">Remember that we said that in contract law, it is not the business of the court to ask whether you made a good bargain or not. Consideration must be sufficient — meaning it must be something recognisable in law — but it need not be adequate. The court will not ask whether what you gave was equal in value to what you received.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-37\">There was a case where a woman was married to her husband. She promised to stay faithful for the rest of her life. The question before the court was whether that promise constituted sufficient consideration. The court found that it did.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-38\"><b>Case Example — Chappell &amp; Co. v. Nestlé Co.:<\/b> Nestlé had a promotion where if you collected chocolate bar wrappers and sent them in, they would give you a music record. Chappell (who owned the copyright to the music) argued that there was no real contract because the chocolate wrappers had no real value — they were just rubbish that Nestlé threw away. The court held that it was not for the court to determine whether the consideration was adequate or not. The wrappers constituted sufficient consideration. What people were paying — the wrappers — was part of the bargain, irrespective of whether it seemed worthless. So the court said: as long as there is consideration, it does not matter what the value is. That is the meaning of 'consideration must be sufficient but need not be adequate.'<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-39\">The law does not ask whether you made a very fair deal. As long as there is something — even if it seems small — given in exchange, the court will recognise it.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-2-21\">ii. Consideration Must Move from the Promisee<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-40\">This means that the person who is seeking to enforce the promise must be the one who provided the consideration. You cannot enforce a contract if you did not give anything in exchange. Even if somebody is giving you something, you must also give something in exchange for it. The consideration must come from the person who wants to enforce the promise.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-2-22\">iii. Past Consideration Is Not Good Consideration<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-41\">If you have already performed an act before a promise is made, that past act cannot be the consideration for the new promise. For instance, if a father says 'I always remember how you took care of me all these years, so I will give you my property' — the care that was already given in the past cannot serve as consideration for the new promise. It has already happened. Past consideration is not valid consideration.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-42\">This does not mean that past acts are never recognised, but generally, consideration must be something given at the time of the contract or promised for the future — not something that has already been done before the promise was made.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-2-23\">iv. An Existing Obligation Is Not Good Consideration<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-43\">If you are already under a duty to do something — either by law or by an existing contract — then doing that same thing cannot count as consideration for a new promise. Because it has already been agreed upon. The performance of an existing obligation is not consideration.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-44\">For instance, if we already have a contract that you will clean this classroom every day, and I then promise to pay you extra for the same cleaning, that existing obligation cannot serve as fresh consideration for the new promise. You would need to do something additional or different.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-2-24\">4. Intention to Create Legal Relations<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-45\">The last element we will discuss is the intention to create legal relations. For a contract to be valid, the parties must have intended that their agreement would be legally binding. Not every agreement is intended to be a contract.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-2-25\">A. Social and Domestic Agreements<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-46\">Where the agreement is social, domestic, or between family members — there is a <b>presumption that there<\/b> <b>is no intention to create legal relations<\/b>. It is not enforceable.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-47\">For instance, a promise you make to your mother, or a social arrangement between friends — you do not expect that if you do not do what you promised, you will be sued. There is an understanding that these are not legal obligations. So if you promise your boyfriend or girlfriend something and you do not deliver, they cannot sue you because there is no intention to create legal relations in social or domestic situations.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-2-26\">B. Commercial and Business Agreements<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-48\">However, when you enter into a commercial or business agreement, there is a <b>presumption that there is<\/b> <b>intention to create legal relations<\/b>. When you enter into a contract in a business or commercial context, it is presumed that you intend to be bound.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-49\">What happens then? If you do not perform your promise in a business context, the other party can hold you liable. You can be sued. The court will presume that you intended to be legally bound because it is a commercial arrangement.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-50\">So if you promise something in a purely social setting — say over drinks with friends — the law does not enforce that. But the moment it is a business deal, even if done casually, the law presumes you intended it to be binding.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-2-27\">Summary — Elements of a Valid Contract<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-51\">For a valid and enforceable contract to exist, all four elements must be present:<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-52\"><b>Element<\/b> <b>Key Points<\/b><\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-53\">1. Offer Must be clear, complete, and definite. Distinguish from invitation to treat.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-54\">2. Acceptance Must be unconditional. Silence is not acceptance. Must be communicated properly (mirror image rule, postal r<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-55\">3. Consideration Something of value exchanged. Must be sufficient but need not be adequate. Must move from the promisee.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-56\">4. Intention to Create Legal Relations<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-57\">Social/domestic = presumed NO intention. Commercial/business = presumed YES intention.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-2-p0-58\"><i>These notes were compiled from a class transcript and audio recording (May 21, 2025). The original transcript contained significant<\/i> <i>transcription errors which have been corrected using audio cross-reference and subject-matter context. The lecturer's intended<\/i> <i>meaning has been preserved throughout.<\/i><\/p>", "toc": [{"id": "cil-class-2-0", "t": "Recap from Class 1", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-2-1", "t": "1. Acceptance", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-2-2", "t": "A. Conditional Acceptance Is Not Valid Acceptance", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-2-3", "t": "B. Silence Does Not Constitute Acceptance", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-2-4", "t": "C. Communication of Acceptance", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-2-5", "t": "i. The Mirror Image Rule / Mode of Communication", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-2-6", "t": "ii. The Postal Rule", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-2-7", "t": "2. Termination of an Offer", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-2-8", "t": "A. Withdrawal / Revocation by the Offeror", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-2-9", "t": "B. Rejection by the Offeree", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-2-10", "t": "C. Lapse of Time", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-2-11", "t": "D. Counter-Offer", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-2-12", "t": "E. Death of the Offeror", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-2-13", "t": "3. Consideration", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-2-14", "t": "A. Definition", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-2-15", "t": "B. Examples of Consideration", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-2-16", "t": "C. The Importance of Putting Things in Writing", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-2-17", "t": "D. Consideration Can Be Anything of Value", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-2-18", "t": "E. Consideration Can Be a Right Given Up", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-2-19", "t": "F. Rules of Consideration", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-2-20", "t": "i. Consideration Must Be Sufficient but Need Not Be Adequate", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-2-21", "t": "ii. Consideration Must Move from the Promisee", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-2-22", "t": "iii. Past Consideration Is Not Good Consideration", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-2-23", "t": "iv. An Existing Obligation Is Not Good Consideration", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-2-24", "t": "4. Intention to Create Legal Relations", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-2-25", "t": "A. Social and Domestic Agreements", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-2-26", "t": "B. Commercial and Business Agreements", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-2-27", "t": "Summary — Elements of a Valid Contract", "lv": 2}]}]}, "cil-class-3": {"course": "CIL", "ref": "Class 3", "title": "Terms of Contract", "kicker": "Class notes · 4 June 2025", "words": 4004, "parts": [{"label": "", "note": "", "src": "Class 3 - CIL_Law_of_Engineering_Contract_Class3_Notes.pdf", "html": "<h2 id=\"cil-class-3-0\">Recap from Previous Classes<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-1\">In the previous classes, we talked about the different types of contracts, the elements of a valid contract — offer, acceptance, consideration, and intention to create legal relations. Those concerned the <b>formation<\/b> of contracts: what are the elements that must be present. Today, we are going to be discussing the <b>content<\/b> of the contract — what the contract actually contains.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-3-1\">1. Terms of Contract — Introduction<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-2\"><b>Terms of contract<\/b> refer to the legally binding statements or promises made by parties to the contract which give rise to <b>rights and obligations<\/b>.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-3\">When a contract is being negotiated, various statements are made. The parties say different things — they make different promises. But not everything that is said, not every promise or every statement made during negotiation, will be considered a valid part of the contract.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-4\">Disputes may arise with respect to whether the parties intended that some of the statements made during the negotiation are to be legally binding or not. Not all statements are legally binding. Some of these statements become part of a legally binding contract as <b>terms<\/b>. Some are not considered to be part of the contract and are merely <b>representations<\/b>.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-3-2\">Terms vs. Representations<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-5\">Statements that are legally binding are referred to as <b>terms<\/b>. Statements that are not legally binding are referred to as <b>mere representations<\/b>.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-6\">Those statements that come with binding force — if one of the parties does not do what they promised to do, the other party can enforce the contract. However, some of those statements do not have legal force — the other party cannot be found legally liable to perform under contract law.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-7\">That does not mean the other party might not be able to rely on other types of law to enforce that promise. For instance, if the promise was made with the intention to defraud the other person, you might not be able to enforce it under contract law, but you should be able to use criminal law to seek redress — get the person arrested. You might even be able to use the <b>law of torts<\/b>, which is a different type of law. It is very similar to contract law because they are all common-law based, but it is not contract law.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-8\">When you see documents with 'terms and conditions,' what that is probably saying is: these are the terms of the contract — the statements and promises that are legally binding on all the parties.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-3-3\">2. Tests for Distinguishing Terms from Representations<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-9\">The courts have developed tests for determining whether a statement is a term or a representation. These tests are not absolute — they are not set in stone. They are more like guidelines that help the court determine whether to classify a statement as a term or a mere representation.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-3-4\">Test 1: Stage of the Contract<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-10\">At what stage of the contract was the statement made? Was it made at the early stage of the contract — when the parties were still trying to get the other party to enter into the contract? Or was it made at the later stage — after the parties already know what it is that they want?<\/p><ul><li id=\"cil-class-3-p0-11\">Statements made at the <b>early stage<\/b> of negotiation would most likely be considered <b>representations<\/b>.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-3-p0-12\">Statements made <b>later<\/b> during contract formation would most likely be considered <b>terms<\/b>.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-class-3-5\">Test 2: Agreements Reduced to Writing<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-13\">Was the oral statement reduced to writing? The court looks at whether the parties agreed on terms orally and then eventually put them in writing. If they put certain things in writing but left others out:<\/p><ul><li id=\"cil-class-3-p0-14\">Oral statements that <b>did not find their way<\/b> into the written contract will usually be considered <b>mere<\/b> <b>representations<\/b>.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-3-p0-15\">Statements that <b>were included<\/b> in the written contract — even if originally made orally — will usually be considered <b>terms<\/b>.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-class-3-6\">Test 3: Special Knowledge and Skill<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-16\">When a party who makes the statement has superior knowledge or expertise with respect to the subject matter of the transaction:<\/p><ul><li id=\"cil-class-3-p0-17\">A statement made by a party who has <b>expert knowledge and skill<\/b> = most likely a <b>term<\/b>.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-3-p0-18\">A statement made by a <b>layman<\/b> = most likely a <b>representation<\/b>.<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-19\"><b>Case Example — Car Dealer:<\/b> A car dealer stated that a car was a particular year model with certain mileage and specifications. The buyer, an ordinary consumer, bought the car based on those statements. The court held that the statement made by the car dealer was a <b>term<\/b>, because the car dealer had superior knowledge about cars. If we swap the scenario and the person making the statement is just someone selling their old car who does not know anything about cars, such statements might be considered a mere representation.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-3-7\">Test 4: Importance of the Statement<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-20\">If the party to whom the statement is being made informs the person making the statement that the statement is of great importance — that they would not have entered into the contract if the statement had not been made — the court would usually consider that statement a <b>term<\/b>, not a mere representation.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-21\"><b>Case Example — Hops and Sulphur (Bannerman v. White):<\/b> A party wanted to buy hops (used to make beer). He asked the seller: 'Were these hops treated with sulphur? Because if they were treated with sulphur, I will not buy them — they will not ferment into beer properly.' The seller said the hops were not treated with sulphur. The buyer informed the seller that this was really important to him. He went ahead and bought the hops, only to discover they had been treated with sulphur. The court held that the statement was a <b>term<\/b>, because the buyer had clearly communicated the importance of that statement.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-3-8\">3. Categories of Contractual Terms<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-22\">Once a statement is determined to be a term (not a mere representation), the next question is: what are the implications of breaching that term? Terms are categorised into three:<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-3-9\">A. Conditions<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-23\">Conditions are those terms that have great importance. They are the terms that constitute the <b>main purpose<\/b> of the contract — the things that go to the very root of the contract. If a party breaches a condition, it entitles the innocent party to <b>terminate (repudiate) the contract<\/b> and also to <b>claim damages<\/b> (monetary compensation).<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-24\"><b>Example 1:<\/b> Davido is contracted to perform at a faculty event. The contract says he would come for two days of rehearsals and then perform on the third day. But he does not come at all, or he comes for rehearsals but does not perform. Not performing is a breach of a condition, because the primary aim of that contract is for him to perform. The party who contracted him can terminate the contract, demand a refund, and also claim damages for expenses already incurred.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-25\"><b>Example 2:<\/b> You enter into a contract to buy a car, and instead a bicycle is delivered — or something that was supposed to be a car but does not move. That breach goes to the very root of the contract, and the innocent party can repudiate the contract and claim damages.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-3-10\">B. Warranties<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-26\">Warranties are the less important terms — the minor terms. The breach of a warranty would <b>not<\/b> entitle the innocent party to terminate the contract. The breach of a warranty would <b>only<\/b> allow that party to ask for <b>damages<\/b>.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-27\">If the breach is of a term that money can cure, the court will expect you to use money (damages) to compensate and remedy the defect.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-28\"><b>Example:<\/b> You ask for a black car. A white car is supplied to you. That does not go to the root of the contract, because you can take the car and have it repainted to the colour you want. It only costs money. So you can claim damages but you cannot terminate the contract. Similarly, whether the seats are leather or whether the wheels are alloy — these things can be remedied with money.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-29\"><b>Conditions<\/b> <b>Warranties<\/b><\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-30\">Importance Major / goes to the root Minor / subsidiary<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-31\">Breach entitles party to...<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-32\">Terminate the contract AND claim damages<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-33\">Claim damages ONLY (cannot terminate)<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-34\">Example Ordered a car; bicycle delivered<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-35\">Ordered a black car; white car delivered<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-3-11\">C. Innominate (Intermediate) Terms<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-36\">Innominate or intermediate terms are the hybrid — they sit between conditions and warranties. Most times, the court might not be able to determine immediately whether a breach should be classified as a breach of a condition or a warranty.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-37\"><b>Example:<\/b> You order a Toyota car and what is delivered is a Toyota Highlander instead of a Toyota Camry, or even a Honda CR-V. The court might find it difficult to immediately classify that as a breach of a condition or a warranty.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-38\">For innominate terms, the court uses the <b>substantial benefit test<\/b>: did the breach deprive the innocent party of <b>substantially all the benefits<\/b> they should have enjoyed under the contract?<\/p><ul><li id=\"cil-class-3-p0-39\">If <b>yes<\/b> — the court treats it as a <b>condition<\/b> (innocent party can terminate and claim damages).<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-3-p0-40\">If <b>no<\/b> — the court treats it as a <b>warranty<\/b> (innocent party can only claim damages).<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-class-3-12\">Q&amp;A;: What If Severity Is Subjective?<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-41\"><i>Student question: If something is serious to one party but not to another, how does the court judge severity?<\/i><\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-42\">The parties are free to include anything they want, so they might even expressly state in the contract that a particular term is very important — they can label it a condition. The court would most likely agree with whatever the parties have decided, because the aim of the court is not to rewrite the contract for the parties but to enforce it.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-43\">However, there are cases where the court has decided that even though both parties agreed that something is a condition, it is not serious enough to be treated as one. In such a case, the court asks: can money fix it? For example, if you want everything pink and the car or house was not painted pink — the court might ask whether the other party can simply pay extra money to have it redone. If money can cure it, the court may treat it as a warranty, not a condition.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-3-13\">4. Types of Contractual Terms<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-44\">There are two main types of terms: <b>express terms<\/b> and <b>implied terms<\/b>.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-3-14\">A. Express Terms<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-45\">Express terms are those terms that have been precisely agreed upon by the parties. They may be <b>written or<\/b> <b>oral<\/b>. These are the things that the parties explicitly, specifically agreed on — either by written words or spoken words.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-3-15\">B. Implied Terms<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-46\">Implied terms, on the other hand, are those terms that the parties did not specifically agree upon. The parties probably did not even discuss them at all. But these terms are read into the contract by the courts, by custom, or by statute. They are deemed part of the contract even though the parties did not expressly state them.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-47\">Implied terms are classified into three: terms implied by <b>courts<\/b>, terms implied by <b>custom or usage<\/b>, and terms implied by <b>statute<\/b>.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-3-16\">i. Terms Implied by Courts<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-48\">Courts are usually <b>reluctant<\/b> to imply terms into a contract because of the principle of party autonomy — parties are expected to take care of their own agreements and their own interests. The business of the court is to enforce the contract, not to write or rewrite it for the parties.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-49\">But there are limited instances where the court has to imply terms into a contract. For the court to do so, it uses two tests:<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-3-17\">a. The Officious Bystander Test<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-50\">An officious bystander is an imaginary person — not a real person. The court asks: if an officious bystander were asked, 'Do you think the parties would have included this particular term in their contract if they had<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-51\">thought about it?' If the officious bystander would say 'Of course, yes,' then the court will go ahead and read that term into the contract. If the officious bystander would say 'Not really,' then the court will not imply it.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-52\"><b>Example — Restaurant and Rotten Food:<\/b> You walk into a restaurant and order rice, plantain, and assorted meat — goat meat, beef, chicken, egg. You and the seller agree on the price: 10,000 naira. Those are the express terms. But when you eat the food, you realise it is rotten and full of stones. The seller says: 'Did we agree that I would sell you fresh food? There is no such term in our contract.' You go to court. The court asks an officious bystander: 'Do you think the parties would have put a term in their contract that the food should be fresh and stone-free, if they had thought about it?' The officious bystander would say: 'Of course, yes.' So the court implies the term that the food should be fresh and free from stones.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-3-18\">b. The Business Efficacy Test<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-53\">The court may also imply a term where it is necessary to give business efficacy to the contract — that is, to make the contract workable, more efficient, or even doable in the first place.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-54\"><b>Example — Mechanic and Towing:<\/b> You hire a mechanic to come and fix your car. The express terms are: the mechanic will come to your location, fix the car, and you will pay a sum of money. But when the mechanic arrives, he realises the problem cannot be fixed on the spot — the car needs to be towed to his workshop. You say: 'We did not agree on towing; I will not pay for it.' The mechanic goes to court. The court uses the business efficacy test and says: to be able to perform this contract, it is necessary that the car be towed to the workshop. So the court implies the term that you must pay for towing, because without it, the contract simply cannot be performed.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-55\">The court only implies terms that are beneficial and that the parties would have agreed upon if they had thought about it. The aim is to make the contract easier, more efficient, or doable.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-3-19\">ii. Terms Implied by Custom or Usage<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-56\">There are times when certain terms, which the parties did not expressly agree upon, form part of the contract because that particular practice is recognised, is notorious, and is well known in that industry. The parties in that industry are aware that such terms usually apply to their contracts.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-57\"><b>Example — Agency Fees:<\/b> When you enter into a contract to buy, sell, or lease a house, it has become the custom that agency fees will be paid. Even if the parties did not specifically agree by written or oral words that agency fees will be paid, the court might read such a term into the contract simply because it has been done over time, it has become a custom in that industry, it is notorious — everyone knows it — and it does not go against natural justice.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-58\">The same applies in shipping contracts and other industries where certain terms automatically form part of the contract due to established market customs.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-3-20\">iii. Terms Implied by Statute<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-59\">Statutes are written laws. Some statutes contain provisions that require certain terms to be read into contracts, whether the parties agreed upon them or not. Examples include the <b>Sale of Goods Act<\/b>, the <b>Federal Competition and Consumer Protection Act (FCCPA)<\/b>, and the <b>Hire Purchase Act<\/b>.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-60\">We will focus on the <b>Sale of Goods Act<\/b>, particularly Sections 12 to 15, which imply certain terms into contracts for the sale of goods.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-3-21\">5. Implied Terms Under the Sale of Goods Act<\/h2><h3 id=\"cil-class-3-22\">Section 12 — Implied Condition as to Title<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-61\">The Sale of Goods Act implies that the seller must have <b>good title<\/b> — that is, the seller must either own the property or have authority to transfer ownership. You cannot sell what you do not have. If you do not have the right to the property, you cannot sell it.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-62\">If the parties enter into a contract and it turns out the seller does not have good title — the seller is not the owner, or is borrowing the property — there is an implied condition that has been breached. Since this is a <b>condition<\/b>, the innocent party can <b>terminate the contract and claim damages<\/b>.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-63\">Even though the parties did not expressly agree on a provision about title, Section 12 has read it into the contract: there is a promise that the seller has the right to sell.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-64\">Section 12 also implies two <b>warranties<\/b>:<\/p><ul><li id=\"cil-class-3-p0-65\"><b>Quiet Possession:<\/b> The buyer must be able to enjoy quiet possession of the goods. It should not be the case that the buyer is using the property and people the seller owes keep coming to disturb the buyer's enjoyment. For example, if the seller did not pay customs duties on a car, and customs officials keep harassing the buyer every time they drive it — that is a breach of the implied warranty of quiet possession.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-3-p0-66\"><b>Freedom from Undisclosed Charges and Encumbrances:<\/b> If there is some sort of loan that the seller collected and used the property as collateral, the seller has a duty to inform the buyer before the contract is entered into. Undisclosed charges or encumbrances on the property constitute a breach of this implied warranty.<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-67\"><b>Case Example:<\/b> There was a case where somebody leased a machine meant to spray the lines on roads. It turned out the seller did not have the right to sell, and the buyer was being disturbed — not allowed to use the machine as someone who now had possession. That was depriving the buyer of quiet possession.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-3-23\">Section 13 — Implied Condition as to Description<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-68\">Where goods are sold by description, there is an <b>implied condition<\/b> that the goods will <b>correspond exactly<\/b> <b>with the description<\/b>.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-69\">What are goods sold by description? These are goods where, when the buyer was buying them, the buyer did not have the opportunity to see, touch, or feel the physical goods. The only thing used in determining whether to buy was the description provided by the seller — a picture, a listing, specifications, etc.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-70\">This is different from buying at a market where you can pick up the item, test it, try it in your mouth, or feel the texture. When you buy from a market, you use your senses to determine how good the goods are.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-71\"><b>Example — Online Shopping:<\/b> Buying something online is a good example of sale by description. You cannot test or touch the goods — you can only see the picture provided by the seller. Many people have experienced buying what they thought was a full-sized fridge from an online platform like Temu, only to realise it is a miniature decorative toy. The picture was taken in a way that made it look like a full-sized appliance.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-72\">If the goods do not correspond with the description, then the implied condition has been breached, and the other party can <b>terminate the contract<\/b>.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-3-24\">Section 14 — Implied Terms as to Quality and Fitness for Purpose<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-73\">The general rule is <i><b>caveat emptor<\/b><\/i> — 'let the buyer beware.' The buyer has the responsibility to do due diligence, to inspect the goods, and to make decisions that are in their best interest. The aim of the court is<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-74\">not simply to protect you from making bad decisions.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-75\">However, where the seller sells goods in the course of business, the law implies two important conditions:<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-3-25\">(a) Merchantable / Satisfactory Quality<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-76\">The goods must be of <b>satisfactory quality<\/b>, meaning a reasonable person would regard them as acceptable considering their description, price, and other relevant circumstances. They do not have to be the best quality, but they must have acceptable quality.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-3-26\">(b) Fitness for Purpose<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-77\">For a party to rely on this provision, two things must be shown:<\/p><ul><li id=\"cil-class-3-p0-78\">The buyer <b>made known<\/b> to the seller the specific purpose for which the goods are required.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-3-p0-79\">The buyer <b>relied on the seller's skill or judgment<\/b>.<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-80\">If both conditions are met, there is an implied condition that the goods will be reasonably fit for that purpose.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-81\"><b>Example 1 — Tomatoes:<\/b> If you go to buy tomatoes and you need the hard ones specifically for frying eggs, you cannot go back and sue the seller for selling tomatoes that are not fit for your purpose — unless you informed the seller what you needed them for and relied on the seller's judgment.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-82\"><b>Example 2 — Running Shoes:<\/b> You need lightweight running shoes that will protect your ankle. You tell the seller the specific purpose, and the seller says: 'Buy these ones.' You rely on that judgment and buy them. If the shoes turn out to be too heavy and not suitable for running, you can sue the seller for breaching the implied condition as to fitness for purpose.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-83\"><b>Example 3 — Fan for a Child:<\/b> You want a fan that blows air (not the newer AC fans that blow cool mist) specifically because your child likes to stand in front of it and sing, and the air makes their voice shake. You tell the seller this specific purpose and rely on their expertise. If the fan turns out not to be fit for that purpose, you can bring an action.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-84\">However, if you told the seller the purpose but <b>did not rely<\/b> on the seller's opinion — you still went ahead and bought something else — you cannot rely on this implied condition.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-3-27\">Section 15 — Implied Condition in Sale by Sample<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-85\">Where goods are sold by reference to a sample, three conditions are implied:<\/p><ul><li id=\"cil-class-3-p0-86\">The <b>bulk of the goods<\/b> must correspond with the <b>sample in quality<\/b>. You cannot show a high-quality sample and then supply goods of lesser quality.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-3-p0-87\">The buyer must have a <b>reasonable opportunity to compare<\/b> the bulk of the goods with the sample.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-3-p0-88\">The goods must be <b>free from latent defects<\/b> — defects that would not be apparent upon reasonable examination of the sample. If there were defects on the body of the sample, the buyer would have seen them and negotiated accordingly. But if the defects are hidden and not obvious, the law protects the buyer.<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-89\">If the goods do not correspond with the sample or have latent defects, the innocent party can terminate the contract.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-90\">Even if the parties did not add this into the contract expressly, the court can read such terms into the contract because the law (the Sale of Goods Act) provides for it.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-3-28\">6. Closing Remarks<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-91\">Exclusion clauses were mentioned but not covered in detail in this class. The lecturer noted that slides would be shared for all previous classes.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-92\">In the next class, a different lecturer will take over to cover <b>capacity<\/b> and other remaining topics.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-3-29\">Summary — Terms of Contract at a Glance<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-93\"><b>Topic<\/b> <b>Key Points<\/b><\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-94\">Terms vs. Representations<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-95\">Terms = legally binding statements that form part of the contract. Representations = non-binding statements made during negotiation.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-96\">4 Tests to Distinguish<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-97\">1. Stage of contract (early = representation, late = term) 2. Reduced to writing (in writing = term, not = representation) 3. Special knowledge (expert = term, layman = representation) 4. Importance communicated (important = term)<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-98\">3 Categories of Terms<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-99\">Conditions: major; breach = terminate + damages Warranties: minor; breach = damages only Innominate: hybrid; court uses substantial benefit test<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-100\">Express vs. Implied Terms<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-101\">Express: specifically agreed upon (written or oral) Implied: read in by courts, custom/usage, or statute<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-102\">Implied by Courts<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-103\">Officious bystander test + Business efficacy test Courts are reluctant; only where necessary<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-104\">Sale of Goods Act Ss.12-15<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-105\">S.12: Title (condition) + quiet possession (warranty) S.13: Sale by description — must correspond exactly S.14: Quality and fitness for purpose (caveat emptor) S.15: Sale by sample — bulk must match sample<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-3-p0-106\"><i>These notes were compiled from a class transcript and lecture slides (June 4, 2025). Transcription errors have been corrected<\/i> <i>using the slide content as reference. The lecturer's intended meaning has been preserved throughout.<\/i><\/p>", "toc": [{"id": "cil-class-3-0", "t": "Recap from Previous Classes", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-3-1", "t": "1. Terms of Contract — Introduction", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-3-2", "t": "Terms vs. Representations", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-3-3", "t": "2. Tests for Distinguishing Terms from Representations", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-3-4", "t": "Test 1: Stage of the Contract", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-3-5", "t": "Test 2: Agreements Reduced to Writing", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-3-6", "t": "Test 3: Special Knowledge and Skill", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-3-7", "t": "Test 4: Importance of the Statement", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-3-8", "t": "3. Categories of Contractual Terms", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-3-9", "t": "A. Conditions", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-3-10", "t": "B. Warranties", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-3-11", "t": "C. Innominate (Intermediate) Terms", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-3-12", "t": "Q&A;: What If Severity Is Subjective?", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-3-13", "t": "4. Types of Contractual Terms", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-3-14", "t": "A. Express Terms", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-3-15", "t": "B. Implied Terms", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-3-16", "t": "i. Terms Implied by Courts", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-3-17", "t": "a. The Officious Bystander Test", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-3-18", "t": "b. The Business Efficacy Test", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-3-19", "t": "ii. Terms Implied by Custom or Usage", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-3-20", "t": "iii. Terms Implied by Statute", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-3-21", "t": "5. Implied Terms Under the Sale of Goods Act", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-3-22", "t": "Section 12 — Implied Condition as to Title", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-3-23", "t": "Section 13 — Implied Condition as to Description", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-3-24", "t": "Section 14 — Implied Terms as to Quality and Fitness for Purpose", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-3-25", "t": "(a) Merchantable / Satisfactory Quality", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-3-26", "t": "(b) Fitness for Purpose", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-3-27", "t": "Section 15 — Implied Condition in Sale by Sample", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-3-28", "t": "6. Closing Remarks", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-3-29", "t": "Summary — Terms of Contract at a Glance", "lv": 2}]}]}, "cil-class-4": {"course": "CIL", "ref": "Class 4", "title": "Capacity to Contract", "kicker": "Class notes · Folake Adewumi · Delivered via Zoom", "words": 2251, "parts": [{"label": "", "note": "", "src": "Class 4 - CIL_Law_of_Engineering_Contract_Class4_Notes.pdf", "html": "<h2 id=\"cil-class-4-0\">1. Introduction — What is Capacity to Contract?<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-1\">Capacity to contract refers to the <b>legal competence<\/b> of a person or organisation to enter into a binding, enforceable agreement. It requires being of sound mind, of legal age, and not disqualified by law.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-2\">In simpler terms, it is the ability — being in the right state of mind — to contract with another party.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-4-1\">2. The General Rule<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-3\">The general rule according to law is that <b>everybody is fully capable of entering into a contract<\/b>. That is the general principle.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-4\">However, the law is living — it is not static. It evolves with man. When you have general rules, there are always exceptions. Why? Because the law evolves with society. What applied yesterday may not suffice for the present. The law will find exceptions so that there will be equity and justice. The purpose of law is for equity and for justice. Man is not made for law — law is made for man. So we do not let law stand the way it is; we keep improving on it, and it is the improvement of the law that brings about exceptions.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-4-2\">3. Exceptions — Persons with Limited Capacity<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-5\">If we say everybody can enter into a contract, there are some people who do not have the prerequisites — they do not have the mental ability or the social ability to be a party to a contract. The law says there is a possibility that those people are victims, and so, in the process of trying to protect those people, we have exceptions.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-6\">The law regards certain <b>classes of persons as needing protection<\/b>, so certain limitations are imposed upon their rights to contract. These persons include:<\/p><ul><li id=\"cil-class-4-p0-7\"><b>Infants<\/b> (children / minors)<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-4-p0-8\"><b>Insane persons<\/b> (mentally disordered persons)<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-4-p0-9\"><b>Drunken persons<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-class-4-p0-10\"><b>Illiterate persons<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-class-4-p0-11\"><b>Corporations<\/b><\/li><\/ul><h2 id=\"cil-class-4-3\">4. Infants (Minors)<\/h2><h3 id=\"cil-class-4-4\">A. Who Is an Infant in Law?<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-12\">In contractual matters, an infant in Nigeria is a person under the age of 21 years under the common law. Common law has traditionally afforded protection to this category of persons from entering into obligations that they would not have were they more experienced. The degree of protection depends on the category of contract involved.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-4-5\">B. The Age of Majority — 21 or 18?<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-13\">The answer to the question 'who is a child?' depends on the context — criminal, marriage, voting, driving, religious, or cultural. The National Child Welfare Policy of 1989 defines a child as anybody who is 12 years or below. The voting age is 18. In criminal law, a child is a person under 14, and young persons are between 14 and 17.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-14\">The focus in this class is on <b>contractual capacity<\/b>. Contracts made by infants are governed by the rules of common law as altered by the Infants Relief Act 1874. A child under common law is anyone below the age of 21.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-15\">However, the age of majority (maturity), which was formerly 21, had been reduced to 18 in Britain when it was realised that more and more people between 18 and 21 years were playing active roles in the commercial scene.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-16\"><b>Section 277 of the Child's Rights Act (2003)<\/b>, which has been adopted by 34 states in Nigeria, defines a 'child' as a person under the age of eighteen years. However, <b>Kano<\/b> and <b>Zamfara<\/b> have not yet domesticated the Act.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-4-6\">Relevant Cases<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-17\"><b>Folata v. Dowoma<\/b> <i>(1970) NWLR 105<\/i> — In this case, a youth sold a family house. It was held that under Muslim law, maturity is determined by physical development or a declaration of the youth in question, or by reaching the age of 15 lunar months.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-18\"><b>Labinjoh v. Abake<\/b> <i>(1974) 5 NLR 33<\/i> — The argument that contractual capacity in Nigeria should be when a person attains puberty or physical maturity was rejected. 18 years in contract of trade was held not necessary.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-19\"><b>Discussion Question from the Slide:<\/b> <i>Is the reduction of age of majority in Britain and Nigeria beneficial or<\/i> <i>prejudicial to a child? Will you advocate a further reduction especially in this computer age?<\/i><\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-4-7\">5. Types of Contracts Under Infant Capacity<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-20\">The fact that we said this set of people cannot be involved in contracts does not mean that they are strictly stopped from all contracts. There are categories of contracts that children can enter into, and ones that they obviously should not.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-21\"><b>Important:<\/b> It is <b>not a criminal offence<\/b> to enter into a contract with someone who is underage. It is only when criminal conduct is involved (e.g., fraud) that criminal law applies. Contracting with a minor is a <b>civil<\/b> matter, not a criminal one. Nobody will be jailed; the issue is about damages and enforceability.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-4-8\">(a) Contracts That Are Binding<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-22\">An infant is bound by the following contracts:<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-4-9\">(i) Contracts for Necessaries<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-23\">Necessaries have been defined as 'goods suitable to the condition in life of the infant and to his actual requirements at the time of sale and delivery.' The term covers essential things such as food, drink, clothing, and shelter. It also covers items which, though not strictly necessary, are appropriate to the infant's financial and social status.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-24\">So a person under 18 can go to Yaba market and buy clothing. A child can go to a restaurant and buy food. Some of you got an apartment in Part 1 when you were not even 18 — under the law, that is agreed upon because shelter is a necessary.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-4-10\">(ii) Beneficial Contracts of Service<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-25\">These are contracts under which an infant receives training or instruction for their future career. They are a form of necessaries in that by entering into a contract of service, apprenticeship, instruction, or training, the infant receives real benefit by being equipped to earn a livelihood.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-26\">The validity of such a contract is, however, subject to the condition that the contract as a whole is substantially to the infant's advantage and benefit. It is possible that while some of the terms may appear to exploit the infant's inexperience, others may be manifestly to his benefit.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-27\">Examples include: going to school (education is a beneficial contract), apprenticeships, learning tailoring, carpentry, and other trades. These are contracts you can enter into as a younger person below 18 because they benefit you in the future.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-4-11\">(b) Voidable Contracts<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-28\">These are contracts in respect of which an infant acquires an interest in property of a permanent nature, to which continuing obligations attach. Examples include <b>leases<\/b>, <b>partnerships<\/b>, and <b>contracts for the<\/b> <b>purchase of shares<\/b> in a company.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-29\">The general rule is that such contracts are regarded as valid and binding on an infant unless they are repudiated either during infancy or within a <b>reasonable time of reaching his majority<\/b>.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-30\">The validity of the contract is in the hands of the child. If a 15-year-old girl or 16-year-old boy buys shares or leases a property, the contract will be valid until the child says 'I do not want to go ahead anymore — I want my money back.' The other party has no option but to return the money. This is because the law is trying to protect the child — there are times a child is being taken advantage of.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-31\">When the child reaches the age of majority (18 years), they must <b>ratify<\/b> the contract — that is, come back and say 'I want to go ahead with it.' If they do not ratify within a reasonable time, or if they reject or refuse the contract, the contract will not go ahead, and the child can be free from liability.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-4-12\">Example — Sports Betting<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-32\">There was an issue on the internet where a 12-year-old boy won millions on SportyBet and they refused to credit him because he was under 18. The betting site's rules state that you must be 18 before you can bet. Should the child have been betting in the first place? They were collecting his money, yes, but was he supposed to have paid it? The betting site had clearly stated the age requirement. The law also encourages morality.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-4-13\">(c) Void Contracts<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-33\"><b>Section 1 of the Infant's Relief Act 1874<\/b> provides that the following contracts, if entered into by an infant, are <b>absolutely void<\/b> — null and void, with no basis whatsoever:<\/p><ul><li id=\"cil-class-4-p0-34\"><b>(a) Contracts for the payment of money lent or to be lent.<\/b> You cannot lend money to a child. If they come tomorrow and say they are not paying, they will not pay you. A child cannot borrow money from a bank — it is illegal.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-4-p0-35\"><b>(b) Contracts for goods supplied or to be supplied which are not necessaries.<\/b> You cannot buy and sell with a child if the goods are not necessaries (food, shelter, clothing, beneficial services).<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-4-p0-36\"><b>(c) Accounts stated<\/b> — i.e., an admission of debt or IOU. A child cannot sign an IOU or be involved in account settlements.<\/li><\/ul><h2 id=\"cil-class-4-14\">6. Other Persons with Limited Capacity (From Slides)<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-37\">The following categories were shown on the slides but will be covered in more detail in the next class. A brief summary from the slide content:<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-4-15\">A. Insane Persons<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-38\">Contracts made with mentally disordered persons are <i>prima facie<\/i> valid — that is, the insane person is bound by the contract unless he can prove: (i) that owing to his mental state he did not understand the nature of the contract, and (ii) that his condition was known to the other party. Where these two conditions are proved, the contract becomes voidable and can be set aside at the option of the insane person.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-39\">However, contracts entered into by a mentally disordered person during <b>lucid intervals<\/b> are valid and binding. Even while suffering from the disorder, such a person is liable for necessaries (goods and services suitable to his position in life), and the obligation is to pay a <b>reasonable price<\/b>.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-4-16\">B. Drunken Persons<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-40\">The rules governing a drunkard's contractual liability are similar to those for insane persons. Where a person enters into a contract in a state of drunkenness, the contract is voidable at the option of the drunkard. However, the contract can be expressly or impliedly ratified when the person returns to a state of soberness.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-4-17\">C. Corporations<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-41\">A company registered under the Companies and Allied Matters Decree 1990 is recognised to have a legal personality, independent of the persons who are its members. The <b>Ultra Vires Doctrine<\/b> provides that companies must operate within their stated objectives. Acts beyond their stated objects may be void.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-4-18\">7. Additional Slide Content — Waiver &amp; Promissory Estoppel<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-42\">The slides also contained the following topics which the lecturer briefly displayed:<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-4-19\">Waiver of a Contractual Right<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-43\">The rule that performance of a duty already owed to a promisor is not sufficient consideration applies to waiver of contractual rights as well. Thus, where a contracting party waives his rights under a contract, wholly or in part, the waiver is not binding unless consideration is given for it. This is better understood when discussed in relation to obligations created by debts. This is often referred to as the rule in <b>Pinnel's case<\/b>.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-4-20\">Promissory Estoppel<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-44\">The rule in Pinnel's case can be circumvented by the doctrine of <b>promissory estoppel<\/b>. Where a party has waived or promised to waive his contractual rights against another, and that other party has changed his position in reliance on the waiver or promise, it would be unjust to allow an action against him on the original contract to succeed. Equity is fairness, and so it does not look with favour at a man who promises relief to another and then goes back on the promise.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-4-21\">Exemption, Exclusion, or Limitation Clauses<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-45\">In entering into a contract, one of the parties may wish to escape from some obligations in certain events. The practice is to insert clauses which either exempt or exclude himself from such obligations, or simply limit the extent of his liability under the contract. In the spirit of freedom of contract, exclusion clauses are perfectly legitimate and the courts are normally prepared to uphold their validity. However, the courts will take into account the relative bargaining power of the parties.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-46\">Three requirements for valid exclusion clauses:<\/p><ul><li id=\"cil-class-4-p0-47\"><b>(a) Notice:<\/b> The document relied on as containing the exclusion clause must be a contractual document, not a voucher or receipt after payment.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-4-p0-48\"><b>(b) Notice must be given<\/b> at or before the time of contracting.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-4-p0-49\"><b>(c) Signature:<\/b> A party who signs a document is generally bound by its terms, including exclusion clauses.<\/li><\/ul><h2 id=\"cil-class-4-22\">8. Coming Up in the Next Class<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-50\">The next class will cover <b>restitution by an infant<\/b>, and more detailed treatment of insane persons, drunken persons, and corporations.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-4-23\">Summary — Capacity to Contract<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-51\"><b>Topic<\/b> <b>Key Points<\/b><\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-52\">General Rule Everybody is fully capable of entering into a contract.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-53\">Protected Classes<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-54\">Infants, insane persons, drunken persons, illiterate persons, corporations.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-55\">Age of Majority<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-56\">Common law: 21 years. Child's Rights Act 2003: 18 years. Kano and Zamfara have not domesticated the Act.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-57\">Binding Contracts (Infants)<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-58\">(i) Necessaries — food, clothing, shelter (ii) Beneficial contracts of service — education, apprenticeship<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-59\">Voidable Contracts (Infants)<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-60\">Shares, leases, partnerships. Valid unless repudiated during infancy or within reasonable time of reaching majority.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-61\">Void Contracts (Infants)<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-62\">S.1 Infant's Relief Act 1874: (a) Money lent (b) Non-necessary goods (c) IOUs<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-63\">Insane / Drunken<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-64\">Prima facie valid; voidable if condition proved and known to other party. Still liable for necessaries.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-65\">Corporations Legal personality; limited by Ultra Vires Doctrine.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-4-p0-66\"><i>These notes were compiled from a Zoom class transcript and lecture slides. Transcription errors have been corrected and slide<\/i> <i>content integrated. Lecturer: Folake Adewumi.<\/i><\/p>", "toc": [{"id": "cil-class-4-0", "t": "1. Introduction — What is Capacity to Contract?", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-4-1", "t": "2. The General Rule", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-4-2", "t": "3. Exceptions — Persons with Limited Capacity", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-4-3", "t": "4. Infants (Minors)", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-4-4", "t": "A. Who Is an Infant in Law?", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-4-5", "t": "B. The Age of Majority — 21 or 18?", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-4-6", "t": "Relevant Cases", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-4-7", "t": "5. Types of Contracts Under Infant Capacity", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-4-8", "t": "(a) Contracts That Are Binding", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-4-9", "t": "(i) Contracts for Necessaries", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-4-10", "t": "(ii) Beneficial Contracts of Service", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-4-11", "t": "(b) Voidable Contracts", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-4-12", "t": "Example — Sports Betting", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-4-13", "t": "(c) Void Contracts", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-4-14", "t": "6. Other Persons with Limited Capacity (From Slides)", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-4-15", "t": "A. Insane Persons", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-4-16", "t": "B. Drunken Persons", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-4-17", "t": "C. Corporations", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-4-18", "t": "7. Additional Slide Content — Waiver & Promissory Estoppel", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-4-19", "t": "Waiver of a Contractual Right", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-4-20", "t": "Promissory Estoppel", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-4-21", "t": "Exemption, Exclusion, or Limitation Clauses", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-4-22", "t": "8. Coming Up in the Next Class", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-4-23", "t": "Summary — Capacity to Contract", "lv": 2}]}]}, "cil-class-5": {"course": "CIL", "ref": "Class 5", "title": "Capacity (contd.), Illiterate Persons & Privity of Contract", "kicker": "Class notes · 16 June 2026 · Folake Adewumi", "words": 1139, "parts": [{"label": "", "note": "", "src": "Class 5 - CIL_Law_of_Engineering_Contract_Class5_Notes.pdf", "html": "<h2 id=\"cil-class-5-0\">1. Infants / Minors — Recap<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-1\">The general rule when it comes to infants is that they do not have the right to contract. Know the case study of <b>Labinjoh v. Abake<\/b>.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-5-1\">Contracts That Are Binding on Infants<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-class-5-p0-2\"><b>Contracts for Necessaries:<\/b> Includes food, clothing, and shelter.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-5-p0-3\"><b>Contracts of Service / Education (Apprenticeship):<\/b> Training contracts that benefit the minor's future.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-class-5-2\">Voidable Contracts<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-4\">Contracts for lease, buying shares, and similar interests are <b>voidable<\/b>. Being voidable means the minor can terminate at any point in time.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-5\">When a minor comes to lease a property from you, you can enter into a contract signed by the <b>minor's<\/b> <b>guardian<\/b> — so that the minor does not come all of a sudden to void the contract.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-5-3\">When a Child Commits an Offence<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-6\">When a child commits a common offence, the law allows it to go, then equity checks for justice (a kind of restitution). Below 12 years, the approach is <b>deterrent<\/b> (warning/correction). From 12 and above, the child is classified as a <b>juvenile<\/b>.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-5-4\">2. Insane Persons<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-7\">Insane persons may have signed their contract in a <b>lucid state<\/b>. The general rule is that an insane person <b>can<\/b> enter into a contract.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-8\">As with other areas of law, there is a general rule and an exception. The exception is: the insane person can prove to the court that he was insane when he signed the contract. A <b>neurologist and psychiatrist<\/b> can test for his current state before bringing him to court. Additionally, the insane person must prove that <b>the other<\/b> <b>party was aware of his condition<\/b>.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-5-5\">What to Do When an Insane Person Approaches to Enter a Contract<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-9\">If an insane person approaches you to enter into a contract, what should you do?<\/p><ul><li id=\"cil-class-5-p0-10\">Bring a <b>psychiatrist<\/b> to assess their mental state.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-5-p0-11\">Bring their <b>next of kin<\/b>.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-5-p0-12\">Bring a person whom they can <b>transfer their roles to<\/b> (a representative).<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-5-p0-13\">Bring <b>third-party witnesses<\/b>.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-class-5-6\">If Two Insane Persons Enter Into a Contract<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-14\">If two insane persons enter into a contract with each other, they can either <b>rectify and ratify<\/b> the contract, or <b>nullify<\/b> it entirely.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-5-7\">3. Drunken Persons<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-15\">The same right that an insane person enjoys, a <b>drunkard can also enjoy<\/b> — that is, if he can prove that he was drunk at the time the contract was signed. The contract becomes voidable at the option of the drunkard.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-5-8\">4. Corporations / Companies<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-16\">A company can be contracted with, but with some exceptions. There is something called <b>ultra vires<\/b>. When you see and find out that a company is going outside of their stated objectives, and they cannot deliver on what they promised, you can <b>nullify or void<\/b> the contract.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-5-9\">5. Illiterate Persons<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-17\">An illiterate is a person who <b>cannot read or write in the language of the contract<\/b>. Special protections and procedures apply when contracting with illiterate persons.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-5-10\">The Illiterate Jurat — Procedure for Contracting with Illiterates<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-18\">There is a person known as the <b>writer<\/b> (or jurat writer) — a person who writes the contract on behalf of the illiterate. The writer must:<\/p><ul><li id=\"cil-class-5-p0-19\"><b>Write the name, thumbprint, and address<\/b> of the illiterate party (the illiterate jurat), so as to find them if anything happens.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-5-p0-20\"><b>Write exactly what the illiterate should do<\/b> under the contract.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-5-p0-21\"><b>Read out and explain<\/b> the entire contract to the illiterate, and ask if that is what they want.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-5-p0-22\"><b>Put a witness<\/b> (and ideally a third party) to witness the signing of the contract. A <b>video recording<\/b> is also advisable.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-class-5-11\">Consequences and Rules Regarding Illiterate Contracts<\/h3><h3 id=\"cil-class-5-12\">Consequence 1: Writer Must Not Benefit<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-23\">If the writer is the one benefiting the most from the contract, the contract is <b>void<\/b>. The writer is supposed to be a neutral party who writes, explains, and ensures the illiterate understands. The writer should not be the one gaining from the arrangement.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-5-13\">Consequence 2: Third-Party Rights<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-24\">If the document creates rights between a third party and the illiterate, the contract is <b>enforceable<\/b> — but only if the third party can prove to the court that the illiterate was aware of the contract. This is possible provided the writer is not the one benefiting.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-25\">For example, if you enter a contract with an illiterate but the illiterate did not sign, the writer can get a third party who was present at the time to prove that the contract was explained and agreed upon.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-5-14\">Consequence 3: Illiterate Can Enforce<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-26\">The illiterate can also <b>enforce the document<\/b> — if he can call evidence to prove what happened at the time the document was prepared. For example, in a contract between two illiterates, the law can be more lenient because neither party could read the terms.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-5-15\">Penalty for Non-Compliance<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-27\">The fine for failing to follow the illiterate jurat procedure is <b>100 naira<\/b>, or an option of <b>6 months<\/b> <b>imprisonment<\/b>.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-5-16\">6. Privity of Contract<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-28\">If Mr. A and Mr. B enter into a contract, they are the <b>only ones<\/b> who should benefit from the contract or seek redress. A third party has <b>no right<\/b> to benefit from or seek redress under the contract.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-5-17\">Exceptions to Privity of Contract<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-29\">The following are recognised exceptions where a third party can have rights:<\/p><ul><li id=\"cil-class-5-p0-30\"><b>Next of Kin / Property Law Cases:<\/b> When the children obtain ownership or letters of administration from the government to administer property left by deceased parents.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-5-p0-31\"><b>Transfer of Ownership:<\/b> When a transfer of ownership is made (assignment).<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-5-p0-32\"><b>Third-Party Insurance Contract:<\/b> The injured third party can claim directly from the insurance company.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-5-p0-33\"><b>Trusteeship:<\/b> When a person (trustee) can access a property on behalf of beneficiaries (e.g., inheritance/estates).<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-5-p0-34\"><b>Contractual Provision for Third Party:<\/b> When you include in your contract that the benefit should go to another person if you are not capable.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-5-p0-35\"><b>Agencies:<\/b> An agent acting on behalf of a principal can bind the principal to the contract.<\/li><\/ul><h2 id=\"cil-class-5-18\">Summary — Class 5 at a Glance<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-36\"><b>Topic<\/b> <b>Key Points<\/b><\/p><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-37\">Infants (Recap)<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-38\">No right to contract. Binding: necessaries + service/education. Voidable: lease, shares (guardian can sign). Juvenile from 12.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-39\">Insane Persons<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-40\">General rule: can contract. Exception: must prove insanity at time of signing + other party knew. Bring psychiatrist, next of kin, witnesses.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-41\">Drunken Persons<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-42\">Same rights as insane persons. Must prove drunkenness at time of contract.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-43\">Corporations Can be contracted with. Ultra vires: if company acts outside stated objectives, contract can be nullified/voided.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-44\">Illiterate Persons<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-45\">Cannot read/write in contract language. Illiterate jurat: writer records name, thumbprint, address; reads out and explains; witness + video. Writer must NOT benefit. Penalty: I100 fine or 6 months imprisonment.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-46\"><b>Topic<\/b> <b>Key Points<\/b><\/p><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-47\">Privity of Contract<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-48\">Only parties to the contract can benefit or seek redress. Exceptions: next of kin/property, transfer of ownership, third-party insurance, trusteeship, contractual provision for third party, agencies.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-5-p0-49\"><i>These notes were reconstructed from handwritten class notes (dated 16/6/2026). Content has been structured and corrected for<\/i> <i>clarity. Lecturer: Folake Adewumi.<\/i><\/p>", "toc": [{"id": "cil-class-5-0", "t": "1. Infants / Minors — Recap", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-5-1", "t": "Contracts That Are Binding on Infants", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-5-2", "t": "Voidable Contracts", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-5-3", "t": "When a Child Commits an Offence", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-5-4", "t": "2. Insane Persons", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-5-5", "t": "What to Do When an Insane Person Approaches to Enter a Contract", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-5-6", "t": "If Two Insane Persons Enter Into a Contract", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-5-7", "t": "3. Drunken Persons", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-5-8", "t": "4. Corporations / Companies", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-5-9", "t": "5. Illiterate Persons", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-5-10", "t": "The Illiterate Jurat — Procedure for Contracting with Illiterates", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-5-11", "t": "Consequences and Rules Regarding Illiterate Contracts", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-5-12", "t": "Consequence 1: Writer Must Not Benefit", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-5-13", "t": "Consequence 2: Third-Party Rights", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-5-14", "t": "Consequence 3: Illiterate Can Enforce", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-5-15", "t": "Penalty for Non-Compliance", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-5-16", "t": "6. Privity of Contract", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-5-17", "t": "Exceptions to Privity of Contract", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-5-18", "t": "Summary — Class 5 at a Glance", "lv": 2}]}]}, "cil-class-6": {"course": "CIL", "ref": "Class 6", "title": "Privity of Contract & Vitiating Elements (Mistake)", "kicker": "Class notes · Folake Adewumi · Delivered via Zoom / YouTube Live", "words": 1906, "parts": [{"label": "", "note": "", "src": "Class 6 - CIL_Law_of_Engineering_Contract_Class6_Notes.pdf", "html": "<h2 id=\"cil-class-6-0\">1. Privity of Contract — Recap and Scenario<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-1\">Privity of contract was introduced in the previous class (Class 5). The general rule is that a person who is not a party to a contract (a stranger to the contract) cannot receive the benefits or bear the obligations under it. A third party cannot enforce the agreement against A or B because they were not a party to the agreement.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-6-1\">Scenario: Yaba Local Government Water Tank<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-2\">The Yaba Local Government Council (<b>Party A<\/b>) entered into a contract with a large construction company (<b>Party B<\/b>). Party B purchased a high-capacity automated water tank from a manufacturer (<b>Party C<\/b>). The contract between B and C guaranteed that the tank would operate at at least 95% efficiency. After installation, the tank frequently broke down, reducing efficiency to 60%. This caused Party A to lose a lot of money — they had to hire tankers to bring in water, which affected their budget.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-3\"><b>Question:<\/b> Can Party A directly sue Party C for breach of contract?<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-4\"><b>Answer: No.<\/b> Party C is a stranger to the contract with Party A. The contract was between A and B, and a separate contract was between B and C. Party A is not in direct contract with Party C. This is the principle of privity of contract.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-5\"><b>The proper remedy:<\/b> Party A sues Party B (because they were in direct contract). Then Party B's lawyer applies to the court to join Party C as a co-defendant. This way, all necessary parties and evidence are in one case, avoiding duplicate litigation and wasting taxpayers' money.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-6-2\">2. Exceptions to the Doctrine of Privity of Contract<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-6\">Strict adherence to the doctrine of privity has been found to cause hardship, especially in commercial practice. The following exceptions have been recognised:<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-6-3\">(a) Agency<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-7\">Under the law of agency, a person (the <b>agent<\/b>) can enter into a contract on behalf of another person (the <b>principal<\/b>). Whatever the agent does is deemed as if the principal did it. The agent is just an intermediary acting on your behalf. Your letter of authority speaks for you.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-8\">The agent does not have to use the principal's name. Even if they sign in their own name, because they have authority from the principal, the principal is bound. If you sue the agent, it is like suing the same person twice — the agent would typically be a witness in the case, not a party.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-9\"><b>Scenario — Alex the Project Manager:<\/b> Alex, an automation engineer, is hired as project manager by TechCorp. TechCorp gives Alex explicit authority to acquire necessary components. Alex signs a contract with a robotic supplier for three robotic arms. When the arms are delivered, TechCorp refuses to pay, claiming they never directly signed a contract with the supplier. <b>Can the supplier sue TechCorp?<\/b> Yes,<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-10\">because Alex was acting as an agent with authority from TechCorp. Whatever Alex did is deemed as if TechCorp did it.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-6-4\">(b) Trusteeship<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-11\">A trust is the equitable assignment of the benefit of a contract by the party entitled to that benefit to a third party. If a grandparent creates a trust (e.g., a will) with a trustee, and the trustee does not do as directed — does not remit the benefits to the beneficiary — the beneficiary can sue the trustee, even though the beneficiary was not present when the will was signed.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-6-5\">(c) Assignment<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-12\">This relates especially to landed property. If Mr. A sells land to Mr. B, and after some years Mr. B sells it to Mr. C, Mr. C has rights over the property. If parents leave a property for their children and they are no longer around, the children can sue anyone who infringes on the property. The original company does not need to be aware of the assignment — it is not their business.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-6-6\">(d) Covenants Relating to Land<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-13\">Anything that has to do with land — covenants (promises attached to the land) run with the land and can be enforced by successors.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-6-7\">(e) Third-Party Insurance<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-14\">If you own a car and have insured it, and you cause an accident, the injured third party can apply directly to your insurance company without going through you. They just need your car registration number and other details that the insurance company holds.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-6-8\">(f) Statutory Exceptions<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-15\">A third party may take the benefit of a contract of which he was not a party. The Workmen's Compensation Act is an example — if an employee is a process defendant in a policy, he can bring a claim against the insurance company directly.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-6-9\">(g) Restrictive Covenants / Conditions Relating to Use of Land<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-16\">Restrictions relating to the use of land can bind subsequent purchasers even if they were not party to the original agreement.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-6-10\">3. Vitiating Elements — Introduction<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-17\">The <b>vitiating elements<\/b> are factors that can make a contract void or voidable. A party to a contract may make it void or of no manner of effect whatsoever.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-18\">The vitiating elements include:<\/p><ul><li id=\"cil-class-6-p0-19\"><b>Mistake<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-class-6-p0-20\"><b>Misrepresentation<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-class-6-p0-21\"><b>Duress<\/b> and <b>undue influence<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-class-6-p0-22\"><b>Illegality<\/b><\/li><\/ul><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-23\">For example, a contractor buys a car, thinking they are getting a good bargain. Later, a discovery is made that the car had been in a major accident and the frame was compromised. If it can be said that the car was<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-24\">structurally unsafe and the engineer could not have discovered this even with reasonable care, the effect is that no contract may have resulted.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-6-11\">4. Mistake in Contract Law<\/h2><h3 id=\"cil-class-6-12\">A. What Is (and Is Not) Mistake in Law<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-25\">Mistake in the legal sense is <b>more limited in meaning<\/b> than in general usage. In law, mistake relates to the <b>identity<\/b> of the subject matter or its <b>existence<\/b>.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-26\"><b>Analogy — Ladipo Market Engine:<\/b> You are an engineer and you go to Ladipo Market to buy a used generator engine. You see one that looks right, the price is good, and you buy it. At home, you discover it cannot generate as much power as you expected. In the normal sense, you made a mistake. But in law, <b>that<\/b> <b>is not a mistake<\/b>. You went to the market, you saw what you wanted, and you bought it. You got what you asked for.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-27\">It becomes a mistake in law when you <b>specifically told them<\/b> you wanted an engine with 500 capacity, and they gave you one with 300 capacity instead. You specified the identity, but they gave you a different one. <b>That<\/b> is mistake in law.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-28\">The law limits the scope of mistake so that you cannot claim for what you did not ask for. It would not be fair to the seller if you went back and said 'it did not do what I wanted' when you never told them what you wanted. The law is trying to ensure fairness, equity, and justice.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-6-13\">B. Mistake at Common Law<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-29\">Under common law, mistake generally renders a contract <b>void<\/b> — as though the contract never existed. Its effect is that no contract may have resulted. However, equity may try to find a remedy if the mistake does not affect the very foundation of the contract.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-6-14\">5. Types of Mistake<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-30\">Mistake in law manifests itself in three forms: <b>common mistake<\/b>, <b>mutual mistake<\/b>, and <b>unilateral mistake<\/b>.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-6-15\">(i) Common Mistake<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-31\">Common mistake occurs where <b>both parties share the same mistake<\/b>. In this case, the intentions of the parties are known to each other — they are looking at the same thing — but they are both wrong about something crucial.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-32\"><b>Example — Steel Beams:<\/b> An engineer and a supplier sign a contract for a specific batch of steel beams. Unknown to both parties, the warehouse where the beams were stored had caught fire the night before, and all the beams were destroyed. At the time of signing the contract, the subject matter of the contract no longer existed. Both parties made the same mistake — they both believed the beams still existed. Neither party is at fault. The contract is void because the thing they agreed upon was not existing at the time of the agreement.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-33\">In common mistake, <b>none of the parties are liable<\/b>. They both bear the loss. The thing was not existing at the time they signed the contract.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-6-16\">(ii) Mutual Mistake<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-34\">Mutual mistake occurs where <b>both parties make mistakes, but about different things<\/b>. They are at cross-purposes — each party is thinking of something different, and neither knows what the other truly intended.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-35\"><b>Example — MTN vs Airtel:<\/b> Your mother sends you to buy 200 naira MTN credit. You mistakenly buy 200 naira Airtel credit instead. On getting back, she says to return it immediately because she does not use Airtel. This is a mutual mistake — you bought something different from what was requested. The credit card itself works fine; it is the <b>identity<\/b> that is wrong. If you can prove you bought the wrong one and have not used it, the seller should be able to take it back.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-36\">Mutual mistake has a lot to do with <b>identifying<\/b> something, not with whether the subject matter itself works.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-6-17\">(iii) Unilateral Mistake<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-37\">Unilateral mistake occurs where <b>only one party is mistaken<\/b>, and the other party either knew or should have known about the mistake. The mistaken party can contain their contract. In other words, one party knows something that the other party does not.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-38\"><b>Example — Solar Grid Bid Error:<\/b> An electrical engineer is submitting a digital bid to install a solar grid. While copying and pasting data in a spreadsheet, a formula breaks, accidentally dropping a zero from the total price — making the bid 50,000 naira instead of 500,000 naira. The client notices the error and knows it is a calculation mistake, but immediately clicks 'Accept Bid' to trap the engineer into a low price.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-39\"><b>Can the engineer be forced to complete the contract at 50,000?<\/b> No. This is a unilateral mistake. The client knew the bid was an error and took advantage of it. The engineer can have the contract set aside.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-6-18\">Summary — Types of Mistake<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-40\"><b>Type<\/b> <b>Who Is Mistaken?<\/b> <b>About What?<\/b> <b>Example<\/b><\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-41\">Common Mistake<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-42\">Both parties (same mistake)<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-43\">Something crucial; subject may not exist<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-44\">Steel beams destroyed in warehouse fire<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-45\">Mutual Mistake<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-46\">Both parties (different mistakes)<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-47\">Identity of the subject matter<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-48\">Buying Airtel credit instead of MTN<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-49\">Unilateral Mistake<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-50\">Only one party; other party knows<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-51\">One party is mistaken and the other exploits it<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-52\">Engineer's bid error: 50k instead of 500k<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-6-19\">Full Class Summary<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-53\"><b>Topic<\/b> <b>Key Points<\/b><\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-54\">Privity of Contract<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-55\">Only parties to a contract can sue or be sued under it. A sues B, then B joins C as co-defendant.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-56\">Exceptions to Privity<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-57\">Agency, trusteeship, assignment, covenants relating to land, third-party insurance, statutory exceptions.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-58\">Vitiating Elements<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-59\">Factors that make a contract void/voidable: mistake, misrepresentation, duress, undue influence, illegality.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-60\"><b>Topic<\/b> <b>Key Points<\/b><\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-61\">Mistake in Law<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-62\">More limited than everyday meaning. Relates to identity or existence. Must specify what you want to claim mistake.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-63\">Common Mistake<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-64\">Both parties share the exact same mistake. Subject matter may not exist. Contract is void. No party is at fault.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-65\">Mutual Mistake<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-66\">Both parties mistaken about different things (cross-purposes). Relates to identity of subject matter.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-67\">Unilateral Mistake<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-68\">Only one party mistaken; the other knows/exploits it. Contract can be set aside.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-6-p0-69\"><i>These notes were compiled from a YouTube live class (SRT subtitles) and lecture slides. Lecturer: Folake Adewumi. Transcription<\/i> <i>errors corrected and slide content integrated.<\/i><\/p>", "toc": [{"id": "cil-class-6-0", "t": "1. Privity of Contract — Recap and Scenario", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-6-1", "t": "Scenario: Yaba Local Government Water Tank", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-6-2", "t": "2. Exceptions to the Doctrine of Privity of Contract", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-6-3", "t": "(a) Agency", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-6-4", "t": "(b) Trusteeship", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-6-5", "t": "(c) Assignment", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-6-6", "t": "(d) Covenants Relating to Land", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-6-7", "t": "(e) Third-Party Insurance", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-6-8", "t": "(f) Statutory Exceptions", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-6-9", "t": "(g) Restrictive Covenants / Conditions Relating to Use of Land", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-6-10", "t": "3. Vitiating Elements — Introduction", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-6-11", "t": "4. Mistake in Contract Law", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-6-12", "t": "A. What Is (and Is Not) Mistake in Law", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-6-13", "t": "B. Mistake at Common Law", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-6-14", "t": "5. Types of Mistake", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-6-15", "t": "(i) Common Mistake", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-6-16", "t": "(ii) Mutual Mistake", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-6-17", "t": "(iii) Unilateral Mistake", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-6-18", "t": "Summary — Types of Mistake", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-6-19", "t": "Full Class Summary", "lv": 2}]}]}, "cil-class-7": {"course": "CIL", "ref": "Class 7", "title": "Misrepresentation", "kicker": "Class notes · 2 July 2026 · Folake Adewumi", "words": 1531, "parts": [{"label": "", "note": "", "src": "Class 7 - CIL_Law_of_Engineering_Contract_Class7_Notes.pdf", "html": "<h2 id=\"cil-class-7-0\">1. Opening Scenario — AI Navigation Vans<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-1\">A company pitches AI-navigation vans to a transport firm. During the pitch, the company states: 'Our AI navigation system is fully capable of navigating without any human intervention. We tested it extensively in Jos under extreme weather conditions — the cold, the rain — and it was working perfectly.' Impressed, the firm signs a <b>10 billion naira<\/b> contract.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-2\">Three months later, during the very first rain, three vans lose track of the road and crash into ditches, causing massive traffic chaos. When the black box data is downloaded, two shocking things are discovered: (1) the AI sensor goes blind when water drops on the sensor casing, and (2) the manufacturers <b>never actually tested<\/b> <b>it in Jos<\/b> — they only simulated the weather conditions on a computer in a lab.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-3\"><b>Can the buyer tear up the contract and get their 10 billion naira back?<\/b> The statements were made during the pitch — before the contract — and were not written into the terms of the contract. This is the topic of today's class: <b>misrepresentation<\/b>.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-7-1\">2. What is Misrepresentation?<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-4\">Misrepresentation is an <b>untrue statement<\/b> made by <b>one party<\/b> to a contract, to <b>the other<\/b>, <b>before or at the<\/b> <b>time of contracting<\/b>, with regard to some <b>existing fact<\/b> or to some <b>past event<\/b>, which is one of the causes that <b>induced<\/b> the other party to enter into the contract.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-5\">Statements made at the initial stage of a contract are called <b>representations<\/b>. When those representations turn out to be wrong, they become <b>misrepresentations<\/b>. Even if those things are not written in the terms of the contract, you can be sued.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-6\">You have to watch what you say. As an engineer, if you want to get a deal or contract, you may start saying things that indirectly or later you will not or cannot do. The law holds you accountable for those statements.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-7-2\">3. Effect of Misrepresentation<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-7\">If misrepresentation is detected, the resultant effect is that the contract can either be <b>void<\/b> or <b>voidable<\/b>.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-7-3\">4. Conditions for Misrepresentation<\/h2><h3 id=\"cil-class-7-4\">(a) Must Be of Fact, Not of Law<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-8\">A misrepresentation must be a statement of <b>fact<\/b>, not of <b>law<\/b>. Citizens are expected to know the law. If someone tells you 'the Lagos Land Use Act says you can build a three-storey building here' when in reality only one storey is allowed, and you relied on that — the law does not consider this misrepresentation<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-9\">because you should have checked the law yourself.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-7-5\">(b) Must Be of Fact, Not of Opinion or Intention<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-10\">If someone says 'I <i>think<\/i> you can build three storeys' — that is an opinion, and you should not rely on it. Similarly, if someone says 'I <i>intend<\/i> to give you this house' or 'I <i>promise<\/i> to renovate the property' — those are statements of intention, not of fact. If they do not follow through, it is not misrepresentation. If someone wants to give you something, <b>let it be written down<\/b> — a deed of gift, for instance.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-7-6\">(c) Statement Must Be Addressed to the Party with Intention to Induce<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-11\">The statement must have been addressed to the specific person with the intention to induce them to enter into the contract.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-7-7\">5. Silence and Misrepresentation<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-12\"><b>General rule:<\/b> Silence does not amount to misrepresentation. If you assumed something and the other party simply stayed quiet, that is not their problem — it is business. You should do your own investigation.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-13\"><b>Example:<\/b> You want to buy land for your company. You tell the seller 'I heard there will be an expressway near this axis.' The seller stays quiet. You buy, only to discover the expressway is very far. The seller's silence is not misrepresentation.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-7-8\">Exceptions — Contracts of Utmost Good Faith (Uberrimae Fidei)<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-14\">In certain types of contracts, silence <b>can<\/b> amount to misrepresentation:<\/p><ul><li id=\"cil-class-7-p0-15\"><b>Insurance contracts<\/b> — you must disclose everything; what you do not tell them, you cannot hold against them later.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-7-p0-16\"><b>Land acquisition<\/b> — if you are not the owner, you must not stay silent and sell property that is not yours.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-7-p0-17\"><b>Shares<\/b> — material information must be disclosed.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-7-p0-18\"><b>Position of credit<\/b> — full disclosure required.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-7-p0-19\"><b>Family arrangements<\/b> — silence in family deals is at your own peril.<\/li><\/ul><h2 id=\"cil-class-7-9\">6. Three Types of Misrepresentation<\/h2><h3 id=\"cil-class-7-10\">(i) Fraudulent Misrepresentation<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-20\">A misrepresentation is fraudulent when the false statement is made <b>knowingly<\/b>, <b>without believing in its<\/b> <b>truth<\/b>, or <b>recklessly/carelessly<\/b> as to whether it is true or false. The focus is on <b>intention<\/b> — the person intended to make a false statement to deceive the other party.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-21\"><b>Case Example — Property No. 19 vs No. 21:<\/b> An agent wanted to sell property No. 19, but described it to the buyer using the specifications of property No. 21 (which matched what the buyer wanted). They signed the agreement for No. 21. There was already a court decision that had assigned No. 21 to another person, which the seller failed to disclose. The court found this was a pure level of fraud — the agent knowingly misrepresented which property was being sold.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-22\"><b>Remedy: Rescission + Damages<\/b><\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-7-11\">(ii) Innocent Misrepresentation<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-23\">Innocent misrepresentation arises where the party making the statement has <b>no intention to deceive<\/b> and has an <b>honest belief<\/b> in the truth of the statement.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-24\"><b>Example — Machine Labelled 2024:<\/b> You wanted a 2024 machine. The supplier told you it was a 2024 model. But there was a labelling error by the manufacturer — it was actually made in 2021, with the label mistakenly showing 2024. The supplier genuinely believed it was a 2024 model. The supplier should not be punished for what they did not do deliberately.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-25\"><b>Remedy: Rescission + Indemnity<\/b> (NO damages)<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-26\">Common law did not provide damages for innocent misrepresentation. Equity provides <b>indemnity<\/b> instead.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-7-12\">(iii) Negligent Misrepresentation<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-27\">A negligent misrepresentation is one made <b>carelessly<\/b>, or without reasonable grounds to believe it to be true. It often manifests in <b>fiduciary or professional relationships<\/b> where trust is placed in the person making the statement.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-28\"><b>Example — Civil Engineer and Bridge:<\/b> A civil engineer tells a client that a bridge can support 50 tons, but he used outdated formulas and skipped the mandatory safety recalculation. As a professional, you cannot afford to be careless — such mistakes can cost lives. People who give you the job trust you.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-29\"><b>Remedy: Rescission + Damages<\/b><\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-30\"><b>Type<\/b> <b>Intention<\/b> <b>Example<\/b> <b>Remedy<\/b><\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-31\">Fraudulent Knows it is false; intends to deceive<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-32\">Agent describes property No. 19 as No. 21<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-33\">Rescission + Damages<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-34\">Innocent No intention to deceive; honest belief<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-35\">Machine labelled 2024 but actually 2021<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-36\">Rescission + Indemnity (NO damages)<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-37\">Negligent Careless; no reasonable grounds to believe true<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-38\">Engineer uses outdated formulas for bridge<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-39\">Rescission + Damages<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-7-13\">7. Remedies Explained<\/h2><h3 id=\"cil-class-7-14\">Rescission<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-40\">The purpose of rescission is to <b>restore the parties to their status quo<\/b> — the position they were in before the contract ever occurred. The person who paid money gets their money back; the person who supplied goods gets their goods back. It is as if the contract never happened.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-7-15\">Damages<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-41\">Damages are monetary compensation for losses suffered as a result of the misrepresentation. They can include medical costs, emotional trauma, and exemplary damages (to make an example of the wrongdoer). Damages can even exceed the original contract value — for example, a product worth 2 million naira could result in damages of 50 million naira depending on the harm caused.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-42\">Damages are available for <b>fraudulent and negligent<\/b> misrepresentation. They are <b>NOT available<\/b> for innocent misrepresentation.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-7-16\">Indemnity<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-43\">Indemnity is an equitable remedy that covers <b>foreseeable expenses<\/b> incurred in reliance on the contract. It applies where damages are not available (i.e., innocent misrepresentation).<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-44\"><b>Example:<\/b> The item you want to buy is in Sokoto. You spend 500,000 naira on logistics to go and pick it up. On arrival, you discover it does not meet specifications. Even though the misrepresentation was innocent, equity says it is not fair for you to lose the 500,000 naira you spent on logistics. Indemnity covers that cost.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-45\">However, indemnity only covers what was <b>foreseeable<\/b> at the time of the contract. If you then shipped the item to America and employed people there — those costs were not foreseeable and are NOT covered by indemnity.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-7-17\">Summary — Class 7 at a Glance<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-46\"><b>Topic<\/b> <b>Key Points<\/b><\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-47\">Definition Untrue statement by one party to the other, before/at time of contracting, regarding existing fact/past event, that induced the other to enter into the contract.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-48\">Effect Contract can be void or voidable.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-49\">Conditions Must be of fact (not law, opinion, or intention). Must be addressed to the party with intent to induce.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-50\">Silence Generally NOT misrepresentation. Exceptions: insurance, land acquisition, shares, credit, family arrangements.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-51\">Fraudulent Knowingly false, no belief in truth, reckless. Remedy: Rescission + Damages.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-52\">Innocent Honest belief, no intention to deceive. Remedy: Rescission + Indemnity (no damages).<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-53\">Negligent Careless, no reasonable grounds to believe true. Remedy: Rescission + Damages.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-54\">Rescission Restores parties to pre-contract position (status quo ante).<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-55\">Damages Monetary compensation; can exceed contract value. Available for fraudulent and negligent only.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-56\">Indemnity Equitable remedy: foreseeable expenses only. Available for innocent misrepresentation.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-7-p0-57\"><i>These notes were compiled from a class transcript (July 2, 2026). Transcription errors corrected and content structured for clarity.<\/i> <i>Lecturer: Folake Adewumi.<\/i><\/p>", "toc": [{"id": "cil-class-7-0", "t": "1. Opening Scenario — AI Navigation Vans", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-7-1", "t": "2. What is Misrepresentation?", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-7-2", "t": "3. Effect of Misrepresentation", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-7-3", "t": "4. Conditions for Misrepresentation", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-7-4", "t": "(a) Must Be of Fact, Not of Law", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-7-5", "t": "(b) Must Be of Fact, Not of Opinion or Intention", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-7-6", "t": "(c) Statement Must Be Addressed to the Party with Intention to Induce", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-7-7", "t": "5. Silence and Misrepresentation", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-7-8", "t": "Exceptions — Contracts of Utmost Good Faith (Uberrimae Fidei)", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-7-9", "t": "6. Three Types of Misrepresentation", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-7-10", "t": "(i) Fraudulent Misrepresentation", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-7-11", "t": "(ii) Innocent Misrepresentation", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-7-12", "t": "(iii) Negligent Misrepresentation", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-7-13", "t": "7. Remedies Explained", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-7-14", "t": "Rescission", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-7-15", "t": "Damages", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-7-16", "t": "Indemnity", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-7-17", "t": "Summary — Class 7 at a Glance", "lv": 2}]}]}, "cil-class-8": {"course": "CIL", "ref": "Class 8", "title": "Duress, Undue Influence, Illegality & Void Contracts", "kicker": "Class notes · 30 July 2026", "words": 1355, "parts": [{"label": "", "note": "", "src": "Class 8 - CIL_Law_of_Engineering_Contract_Class8_Notes.pdf", "html": "<h2 id=\"cil-class-8-0\">1. Recap — Elements of a Valid Contract<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-1\">The elements of a valid contract are: <b>offer<\/b>, <b>acceptance<\/b> (which must be unequivocal), <b>consideration<\/b> (which must be furnished by both parties), <b>capacity<\/b> to contract, and <b>intention to create a legal relationship<\/b>. When all of these elements are present, you have a valid and binding contract.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-2\">However, even when all these elements are present, there are <b>vitiating elements<\/b> that can render the contract void or voidable. The vitiating elements discussed so far include <b>mistake<\/b> and <b>misrepresentation<\/b>. Today we add <b>duress and undue influence<\/b>, and <b>illegality<\/b>.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-8-1\">Voidable vs. Void — Quick Distinction<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-3\"><b>Voidable:<\/b> The contract exists but the aggrieved party has the option to either continue with it or set it aside (rescission). For example, if Emmanuel posts on his WhatsApp status 'I want to sell one of my cars,' and a buyer sends money thinking it is the Lexus but Emmanuel intended to sell the Volvo — the buyer might decide to keep the Volvo (perhaps use the extra money to buy something else) or demand rescission. The choice is the aggrieved party's.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-4\"><b>Void:<\/b> The contract is void from inception. It can never be valid. There is an illegality in it. No court will enforce it.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-8-2\">2. Duress<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-5\">Duress is a vitiating element that affects the validity of a contract. Every contract should reflect <b>consensus<\/b> <b>ad idem<\/b> — a meeting of the minds. Both parties must agree willingly. If at the time you were making your acceptance, there was a gun behind you — that is duress. The consent was not freely given.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-6\">There is a principle in law: <i><b>Non est factum<\/b><\/i> — when you sign a document, you are bound by its terms. However, if you signed under duress (under threat of violence, coercion, or physical harm), the contract is voidable at your option because the essential element of free consent was absent.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-7\">While duress may not necessarily involve moral turpitude or criminal acts, the law refuses to enforce the contract because it lacks the essential contractual element of voluntary consent.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-8-3\">3. Undue Influence<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-8\">Undue influence is closely related to duress but is more subtle. It occurs when one party uses their position of power or authority over the other to obtain consent.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-9\"><b>Example:<\/b> You are in your chairman's office. A document is placed in front of you. The chairman is on a phone call. There is a certain silence and pressure in the room. You feel compelled to sign. Was that a voluntary contract freely entered into? There was no physical threat, but the power dynamic influenced your<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-10\">decision.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-11\">The consensus ad idem — the meeting of the minds — must be genuine. If consent was obtained through the exercise of undue influence, the contract is voidable.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-8-4\">4. Professional Duty and Negligent Advice<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-12\">As professionals — whether engineers, lawyers, or other experts — if a party comes to you for professional advice and you are not sure of the answer, <b>do not give an immediate answer<\/b>. Say 'Can I get back to you on that?' You can then consult your colleagues, do your research, and confirm the correct position before advising.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-13\">In countries abroad — the UK, Canada, and the US — professionals have lost their licences permanently for giving negligent advice. Their right to practise has been completely taken away. So as an engineer, if you give non-professional advice and there is a problem, you risk losing your licence.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-8-5\">5. Illegal Contracts<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-14\">Something becomes illegal <b>only when the law says it is illegal<\/b>. If the law does not prohibit it, it is not illegal.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-8-6\">Definition<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-15\">An illegal contract is a contract that involves the <b>commission of a crime<\/b>, a <b>civil wrong<\/b>, or an act <b>contrary<\/b> <b>to public policy<\/b>, or which violates an <b>explicit statutory provision<\/b>.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-8-7\">Latin Maxim<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-16\"><i><b>Ex turpi causa non oritur actio<\/b><\/i> — no action arises from a dishonorable cause. Courts will refuse to enforce an illegal contract. No court will grant relief on an illegal contract.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-8-8\">Scenario — Election Rigging<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-17\">Victor wants to contest a senatorial seat. He approaches Barnabas and says 'I will give you 10 million naira — 5 million upfront, 5 million when the job is done — to rig the election in my favour.' Barnabas takes the 5 million but does not deliver. Victor cannot go to court to demand the balance or specific performance, because the contract is illegal and contrary to public policy. Courts will not enforce it.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-8-9\">In Pari Delicto — Both Parties Equally at Fault<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-18\">When both parties are <b>in pari delicto<\/b> (equally at fault), benefits received are generally <b>irrecoverable<\/b>. In the election rigging example, the 5 million naira already paid is gone — it cannot be recovered. The balance of 5 million is also gone. Both parties lose.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-8-10\">Examples of Illegal Contracts<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-class-8-p0-19\">Contracts to commit <b>murder<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-class-8-p0-20\">Contracts for <b>bribery<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-class-8-p0-21\">Contracts for <b>drug trafficking<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-class-8-p0-22\">Contracts involving <b>corruption in public procurement<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-class-8-p0-23\">Contracts for <b>prostitution<\/b> (prohibited by law)<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-8-p0-24\">Contracts for <b>tax evasion<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-class-8-p0-25\">Contracts that violate <b>statutory restrictions<\/b> (e.g., parking where the law says you cannot)<\/li><\/ul><h2 id=\"cil-class-8-11\">6. Void Contracts<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-26\">A void contract is one that is <b>void from inception<\/b>. From the very beginning, the contract has no legal effect. It is as if it never existed.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-8-12\">Examples of Void Contracts<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-class-8-p0-27\"><b>Agreements with a minor<\/b> — subject to exceptions (contracts for necessaries like food, clothing, shelter; and beneficial contracts of service like education).<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-8-p0-28\"><b>Contracts to perform an impossible act<\/b> — you cannot enforce rights in a void contract because the act itself is impossible.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-8-p0-29\"><b>Contracts without consideration<\/b> — subject to exceptions (deeds do not require consideration).<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-8-p0-30\"><b>Contracts based on mutual mistake<\/b> — where both parties are mistaken about a fundamental fact, the contract is void from inception.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-8-p0-31\"><b>Illegal contracts<\/b> — since they lack legal effect by definition.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-class-8-13\">Characteristics of Void Contracts<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-class-8-p0-32\">Void from inception — never had legal force.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-8-p0-33\">Do not necessarily involve criminal conduct — they are simply void.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-8-p0-34\">You cannot enforce rights under a void contract.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-8-p0-35\">Impossibility of performance or illegality can make a contract void.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-class-8-14\">Illegal vs. Void — Key Distinction<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-36\">Not all void contracts are illegal, and not all illegal contracts are void in the same way. Void contracts simply have no legal effect from the beginning (e.g., contracts with a minor for non-necessaries). Illegal contracts go further — they involve an act that the law actively prohibits, and courts will not only refuse to enforce them but may also impose penalties.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-8-15\">7. Importance of These Rules<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-37\">The rules on illegality and void contracts serve to:<\/p><ul><li id=\"cil-class-8-p0-38\"><b>Protect public interest<\/b> and criminal activities from being legitimised.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-8-p0-39\"><b>Protect innocent members<\/b> of society.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-8-p0-40\"><b>Encourage lawful business practices.<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-class-8-p0-41\"><b>Preserve judicial integrity<\/b> — courts should not be instruments of illegality.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-8-p0-42\"><b>Maintain economic order.<\/b><\/li><li id=\"cil-class-8-p0-43\"><b>Protect contractual fairness<\/b> and prevent abuse of legal processes.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-class-8-16\">Challenges in Nigeria<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-44\">The class discussed the following challenges to enforcement in Nigeria:<\/p><ul><li id=\"cil-class-8-p0-45\"><b>Corruption<\/b> — widespread in the system.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-8-p0-46\"><b>Insecurity<\/b> — affects commerce and contract enforcement.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-8-p0-47\"><b>Executive interference in the judiciary<\/b> — undermining independence of courts.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-8-p0-48\"><b>Lack of civic education<\/b> — many citizens do not know their rights.<\/li><\/ul><h2 id=\"cil-class-8-17\">Summary — Class 8 at a Glance<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-49\"><b>Topic<\/b> <b>Key Points<\/b><\/p><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-50\">Recap Elements: offer, acceptance, consideration, capacity, intention. Vitiating elements: mistake, misrepresentation, duress, illegality.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-51\">Voidable vs. Void<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-52\">Voidable: exists but can be set aside at aggrieved party's option. Void: void from inception; no legal effect; cannot be enforced.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-53\">Duress Consent obtained by force/threat. Consensus ad idem absent. Non est factum: signing = bound, but not if under duress.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-54\">Undue Influence<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-55\">Consent obtained through position of power. More subtle than duress. Contract voidable.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-56\">Professional Duty<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-57\">Do not give advice you are not sure of. Consult colleagues. Abroad: professionals lose licences for negligent advice.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-58\">Illegal Contracts<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-59\">Involves crime, civil wrong, or act contrary to public policy. Ex turpi causa non oritur actio. Courts will not enforce. In pari delicto: benefits irrecoverable.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-60\">Void Contracts<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-61\">Void from inception. Examples: minor (with exceptions), impossible acts, no consideration, mutual mistake, illegality.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-62\">Importance Protect public interest, innocent parties, judicial integrity, economic order. Nigeria challenges: corruption, insecurity.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-8-p0-63\"><i>These notes were compiled from a class transcript (July 30, 2026). Transcription errors corrected and content structured for clarity.<\/i><\/p>", "toc": [{"id": "cil-class-8-0", "t": "1. Recap — Elements of a Valid Contract", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-8-1", "t": "Voidable vs. Void — Quick Distinction", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-8-2", "t": "2. Duress", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-8-3", "t": "3. Undue Influence", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-8-4", "t": "4. Professional Duty and Negligent Advice", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-8-5", "t": "5. Illegal Contracts", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-8-6", "t": "Definition", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-8-7", "t": "Latin Maxim", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-8-8", "t": "Scenario — Election Rigging", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-8-9", "t": "In Pari Delicto — Both Parties Equally at Fault", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-8-10", "t": "Examples of Illegal Contracts", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-8-11", "t": "6. Void Contracts", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-8-12", "t": "Examples of Void Contracts", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-8-13", "t": "Characteristics of Void Contracts", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-8-14", "t": "Illegal vs. Void — Key Distinction", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-8-15", "t": "7. Importance of These Rules", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-8-16", "t": "Challenges in Nigeria", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-8-17", "t": "Summary — Class 8 at a Glance", "lv": 2}]}]}, "cil-class-9": {"course": "CIL", "ref": "Class 9", "title": "Duress, Undue Influence, Discharge & Remedies", "kicker": "Class notes · 6 August 2026 · Delivered via Zoom", "words": 1443, "parts": [{"label": "", "note": "", "src": "Class 9 - CIL_Law_of_Engineering_Contract_Class9_Notes.pdf", "html": "<h2 id=\"cil-class-9-0\">1. Duress — Detailed Treatment<\/h2><h3 id=\"cil-class-9-1\">Essential Elements of Duress<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-1\"><b>Threat<\/b> — to life, body, or goods, before the contract was accepted and signed.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-2\"><b>Lack of free will<\/b> — the party did not sign at their own will.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-3\"><b>Illegitimate pressure<\/b> — the pressure applied was not part of legitimate negotiation.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-4\"><b>Pressure induced the contract<\/b> — the contract became a 'total package' because the party was forced to sign.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-class-9-2\">Types of Duress<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-5\"><b>Physical duress<\/b> — threat of violence or bodily harm (e.g., gun pointed at your head).<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-6\"><b>Economic duress<\/b> — exploiting economic pressure (e.g., engineers in a partnership where the mechanical engineer demands 60% at the critical point of the project, knowing the others cannot proceed without him).<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-7\"><b>Duress to goods<\/b> — threat to damage or withhold someone's property.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-class-9-3\">Importance and Benefits<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-8\">Duress as a legal concept protects contractual freedom, promotes fairness, prevents exploitation, and upholds justice. Its benefits include protecting weaker parties, discouraging coercion, ensuring voluntary agreements (consensus ad idem), and strengthening commercial confidence. If someone makes you sign a document under duress, you can go to court and have it nullified.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-9-4\">Challenges of Proving Duress<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-9\"><b>Difficult to prove<\/b> — you must have solid evidence (documents, voice notes, photos).<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-10\"><b>Economic pressure<\/b> is hard to distinguish from legitimate negotiation.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-11\"><b>Delays in seeking rescission<\/b> may bar relief — if you do not challenge promptly, you may lose your right.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-12\"><b>Evidentiary challenges<\/b> — do you have a picture of the gun? Do you have the voice notes of the threats?<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-class-9-5\">Case Law<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-13\"><b>Okonkwo v. Cooperative Commerce Bank PLC<\/b> — the court recognised that consent obtained under improper pressure may render a transaction voidable where coercion is established.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-9-6\">Student Q&amp;A Highlights<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-14\"><i>Q: Are there objective standards for determining duress?<\/i> The court listens to the parties, examines documents, hears witnesses, and deduces the actual problem objectively.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-15\"><i>Q: What if I expended more energy than my partners?<\/i> You must prove it to the satisfaction of the court. If you cannot, the original agreement (e.g., 50-50) stands.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-9-7\">2. Undue Influence — Detailed Treatment<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-16\">Undue influence occurs when one party uses a position of dominance or trust to improperly influence another party's decision to enter into a contract.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-9-8\">Types of Undue Influence<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-17\"><b>Actual undue influence<\/b> — there is direct evidence of improper pressure being applied.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-18\"><b>Presumed undue influence<\/b> — arises automatically from certain types of relationships where one party is in a position of trust or authority over the other.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-class-9-9\">Relationships Where Undue Influence Is Presumed<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-19\">Solicitor (lawyer) and client<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-20\">Doctor and patient<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-21\">Parent and child<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-22\">Trustee and beneficiary<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-23\">Spiritual adviser and follower (imam and members of his mosque, pastor and his congregants)<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-24\"><b>Important:<\/b> A fiduciary relationship does <b>not automatically<\/b> prove undue influence. The court will listen to the parties and then determine whether advantage was taken. It must still be proved.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-9-10\">Example — Landlord and Tenant<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-25\">A landlord who constantly increases rent to frustrate a tenant into leaving is exercising undue influence. The lecturer shared a case where a landlord would check the tenant's dustbin — seeing corned beef, assorted food — and conclude the tenant had enough money to pay more rent. That is undue influence. Most states, including Lagos, have laws against such exploitation and set standard rent levels for each area.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-9-11\">3. Discharge of Contracts<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-26\">Discharge of contract refers to the <b>termination of contractual obligations<\/b>. Everything that has a beginning has an end. At what point does the contractual obligation come to an end?<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-9-12\">Modes of Discharge<\/h3><h3 id=\"cil-class-9-13\">(a) Discharge by Performance<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-27\">The parties completely perform their obligations. Example: a contractor completes construction according to the contract, hands over the key, there is an opening ceremony — the contractor is discharged and gets paid.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-9-14\">(b) Discharge by Agreement<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-28\">The parties mutually agree to terminate or vary the contract. Both sides consent to end the arrangement.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-9-15\">(c) Discharge by Breach<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-29\">A serious (fundamental) breach allows the innocent party to terminate the contract and sue for damages. Example: a contractor is given two years to build a hall, but after two years, the person is still on the<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-30\">foundation. That is a serious breach.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-9-16\">(d) Discharge by Frustration<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-31\">A supervening event makes performance impossible or radically different from what was contemplated. Examples include:<\/p><ul><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-32\">Destruction of subject matter (e.g., the hall booked for a wedding reception burns down).<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-33\">Death in contracts requiring personal performance (e.g., a musician hired for a wedding passes away).<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-34\">Natural disasters making performance impossible.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-35\">Change in law rendering the contract impossible or illegal.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-class-9-17\">(e) Discharge by Operation of Law<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-36\">Examples include bankruptcy, merger, and limitation periods (time bars). These bring contractual obligations to an end by force of law.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-9-18\">Importance of Discharge Rules<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-37\">Protects commercial certainty.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-38\">Encourages contractual performance and proper completion.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-39\">Provides orderly termination — no chaos, no 'area boys.'<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-class-9-19\">Challenges<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-40\">Determining whether a breach is fundamental enough to terminate.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-41\">Establishing frustration where the event is not obvious.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-42\">Delays in litigation (<i>laches<\/i> and <i>acquiescence<\/i>).<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-43\">Proof of losses.<\/li><\/ul><h2 id=\"cil-class-9-20\">4. Contractual Remedies<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-44\">A remedy is a <b>legal relief<\/b> available to an innocent party following a breach of contract. When you go to court, what are you asking for?<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-9-21\">(a) Damages<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-45\">The most common remedy. Damages involve <b>monetary compensation<\/b> for the breach. Types of damages:<\/p><ul><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-46\"><b>General damages<\/b> — naturally flowing from the breach.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-47\"><b>Special damages<\/b> — specifically proved losses.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-48\"><b>Nominal damages<\/b> — token amount where breach occurred but no real loss.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-49\"><b>Exemplary damages<\/b> — to make an example of the wrongdoer (e.g., neighbour ignores warnings, boasts about connections, and damages your building).<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-50\"><b>Liquidated damages<\/b> — pre-agreed amount stated in the contract itself.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-class-9-22\">(b) Specific Performance<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-51\">An equitable order compelling a party to perform their contractual obligations. Granted where damages would be <b>inadequate<\/b> — where no amount of money can compensate for the breach. Example: sale of unique land or rare property. Under equity, the court can order quantum meruit ('as much as earned') — payment for work already performed even if the contract was not fully completed (e.g., a contractor has done 60% of the work).<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-9-23\">(c) Injunction<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-52\">A court order that <b>restrains a party from committing or continuing a breach<\/b>. Example: if a neighbour's construction is threatening your building's foundation, you can quickly get an injunction to stop them before the damage is done.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-9-24\">(d) Rescission<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-53\">Setting aside the contract and <b>restoring parties to their original positions<\/b>. Most commonly arises in cases of misrepresentation or mistake.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-9-25\">(e) Quantum Meruit<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-54\"><i><b>Quantum meruit<\/b><\/i> (Latin: 'as much as earned'). Allows payment for work already performed where a contract cannot be fully completed or has been discharged in circumstances justifying compensation. Example: a contractor has built 60% of a structure — the court orders payment for the 60% completed.<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-9-26\">(f) Restitution<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-55\">Requires <b>restoration of benefits unjustly obtained<\/b>. The court orders you to return whatever you gained from the contract. Puts the party back in the position they were before the contract.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-56\"><b>Remedy<\/b> <b>What It Does<\/b> <b>When Used<\/b><\/p><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-57\">Damages Monetary compensation Most common; various types<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-58\">Specific Performance<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-59\">Court orders party to perform obligations<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-60\">Where damages are inadequate (unique land, rare property)<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-61\">Injunction Restrains party from continuing breach<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-62\">Urgent: prevent ongoing or imminent harm<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-63\">Rescission Sets aside contract; restores original positions<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-64\">Misrepresentation, mistake<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-65\">Quantum Meruit<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-66\">Payment for work already performed<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-67\">Contract incomplete but work done in good faith<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-68\">Restitution Returns unjustly obtained benefits<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-69\">Breach; unjust enrichment<\/p><h3 id=\"cil-class-9-27\">Importance of Remedies<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-70\">Upholds the sanctity of contracts.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-71\">Facilitates commercial certainty.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-72\">Protects economic interests.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-73\">Enhances confidence in business transactions.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-class-9-28\">Challenges of Remedies<\/h3><ul><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-74\">Delay in obtaining judgments (matters drag on for years in Nigeria).<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-75\">Difficulty enforcing court orders (party claims inability to pay — but courts can place a lien on property).<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-76\">High litigation costs (discourages people; some resort to self-help).<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-77\">Difficulty proving the quantum of damages suffered.<\/li><li id=\"cil-class-9-p0-78\">Limited availability of equitable remedies where claimant has not acted equitably.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"cil-class-9-29\">Case Law<\/h3><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-79\"><b>Bilante International Limited v. NDIC<\/b> — the court affirmed that parties are bound by the terms of their agreement, and that remedies depend on the nature of the breach and the terms of the contract.<\/p><h2 id=\"cil-class-9-30\">5. Conclusion<\/h2><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-80\">The doctrines of illegal and void contracts, duress, undue influence, discharge of contracts, and contractual remedies are central to Nigerian contract law. They ensure that only lawful and voluntary agreements are enforced, protect parties from coercion and exploitation, provide clear mechanisms for terminating contractual obligations, and offer appropriate remedies when breaches occur.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-81\">Nigerian courts continue to apply common law principles, equitable doctrines, and statutory provisions to balance contractual freedom with justice, fairness, and public policy. These principles are indispensable for maintaining certainty and confidence in commercial and private transactions in Nigeria.<\/p><p id=\"cil-class-9-p0-82\"><i>These notes were compiled from a Zoom class transcript with timestamps and speaker labels (August 6, 2026). Transcription<\/i> <i>errors corrected and content structured for clarity.<\/i><\/p>", "toc": [{"id": "cil-class-9-0", "t": "1. Duress — Detailed Treatment", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-9-1", "t": "Essential Elements of Duress", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-9-2", "t": "Types of Duress", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-9-3", "t": "Importance and Benefits", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-9-4", "t": "Challenges of Proving Duress", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-9-5", "t": "Case Law", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-9-6", "t": "Student Q&A Highlights", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-9-7", "t": "2. Undue Influence — Detailed Treatment", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-9-8", "t": "Types of Undue Influence", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-9-9", "t": "Relationships Where Undue Influence Is Presumed", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-9-10", "t": "Example — Landlord and Tenant", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-9-11", "t": "3. Discharge of Contracts", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-9-12", "t": "Modes of Discharge", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-9-13", "t": "(a) Discharge by Performance", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-9-14", "t": "(b) Discharge by Agreement", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-9-15", "t": "(c) Discharge by Breach", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-9-16", "t": "(d) Discharge by Frustration", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-9-17", "t": "(e) Discharge by Operation of Law", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-9-18", "t": "Importance of Discharge Rules", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-9-19", "t": "Challenges", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-9-20", "t": "4. Contractual Remedies", "lv": 2}, {"id": "cil-class-9-21", "t": "(a) Damages", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-9-22", "t": "(b) Specific Performance", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-9-23", "t": "(c) Injunction", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-9-24", "t": "(d) Rescission", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-9-25", "t": "(e) Quantum Meruit", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-9-26", "t": "(f) Restitution", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-9-27", "t": "Importance of Remedies", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-9-28", "t": "Challenges of Remedies", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-9-29", "t": "Case Law", "lv": 3}, {"id": "cil-class-9-30", "t": "5. Conclusion", "lv": 2}]}]}, "bus-block-1": {"course": "BUS", "ref": "Block 1", "title": "Inventory Management", "kicker": "Block 1 · Dr Glorious Adekoya", "words": 1214, "parts": [{"label": "Inventory Management — BUS 323", "note": "", "src": "1.1 Inventory Management (transcript).md", "html": "<h4 id=\"bus-block-1-0\">Page 1<\/h4><p id=\"bus-block-1-p0-1\"><b>Inventory<\/b> [covers] resources used in the day-to-day service of the organisation. There are three resources, which are:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-2\">Raw materials — semi-finished and finished goods <i>(physical)<\/i><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-3\">Human resources — labour (skilled / unskilled)<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-4\">Financial resources — capital<\/li><\/ol><p id=\"bus-block-1-p0-5\">Inventory refers to any kind of resource having economic value and is maintained to fulfil the present and future needs of an organisation. Such resources may be classified into 3:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-6\"><b>Physical resources<\/b> → raw materials, semi-finished goods, finished goods, spare parts<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-7\"><b>Human resources<\/b> — unused labour or man power<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-8\"><b>Financial resources<\/b> → working capital<\/li><\/ol><p id=\"bus-block-1-p0-9\">Inventories are the soul and life wire of any manufacturing organisation; they are also referred to as <b>stock items<\/b> in the operation of the bus[iness].<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-1-p0-10\"><b>Example of resources in different organisation.<\/b> Manufacturer: (Nestlé, Dunlop, breweries, UAC [?]) Semi-finished goods in a particular [firm] will be a finished goods or raw materials to other firm.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-1-1\">Reasons for Carrying Inventory — why must an organisation [carry inventory]?<\/h3><ol><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-11\">They enhance an uninterrupted flow of production by ensuring the necessary input [is] available to permit economic production runs.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-12\">In case of variation in product demand, inventory is kept to combat incidence of stockout.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-13\">It allows flexibility in production scheduling to get the goods out at any cost; it relieves the pressure on the production system.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-14\">They help to decouple successive stages of operation.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-15\">It provides a means of hedging against future prices and delivery uncertainties.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-16\">They provide a means of obtaining economic lot size and quantity discount.<\/li><\/ol><blockquote id=\"bus-block-1-p0-17\">★ <b>When should an order be placed to replenish inventory?<\/b> There are 3 different approaches to check the status of inventory.<\/blockquote><ol><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-18\"><b>Periodic Review System<\/b> — is a system in which orders are placed at regular intervals of time; the quantity varies depending on the inventory on hand and at [the] time of review. This system is often used in those situations where the level of inventory is reviewed at the end of [a] fixed interval of time and units are replenished.<\/li><\/ol><h4 id=\"bus-block-1-2\">Page 2<\/h4><ol start=\"2\"><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-19\"><b>Fixed Order Quantity System<\/b> — is a system in which the level of inventory is monitored regularly, and when it drops to a specified level, a replenish order for a fixed quantity is placed. Variation in demand is allowed by changing the time between orders.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-20\"><b>Optional Replenishment System<\/b> — it comprises a periodic review system with a minimum order quantity restriction. If the calculated order quantity is less than the minimum forecasted value, no order is placed. This avoids replacement of small orders.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-1-3\">Cost Involved in the Management of Inventories<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-1-p0-21\">In order to achieve an optimal level of inventory, an effective balance must be struck between the costs involved in inventory management.<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-22\"><b>Ordering cost<\/b> — the cost incurred in the process of obtaining or ordering raw materials, components or parts from the suppliers (or making an order), e.g. phone call, text messages, email.<\/li><\/ol><p id=\"bus-block-1-p0-23\"><i>Graph:<\/i> cost of ordering (y-axis) vs quantity ordered (x-axis) — a downward-sloping curve labelled &quot;ordering cost&quot;.<\/p><ol start=\"2\"><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-24\"><b>Holding or Carrying cost<\/b> — in order for any quantity to be kept, a unit of items ordered for in inventory, certain cost must be incurred. The higher the quantity of item purchased, the higher the holding cost. This includes storage or rent, salary for store keepers, insurance/security, depreciation or breakage, electricity bills, taxes and interest cost, obsolescence.<\/li><\/ol><p id=\"bus-block-1-p0-25\"><i>Graph:<\/i> cost of holding (y-axis) vs quantity (x-axis) — an upward-sloping curve labelled &quot;holding or carrying cost&quot;.<\/p><blockquote id=\"bus-block-1-p0-26\"><b>N.B.<\/b> The inventory carrying cost per unit may sometimes be expressed as a fixed percentage of the unit cost price of the item; in some cases it may be expressed in Naira value.<\/blockquote><ol start=\"3\"><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-27\"><b>Stockout or Shortage cost<\/b> — these are the costs of running out of stock, e.g. loss of sales, goodwill, customers.<\/li><\/ol><h4 id=\"bus-block-1-4\">Page 3<\/h4><ol start=\"4\"><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-28\"><b>Set-up cost<\/b> — the cost incurred by the producer when starting up a batch, usually at the beginning of the production system: in setting up, cleaning, adjusting and adapting the machine and equipment in readiness for the next production run (batch).<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-29\"><b>Total Inventory Cost<\/b> — is made up of purchase cost, ordering cost, carrying cost and shortage cost.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-1-p0-30\"><span class=\"tex\">\\mathrm{TC} = P_c + O_c + H_o + S_o = (\\text{price} \\times \\text{quantity}) + \\frac{D}{Q}O + \\frac{Q}{2}H<\/span><\/div><p id=\"bus-block-1-p0-31\">where <span class=\"tex-i\">\\frac{D}{Q}O<\/span> is the ordering cost and <span class=\"tex-i\">\\frac{Q}{2}H<\/span> the holding cost.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-1-5\">Factors Involved in Inventory Problem Analysis<\/h3><ol><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-32\">Relevant inventory cost — summation of inventory cost<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-33\">Demand for inventory item.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-34\">Replenishment lead time — length [of time] it will take suppliers to [deliver].<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-35\">Length of planning period.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-36\">Constraints on the inventory system — space, budget, cash, inventory <i>(obstacles / limitations)<\/i>.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-37\">Customer service level — or commitment; the degree of management attention to inventory.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-1-6\">2 Ways of Modelling in Inventory System<\/h3><ol><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-38\">Deterministic inventory modelling<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-39\">Stochastic model or probability<\/li><\/ol><p id=\"bus-block-1-p0-40\"><b>What differentiates the 2 models?<\/b><\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-41\">In deterministic, price is constant, while in probability price varies.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-42\">In deterministic, demand is constant, while [in] stochastic everything varies (holding, ordering cost).<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-43\">Deterministic inventory model relates to those situations in which inventory demand patterns are known and constant. Besides, other relevant variables such as lead time and other cost parameters are also known and constant.<\/li><\/ol><p id=\"bus-block-1-p0-44\"><b>EOQ:<\/b> Under the deterministic model, the simplest of all the models is what is referred to as the simple …<\/p><h4 id=\"bus-block-1-7\">Page 4<\/h4><p id=\"bus-block-1-p0-45\">… [EOQ model]. This model was formulated in 1915 by <b>F. W. Harris<\/b>.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-1-8\">Assumptions — EOQ model<\/h3><ol><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-46\">The demand is known and constant.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-47\">Lead time is zero (instantaneous replenishment).<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-48\">Back order is not allowed.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-49\">No constraints on order size.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-50\">Cost of order is fixed.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-51\">Unit price of items is fixed.<\/li><\/ol><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-1-p0-52\"><span class=\"tex\">\\mathrm{EOQ} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2DO}{H}}<\/span><\/div><p id=\"bus-block-1-p0-53\"><i>Graph:<\/i> cost (y-axis) vs quantity (x-axis) — U-shaped total cost curve with EOQ at the minimum.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-1-p0-54\"><b>Example:<\/b> Kofo Nig Ltd experiences the demand for his product yearly as 600,000 units. It incurred 70k/unit as carrying cost; the cost per order is given as ₦20.<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-55\">What order lot size will minimize total inventory cost for the company?<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-56\">What is the number of times to order per year?<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-57\">What is the annual TIC if the unit price is ₦50?<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-58\">What is the lot size if the carrying cost is 15% of the unit price?<\/li><\/ol><p id=\"bus-block-1-p0-59\"><b>Solution<\/b><\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-60\"><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-1-p0-61\"><span class=\"tex\">D = 600{,}000 \\quad O = ₦20 \\quad H = ₦0.70<\/span><\/div><\/li><\/ol><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-1-p0-62\"><span class=\"tex\">\\mathrm{EOQ} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2DO}{H}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2 \\times 600{,}000 \\times 20}{0.70}} = 5{,}855.4 \\approx 5{,}856\\;\\text{units}<\/span><\/div><ol start=\"2\"><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-63\"><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-1-p0-64\"><span class=\"tex\">\\text{Orders per year} = \\frac{600{,}000}{5{,}856} = 102.46 \\approx 103<\/span><\/div><\/li><\/ol><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-1-p0-65\"><span class=\"tex\">\\text{Interval} = \\frac{5{,}856}{600{,}000} \\times 360 = 3.5 \\approx 4\\;\\text{days}<\/span><\/div><h4 id=\"bus-block-1-9\">Page 5<\/h4><ol start=\"3\"><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-66\"><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-1-p0-67\"><span class=\"tex\">\\mathrm{TIC} = P \\times Q + \\frac{D}{Q}O + \\frac{Q}{2}H<\/span><\/div><\/li><\/ol><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-1-p0-68\"><span class=\"tex\">= 50 \\times 600{,}000 + \\frac{600{,}000}{5{,}856}(20) + \\frac{5{,}856}{2}(0.70)<\/span><\/div><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-1-p0-69\"><span class=\"tex\">= 30{,}000{,}000 + 2{,}049.2 + 2{,}049.6 = ₦30{,}004{,}098.8<\/span><\/div><ol start=\"4\"><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-70\"><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-1-p0-71\"><span class=\"tex\">\\mathrm{EOQ} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2 \\times 600{,}000 \\times 20}{7.5}} = 1{,}788.8 \\approx 1{,}789\\;\\text{units}<\/span><\/div><\/li><\/ol><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-1-p0-72\"><span class=\"tex\">D = 600{,}000 \\quad H = 15\\% \\;\\text{of}\\; ₦50 = 7.5 \\quad O = ₦20<\/span><\/div><h3 id=\"bus-block-1-10\">Discount<\/h3><blockquote id=\"bus-block-1-p0-73\">The one that gives the least total cost is the one to go for.<\/blockquote><table><tr><th>Quantity<\/th><th>Unit price<\/th><\/tr><tr><td>1,000 – 2,000 units<\/td><td>₦50 (discount 2% of ₦50)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>2,001 – 4,000 units<\/td><td>₦48<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>4,001 – 6,000 units<\/td><td>₦45<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>6,001 and above<\/td><td>₦30<\/td><\/tr><\/table><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-1-p0-74\"><span class=\"tex\">\\mathrm{TC}_1 = (50 \\times 600{,}000) + \\frac{600{,}000}{1{,}000}(20) + \\frac{1{,}000}{2}(0.70) = 30{,}012{,}350<\/span><\/div><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-1-p0-75\"><span class=\"tex\">\\mathrm{TC}_2 = (48 \\times 600{,}000) + \\frac{600{,}000}{2{,}001}(20) + \\frac{2{,}001}{2}(0.70) = 28{,}806{,}697.35<\/span><\/div><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-1-p0-76\"><span class=\"tex\">\\mathrm{TC}_3 = (45 \\times 600{,}000) + \\frac{600{,}000}{4{,}001}(20) + \\frac{4{,}001}{2}(0.70) = 27{,}004{,}399.6<\/span><\/div><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-1-p0-77\"><span class=\"tex\">\\mathrm{TC}_4 = (30 \\times 600{,}000) + \\frac{600{,}000}{6{,}001}(20) + \\frac{6{,}000}{2}(0.70) = 18{,}004{,}100.02<\/span><\/div><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-1-p0-78\"><span class=\"tex\">\\mathrm{TC}_5 = (45 \\times 5{,}856) + \\frac{5{,}856}{4{,}001}(20) + \\frac{4{,}001}{2}(0.70) = 264{,}949.62<\/span><\/div><h3 id=\"bus-block-1-11\">Assumptions for EBQ<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-1-p0-79\">Using the 6 in EOQ + 2 other assumptions:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-80\">…<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-1-p0-81\">…<\/li><\/ol>", "toc": [{"id": "bus-block-1-0", "t": "Page 1", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-1-1", "t": "Reasons for Carrying Inventory — why must an organisation [carry inventory]?", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-1-2", "t": "Page 2", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-1-3", "t": "Cost Involved in the Management of Inventories", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-1-4", "t": "Page 3", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-1-5", "t": "Factors Involved in Inventory Problem Analysis", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-1-6", "t": "2 Ways of Modelling in Inventory System", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-1-7", "t": "Page 4", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-1-8", "t": "Assumptions — EOQ model", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-1-9", "t": "Page 5", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-1-10", "t": "Discount", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-1-11", "t": "Assumptions for EBQ", "lv": 3}]}]}, "bus-block-2": {"course": "BUS", "ref": "Block 2", "title": "Management: Evolution, Definition and Functions", "kicker": "Block 2 · Dr S. Edegwa", "words": 4113, "parts": [{"label": "Lecture note", "note": "Course Topic: Evolution of Management, Definition of Management, and Functions of Management", "src": "2. BUS 440 LECTURE NOTE 2025-2026 UNIVERSITY OF LAGOS.docx", "html": "<h3 id=\"bus-block-2-0\">Learning Objectives<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-1\">At the end of this lecture, students should be able to:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-2\">Explain the meaning and importance of management.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-3\">Trace the historical evolution of management thought.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-4\">Discuss the major schools of management.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-5\">Explain the functions of management.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-6\">Apply management principles to engineering projects and industrial organizations.<\/li><\/ul><h2 id=\"bus-block-2-1\">1. Introduction<\/h2><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-7\">Engineering is not only about technical knowledge but also about managing people, projects, materials, finances, and technology. Most engineering failures are not caused by poor technical design alone but by poor planning, ineffective leadership, inadequate communication, and weak control mechanisms. Management provides engineers with the knowledge and skills needed to coordinate resources efficiently to achieve organisational objectives.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-8\">Engineering managers are responsible for:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-9\">Planning engineering projects<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-10\">Managing human and material resources<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-11\">Supervising production<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-12\">Coordinating multidisciplinary teams<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-13\">Ensuring quality and safety<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-14\">Meeting project deadlines and budgets<\/li><\/ul><h2 id=\"bus-block-2-2\">2. Definition of Management<\/h2><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-15\">Management has been defined by several scholars.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-16\"><b>(a) Henri Fayol (1916):<\/b> Management is the process of forecasting, planning, organising, commanding, coordinating, and controlling. This definition emphasises management as a continuous process.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-17\"><b>(b) Harold Koontz and Cyril O'Donnell (1976):<\/b> Management is the process of designing and maintaining an environment in which individuals, working together in groups, efficiently accomplish selected objectives. This definition stresses teamwork and organisational effectiveness.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-18\"><b>(c) Mary Parker Follett (1941):<\/b> Management is the art of getting things done through people. This definition highlights human relations.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-19\"><b>(d) Peter Drucker (1974):<\/b> Management is the organ of institutions that converts resources into productive performance. This definition views management as a productivity tool.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-20\"><b>(e) George Terry (1977):<\/b> Management is a distinct process consisting of planning, organizing, actuating, and controlling performed to determine and accomplish objectives by the use of people and resources.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-21\"><b>Comprehensive Definition:<\/b> Management is the process of planning, organizing, staffing, directing, coordinating, and controlling human, financial, material, and technological resources efficiently and effectively to achieve organisational goals.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-3\">Characteristics of Management<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-22\">Management is:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-23\">Goal-oriented<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-24\">Universal<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-25\">Continuous<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-26\">Dynamic<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-27\">Decision-making<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-28\">People-centered<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-29\">Multidisciplinary<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-30\">Resource-oriented<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-31\">Science and art<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-32\">Results-oriented<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-4\">Importance of Management in Engineering<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-33\">Management enables engineers to:Complete projects within schedule; Control project costs; improve quality; coordinate technical teams, enhance productivity, reduce waste; improve innovation; ensure safety compliance; and achieve customer satisfaction.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-34\">Example: Constructing a bridge requires: Engineers, Architects, Quantity surveyors, Procurement officers, Contractors, and Government agencies<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-35\">Without proper management, even an excellent engineering design may fail during implementation.<\/p><h2 id=\"bus-block-2-5\">3. Evolution of Management<\/h2><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-36\">Management practices have evolved over thousands of years. The evolution can be divided into several stages.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-6\">Stage One: Ancient Management (3000 BC–1800 AD)<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-37\">Management principles existed long before formal theories. Examples include:<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-7\">Ancient Egypt<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-38\">Construction of pyramids required:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-39\">Planning<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-40\">Labour organization<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-41\">Material management<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-42\">Time scheduling<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-8\">China<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-43\">Large irrigation systems and the Great Wall involved:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-44\">Division of labour<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-45\">Coordination<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-46\">Supervision<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-9\">Roman Empire<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-47\">Management principles were applied in:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-48\">Military organization<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-49\">Tax administration<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-50\">Public works<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-51\">Road construction<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-10\">Industrial Crafts<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-52\">During the medieval period:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-53\">Guilds controlled production.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-54\">Masters trained apprentices.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-55\">Quality standards were maintained.<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-56\">Although informal, these practices laid the foundation for modern management.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-11\">Stage Two: Industrial Revolution (1760–1840)<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-57\">The Industrial Revolution transformed production from handcraft to machine production.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-58\">Major changes included:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-59\">Large factories<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-60\">Mass production<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-61\">Specialized labour<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-62\">Capital investment<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-63\">Need for professional managers<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-64\">Problems emerged:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-65\">Poor supervision<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-66\">Low productivity<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-67\">Labour conflicts<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-68\">Inefficient production<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-69\">These challenges led scholars to develop formal management theories.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-12\">Stage Three: Scientific Management School<\/h3><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-13\">Founder<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-70\">Frederick Winslow Taylor (1856–1915)<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-71\">Known as the Father of Scientific Management.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-72\">Taylor believed work should be based on science rather than guesswork.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-14\">Principles of Scientific Management<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-73\">Science instead of rule of thumb<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-74\">Scientific selection of workers<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-75\">Scientific training<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-76\">Cooperation between workers and managers<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-77\">Equal division of work<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-15\">Contributions<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-78\">Time study<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-79\">Motion study<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-80\">Standardization<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-81\">Incentive wage system<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-82\">Productivity improvement<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-16\">Applications in Engineering<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-83\">Scientific management is widely used in:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-84\">Manufacturing<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-85\">Production engineering<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-86\">Assembly lines<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-87\">Construction scheduling<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-88\">Quality control<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-17\">Criticisms<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-89\">Treated workers like machines<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-90\">Ignored social needs<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-91\">Increased work pressure<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-92\">Encouraged repetitive jobs<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-18\">Summary of the Evolution of Management<\/h3><table><tr><th>Period<\/th><th>Major Focus<\/th><th>Key Contributors<\/th><\/tr><tr><td>Ancient Era<\/td><td>Organization of labour<\/td><td>Egyptians, Chinese, Romans<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Industrial Revolution<\/td><td>Factory management<\/td><td>Industrialists<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Scientific Management<\/td><td>Efficiency and productivity<\/td><td>F. W. Taylor<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Administrative Theory<\/td><td>Organizational management<\/td><td>Henri Fayol<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Bureaucratic Theory<\/td><td>Rules and structure<\/td><td>Max Weber<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Human Relations<\/td><td>Employee motivation<\/td><td>Elton Mayo<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Behavioural Science<\/td><td>Human behaviour<\/td><td>Maslow, McGregor, Herzberg<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Modern Management<\/td><td>Systems, quality, technology<\/td><td>Contemporary scholars<\/td><\/tr><\/table><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-19\">Functions of Management<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-93\">The functions of management describe the core activities managers perform. A widely accepted framework includes five functions.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-94\"><b>1. Planning:<\/b> Planning is deciding in advance what to do, how to do it, when to do it, and who should do it.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-20\">Importance<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-95\">Reduces uncertainty<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-96\">Improves resource allocation<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-97\">Sets objectives<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-98\">Supports decision-making<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-99\">Minimizes risk<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-100\"><b>Engineering Example:<\/b> Developing a project schedule, budget, and risk management plan before constructing a highway.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-101\"><b>2. Organizing:<\/b> Organizing involves arranging resources and assigning responsibilities to achieve organizational objectives.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-21\">Activities<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-102\">Designing organizational structures<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-103\">Allocating tasks<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-104\">Delegating authority<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-105\">Coordinating departments<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-106\"><b>Engineering Example:<\/b> Assigning civil, electrical, and mechanical engineers to different work packages on a construction project.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-107\"><b>3. Staffing:<\/b> Staffing involves acquiring, developing, and retaining competent employees.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-22\">Activities<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-108\">Recruitment<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-109\">Selection<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-110\">Training<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-111\">Performance appraisal<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-112\">Career development<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-113\"><b>Engineering Example:<\/b> Hiring qualified project engineers and providing training on new design software and safety procedures.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-114\"><b>4. Directing (Leading):<\/b> Directing involves influencing and motivating employees to achieve organizational goals.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-23\">Activities<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-115\">Leadership<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-116\">Motivation<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-117\">Communication<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-118\">Supervision<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-119\">Conflict resolution<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-120\"><b>Engineering Example:<\/b> A project manager motivating site engineers to meet milestones while maintaining safety standards.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-121\"><b>5. Controlling:<\/b> Controlling is the process of measuring actual performance against planned objectives and taking corrective action where necessary.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-24\">Steps<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-122\">Set performance standards.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-123\">Measure actual performance.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-124\">Compare results with standards.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-125\">Identify deviations.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-126\">Take corrective action.<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-127\"><b>Engineering Example:<\/b> Monitoring project costs, quality, and timelines, and addressing delays or budget overruns promptly.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-25\">Relationship Among the Functions of Management<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-128\">The functions are interdependent and form a continuous cycle:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-129\"><b>Planning<\/b> sets objectives.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-130\"><b>Organising<\/b> allocates resources.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-131\"><b>Staffing<\/b> provides competent personnel.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-132\"><b>Directing<\/b> guides and motivates employees.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-133\"><b>Controlling<\/b> measures performance and feeds information back into future planning.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-26\">Application of Management Functions in Engineering<\/h3><table><tr><th>Function<\/th><th>Engineering Application<\/th><\/tr><tr><td>Planning<\/td><td>Project scheduling, budgeting, feasibility studies<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Organizing<\/td><td>Team formation, work allocation, procurement<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Staffing<\/td><td>Recruitment of engineers and technicians, training<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Directing<\/td><td>Leadership, communication, safety supervision<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Controlling<\/td><td>Quality assurance, cost control, schedule monitoring<\/td><\/tr><\/table><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-134\"><b>Conclusion:<\/b> Management has evolved from informal administrative practices in ancient civilizations to sophisticated theories and modern approaches that combine technical, behavioural, and analytical perspectives. For engineers, technical expertise alone is insufficient; successful project delivery requires effective planning, organizing, staffing, leadership, and control. Mastery of management principles enables engineers to deliver projects on time, within budget, to the required quality standards, and safely and sustainably.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-27\">Suggested References<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-135\">Daft, R. L. (2022). <i>Management<\/i> (14th ed.). Cengage Learning.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-136\">Drucker, P. F. (2008). <i>Management: Revised Edition<\/i>. HarperBusiness.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-137\">Fayol, H. (1949). <i>General and Industrial Management<\/i>. Pitman Publishing.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-138\">Koontz, H., Weihrich, H., &amp; Cannice, M. V. (2020). <i>Essentials of Management<\/i> (11th ed.). McGraw-Hill.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-139\">Robbins, S. P., &amp; Coulter, M. (2022). <i>Management<\/i> (15th ed.). Pearson.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-140\">Schermerhorn, J. R. (2021). <i>Management<\/i> (14th ed.). Wiley.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-141\">Taylor, F. W. (1911). <i>The Principles of Scientific Management<\/i>. Harper &amp; Brothers.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-28\">Lecture II: Management as an Art, a Science, or Both?<\/h3><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-29\">Learning Objectives<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-142\">At the end of this lecture, students should be able to:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-143\">Define management as an art.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-144\">Explain management as a science.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-145\">Distinguish between management as an art and management as a science.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-146\">Discuss why management is regarded as both an art and a science.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-147\">Apply the concept to engineering practice and project management.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-30\">Introduction<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-148\">One of the oldest debates in management is whether management should be regarded as an <b>art<\/b>, a <b>science<\/b>, or <b>both<\/b>. Some scholars argue that management is an art because it requires creativity, leadership, intuition, judgment, and experience in dealing with people. Others argue that management is a science because it is based on systematic knowledge, established principles, theories, and analytical techniques. Modern management scholars generally agree that management combines both characteristics. Managers require scientific knowledge to make rational decisions and artistic skills to apply that knowledge effectively in real-world situations. For engineers, this debate is particularly important because engineering management requires both technical competence and the ability to lead people and manage projects successfully.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-149\"><b>Meaning of Art:<\/b> Art is the practical application of knowledge, skills, creativity, and personal judgment to achieve desired objectives. Art is learned through:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-150\">Practice<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-151\">Experience<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-152\">Observation<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-153\">Creativity<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-154\">Innovation<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-155\">Examples include: Medicine, Architecture, Music, Engineering design, and Teaching.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-156\">Like these professions, management requires practical skills that improve with experience.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-31\">Characteristics of Art<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-157\">Management is regarded as an art because it possesses the following characteristics:<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-158\"><b>1. Practical Application of Knowledge:<\/b> Art involves applying theoretical knowledge to practical situations. Managers use management principles to solve organisational problems. <b>Example:<\/b> An engineering manager applies project scheduling principles to complete a bridge construction project within budget.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-159\"><b>2. Personal Skills:<\/b> Every manager performs differently. Although managers may possess similar educational qualifications, their effectiveness depends on their personal abilities. Examples include:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-160\">Communication<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-161\">Leadership<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-162\">Negotiation<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-163\">Problem-solving<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-32\">3.Creativity: Managers frequently encounter situations without standard solutions. Creativity enables managers to:<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-164\">Develop innovative ideas<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-165\">Improve production processes<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-166\">Introduce new technologies<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-167\">Solve unexpected problems<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-168\">Engineering managers often develop innovative construction methods to reduce project costs.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-169\"><b>4. Experience:<\/b> Management skills improve through experience.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-170\">A manager with many years of industrial experience often handles crises more effectively than a newly appointed manager.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-171\"><b>5. Result-Oriented:<\/b> Like every art, management aims at producing results.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-172\">Successful managers achieve:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-173\">High productivity<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-174\">Employee satisfaction<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-175\">Customer satisfaction<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-176\">Organizational growth<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-33\">6. Continuous Improvement: Artists continually improve their skills through practice.<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-177\">Similarly, managers improve through: Training, Experience, Professional development, and Feedback<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-34\">Why Management is an Art<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-178\">Management is considered an art because managers must:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-179\">Lead people<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-180\">Motivate employees<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-181\">Inspire teamwork<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-182\">Resolve conflicts<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-183\">Negotiate contracts<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-184\">Make sound judgments<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-185\">Exercise creativity<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-186\">No two management situations are exactly alike.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-35\">Meaning of Science<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-187\">Science is a systematic body of knowledge developed through observation, experimentation, measurement, analysis, and verification.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-188\">Scientific knowledge is: Organized, Objective, Testable, Predictable, and Universal<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-189\">Examples include:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-190\">Physics<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-191\">Chemistry<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-192\">Mathematics<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-193\">Engineering<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-194\">Biology<\/li><\/ul><h2 id=\"bus-block-2-36\">Characteristics of Science<\/h2><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-37\">1. Systematic Body of Knowledge: Science is organized into principles and theories. Management has developed systematic theories such as:<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-195\">Scientific Management<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-196\">Administrative Theory<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-197\">Bureaucratic Theory<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-198\">Human Relations Theory<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-199\">Systems Theory<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-200\">Contingency Theory<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-38\">2. Universal Principles: Scientific principles apply in many situations.<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-201\">Examples of management principles include:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-202\">Division of labour<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-203\">Unity of command<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-204\">Delegation<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-205\">Span of control<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-206\">Planning<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-207\">Coordination<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-208\">These principles are applied in organizations worldwide, although they may require adaptation to local conditions.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-209\"><b>3. Cause-and-Effect Relationship:<\/b> Science identifies relationships between variables.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-210\">Management studies relationships such as:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-211\">Motivation → Productivity<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-212\">Training → Employee performance<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-213\">Leadership → Organizational effectiveness<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-214\">Planning → Project success<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-39\">4. Observation and Experimentation: Management knowledge has developed through research and observation.<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-215\">Examples include:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-216\">Time and motion studies<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-217\">The Hawthorne experiments<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-218\">Organizational behaviour research<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-219\">Leadership studies<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-220\"><b>5. Predictability:<\/b> Scientific knowledge helps managers predict outcomes. For example,<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-221\">Effective planning generally reduces project delays and cost overruns.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-222\"><b>6. Continuous Development:<\/b> Management knowledge continually expands through research in:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-223\">Artificial Intelligence<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-224\">Digital transformation<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-225\">Strategic management<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-226\">Supply chain management<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-227\">Engineering project management<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-40\">Why Management is a Science<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-228\">Management is regarded as a science because:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-229\">It has established theories.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-230\">It uses systematic research.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-231\">It applies scientific methods.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-232\">It relies on measurable data.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-233\">It develops general principles.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-234\">It supports evidence-based decision-making.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-41\">Differences Between Management as an Art and Management as a Science<\/h3><table><tr><th>Management as an Art<\/th><th>Management as a Science<\/th><\/tr><tr><td>Based on practical skills<\/td><td>Based on systematic knowledge<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Learned through experience<\/td><td>Learned through education and research<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Requires creativity<\/td><td>Requires analysis and logical reasoning<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Concerned with application<\/td><td>Concerned with theory and principles<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Subjective<\/td><td>More objective<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Focuses on judgment<\/td><td>Focuses on facts and evidence<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Developed through practice<\/td><td>Developed through scientific investigation<\/td><\/tr><\/table><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-42\">Why Management is Both an Art and a Science<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-235\">Modern scholars agree that management is <b>both an art and a science<\/b> because successful managers combine theoretical knowledge with practical skills. Science provides the <b>knowledge<\/b>, while art provides the <b>ability to apply that knowledge effectively<\/b>.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-236\">For example: A project manager understands scientific techniques such as:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-237\">Critical Path Method (CPM)<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-238\">Project Evaluation and Review Technique (PERT)<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-239\">Risk analysis<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-240\">Budgeting<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-241\">Forecasting<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-242\">However, completing a project successfully also requires:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-243\">Leadership<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-244\">Negotiation<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-245\">Conflict resolution<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-246\">Motivation<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-247\">Emotional intelligence<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-248\">Thus, management cannot rely solely on scientific knowledge or artistic ability; both are essential.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-43\">Engineering Perspective<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-249\">Engineering management clearly demonstrates the integration of art and science.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-44\">Management as Science in Engineering<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-250\">Engineering managers use:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-251\">Engineering economics<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-252\">Operations research<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-253\">Statistical quality control<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-254\">Linear programming<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-255\">Simulation models<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-256\">Cost analysis<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-257\">Risk assessment<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-258\">Project scheduling software<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-259\">These involve scientific methods and quantitative analysis.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-45\">Management as Art in Engineering<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-260\">Engineering managers also need to:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-261\">Lead multidisciplinary teams<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-262\">Communicate with stakeholders<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-263\">Motivate employees<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-264\">Resolve workplace conflicts<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-265\">Make ethical decisions<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-266\">Handle uncertainty<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-267\">Encourage innovation<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-268\">These activities depend on interpersonal and leadership skills.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-269\"><b>Practical Example:<\/b> Suppose an engineering company is constructing a highway.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-270\"><b>Scientific Aspect:<\/b> The project manager uses:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-271\">Gantt Charts<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-272\">CPM<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-273\">PERT<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-274\">Cost estimation<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-275\">Productivity analysis<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-276\">Risk management software<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-277\">These are scientific tools.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-46\">Artistic Aspect<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-278\">The same manager must:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-279\">Motivate workers<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-280\">Resolve disputes among contractors<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-281\">Negotiate with government agencies<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-282\">Inspire teamwork<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-283\">Make quick decisions during emergencies<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-284\">These require managerial artistry.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-47\">Advantages of Viewing Management as Both Art and Science<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-285\">Promotes evidence-based decision-making.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-286\">Encourages creativity and innovation.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-287\">Improves organizational performance.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-288\">Supports effective leadership.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-289\">Enhances project success.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-290\">Facilitates problem-solving.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-291\">Balances technical and human factors.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-292\">Produces competent engineering managers.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-48\">Criticisms<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-293\">Some scholars argue that management is <b>not a pure science<\/b> because:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-294\">Human behaviour cannot always be predicted.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-295\">Organizational environments constantly change.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-296\">Managerial decisions involve subjective judgment.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-297\">Cultural differences affect management practices.<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-298\">Others argue that management is <b>more than an art<\/b> because:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-299\">It has developed established theories.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-300\">Research continually improves management knowledge.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-301\">Many managerial decisions rely on quantitative analysis.<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-302\">These criticisms reinforce the view that management is best understood as both an art and a science.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-49\">Contemporary View<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-303\">Today, management is increasingly regarded as a profession that integrates scientific knowledge with practical competence. Advances in data analytics, artificial intelligence, and digital technologies have strengthened its scientific foundation, while globalization and diverse workforces have increased the importance of leadership, communication, adaptability, and ethical judgment. Successful managers therefore combine analytical skills with interpersonal effectiveness.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-50\">Conclusion<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-304\">The debate over whether management is an art or a science has largely been resolved by recognizing that it embodies both dimensions. The scientific aspect provides theories, principles, methods, and analytical tools that guide managerial decision-making. The artistic aspect enables managers to apply those principles creatively, lead people effectively, and respond to complex organizational challenges. For engineering professionals, success depends not only on technical expertise but also on the ability to integrate scientific methods with the art of leadership and people management.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-51\">Key Takeaways<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-305\"><b>Management as an Art:<\/b> Emphasizes practical application, creativity, experience, leadership, and judgment.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-306\"><b>Management as a Science:<\/b> Emphasizes systematic knowledge, research, principles, evidence, and analytical methods.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-307\"><b>Management as Both:<\/b> Effective managers integrate scientific knowledge with practical skills to achieve organisational goals<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-52\">Topic: Evolution of Management Theories<\/h3><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-53\">Learning Objectives<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-308\">At the end of this lecture, students should be able to:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-309\">Explain the concept of management theory.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-310\">Describe the historical evolution of management theories.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-311\">Discuss the major schools of management thought and their proponents.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-312\">Compare classical, neo-classical, and modern management theories.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-313\">Evaluate the relevance of management theories to engineering organizations and project management.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-314\">Apply management theories to contemporary engineering and industrial challenges.<\/li><\/ul><h2 id=\"bus-block-2-54\">1. Introduction<\/h2><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-315\">Management theories are systematic frameworks that explain how organizations should be managed to achieve efficiency, productivity, and organizational goals. They provide managers with principles, models, and guidelines for planning, organizing, leading, motivating, and controlling organizational activities.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-316\">The evolution of management theories reflects changes in economic conditions, technological advancements, industrial development, globalization, and the increasing complexity of organizations. As organizations evolved from small workshops to multinational corporations, management thinking also evolved from simple supervisory practices to sophisticated strategic and systems-oriented approaches.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-317\">For engineers, understanding management theories is essential because engineering projects involve not only technical expertise but also leadership, teamwork, resource allocation, decision-making, innovation, and project control.<\/p><h2 id=\"bus-block-2-55\">2. Meaning of Management Theory<\/h2><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-318\">Management theory refers to the systematic collection of ideas, principles, concepts, and practices developed to explain how organizations should be managed effectively and efficiently.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-319\">According to scholars, management theories help managers:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-320\">Understand organizational behaviour.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-321\">Improve decision-making.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-322\">Increase productivity.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-323\">Motivate employees.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-324\">Solve organizational problems.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-325\">Achieve strategic objectives.<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-326\">Management theories have developed over more than a century and continue to evolve in response to new organizational challenges.<\/p><h2 id=\"bus-block-2-56\">3. Evolution of Management Theories<\/h2><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-327\">The development of management thought can be divided into three major periods:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-328\"><b>Classical Management Theories (1880–1930)<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-329\"><b>Neo-Classical (Behavioural) Management Theories (1930–1950)<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-330\"><b>Modern Management Theories (1950–Present)<\/b><\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-57\">I. Classical Management Theories (1880–1930)<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-331\">The Classical School emerged during the Industrial Revolution when factories expanded rapidly and organizations sought ways to improve productivity and efficiency.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-332\">The classical theorists believed that organizations should be managed scientifically through specialization, standardization, hierarchy, and formal structures.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-333\">The classical school consists of three major theories:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-334\">Scientific Management Theory<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-335\">Administrative Management Theory<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-336\">Bureaucratic Management Theory<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-58\">A. Scientific Management Theory<\/h3><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-59\">Proponent<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-337\">Frederick Winslow Taylor (1856–1915)<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-338\">Known as the <b>Father of Scientific Management<\/b>.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-60\">Background<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-339\">Taylor observed that workers often used inefficient methods and that production depended largely on personal experience rather than scientific analysis.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-340\">He proposed replacing the &quot;rule of thumb&quot; with scientifically developed methods.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-61\">Principles of Scientific Management<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-341\">Science instead of rule of thumb.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-342\">Scientific selection of workers.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-343\">Scientific training and development.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-344\">Cooperation between management and workers.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-345\">Equal division of work and responsibility.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-62\">Contributions<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-346\">Time study<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-347\">Motion study<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-348\">Standardization<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-349\">Differential wage system<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-350\">Work specialization<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-351\">Productivity improvement<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-63\">Advantages<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-352\">Increased efficiency<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-353\">Reduced production costs<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-354\">Higher productivity<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-355\">Better work methods<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-356\">Standardized operations<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-64\">Criticisms<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-357\">Treated workers like machines.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-358\">Ignored employee emotions.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-359\">Reduced creativity.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-360\">Encouraged repetitive work.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-65\">Engineering Application<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-361\">Scientific management is widely applied in:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-362\">Manufacturing systems<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-363\">Production engineering<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-364\">Assembly-line operations<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-365\">Industrial automation<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-366\">Lean production<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-367\">Construction scheduling<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-66\">B. Administrative Management Theory<\/h3><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-67\">Proponent<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-368\">Henri Fayol (1841–1925)<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-369\">Known as the <b>Father of Administrative Management<\/b>.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-370\">Unlike Taylor, Fayol focused on managing the entire organization rather than individual workers.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-68\">Fayol's 14 Principles of Management<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-371\">Division of work<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-372\">Authority and responsibility<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-373\">Discipline<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-374\">Unity of command<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-375\">Unity of direction<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-376\">Subordination of individual interest<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-377\">Remuneration<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-378\">Centralization<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-379\">Scalar chain<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-380\">Order<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-381\">Equity<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-382\">Stability of tenure<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-383\">Initiative<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-384\">Esprit de corps<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-69\">Fayol's Functions of Management<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-385\">Originally, Fayol identified:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-386\">Planning<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-387\">Organizing<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-388\">Commanding<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-389\">Coordinating<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-390\">Controlling<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-391\">These have evolved into the modern management functions of planning, organizing, staffing, leading, and controlling<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-70\">Contributions<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-392\">Universal management principles<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-393\">Management education<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-394\">Organizational coordination<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-395\">Administrative efficiency<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-71\">Criticisms<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-396\">Principles may not fit every situation.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-397\">Overemphasized formal authority.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-398\">Limited attention to employee motivation.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-72\">Engineering Application<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-399\">Engineering firms use Fayol's principles in:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-400\">Project planning<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-401\">Organizational design<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-402\">Construction management<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-403\">Infrastructure development<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-404\">Resource coordination<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-73\">C. Bureaucratic Management Theory<\/h3><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-74\">Proponent<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-405\">Max Weber (1864–1920)<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-406\">Weber believed organizations should be governed by rational rules rather than personal relationships.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-75\">Characteristics<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-407\">Division of labour<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-408\">Formal hierarchy<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-409\">Rules and regulations<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-410\">Merit-based recruitment<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-411\">Written documentation<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-412\">Impersonal relationships<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-76\">Advantages<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-413\">Consistency<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-414\">Accountability<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-415\">Fairness<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-416\">Efficiency<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-417\">Professionalism<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-77\">Criticisms<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-418\">Excessive rigidity<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-419\">Slow decision-making<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-420\">Too much paperwork<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-421\">Resistance to innovation<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-78\">Engineering Application<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-422\">Bureaucratic principles are common in:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-423\">Government engineering agencies<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-424\">Public works departments<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-425\">Regulatory organizations<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-426\">Large construction firms<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-79\">II. Neo-Classical (Behavioural) Management Theories (1930–1950)<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-427\">The behavioural school emerged because classical theories paid insufficient attention to the human side of organizations.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-428\">Behavioural theorists argued that employee motivation, leadership, communication, and social relationships strongly influence organizational performance.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-80\">A. Human Relations Theory<\/h3><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-81\">Proponent<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-429\">Elton Mayo<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-82\">Hawthorne Studies (1924–1932)<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-430\">Conducted at the Western Electric Company.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-83\">Major Findings<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-431\">Employees are motivated by recognition.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-432\">Informal groups influence behaviour.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-433\">Leadership affects productivity.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-434\">Communication improves performance.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-435\">Social factors matter.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-84\">Contributions<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-436\">Human-centred management<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-437\">Employee welfare<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-438\">Teamwork<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-439\">Participation<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-440\">Communication<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-85\">Engineering Application<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-441\">Useful in:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-442\">Team management<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-443\">Safety culture<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-444\">Employee engagement<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-445\">Project collaboration<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-86\">B. Behavioural Science Theory<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-446\">Major contributors include:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-447\">Abraham Maslow<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-448\">Douglas McGregor<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-449\">Frederick Herzberg<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-450\">Chris Argyris<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-87\">Main Ideas<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-451\">Behavioural scientists studied:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-452\">Motivation<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-453\">Leadership<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-454\">Personality<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-455\">Learning<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-456\">Organizational culture<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-457\">Employee satisfaction<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-88\">Contributions<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-458\">Improved leadership<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-459\">Better motivation systems<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-460\">Organizational development<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-461\">Employee participation<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-89\">Engineering Application<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-462\">Behavioural theories improve:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-463\">Team leadership<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-464\">Innovation<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-465\">Knowledge sharing<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-466\">Research and development<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-467\">Cross-functional collaboration<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-90\">III. Modern Management Theories (1950–Present)<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-468\">Modern theories recognize that organizations operate in dynamic and complex environments.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-469\">Managers must adapt to technological, economic, political, and social changes.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-470\">Major modern theories include:<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-91\">A. Systems Theory<\/h3><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-92\">Proponent<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-471\">Often associated with Ludwig von Bertalanffy.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-93\">Main Idea<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-472\">Organizations are systems made up of interrelated and interdependent parts that work together to achieve common goals.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-94\">Components of a System<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-473\">Inputs<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-474\">Transformation process<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-475\">Outputs<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-476\">Feedback<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-477\">Environment<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-95\">Engineering Application<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-478\">Engineering projects involve interconnected systems such as:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-479\">Procurement<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-480\">Design<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-481\">Production<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-482\">Quality control<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-483\">Logistics<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-484\">Customer service<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-485\">Failure in one subsystem affects the entire project.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-96\">B. Contingency Theory<\/h3><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-97\">Major Contributors<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-486\">Fred Fiedler<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-487\">Paul Lawrence<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-488\">Jay Lorsch<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-98\">Main Idea<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-489\">There is <b>no one best way<\/b> to manage.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-490\">Management decisions depend on:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-491\">Organizational size<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-492\">Technology<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-493\">Environment<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-494\">Employees<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-495\">Organizational goals<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-99\">Engineering Application<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-496\">Engineering managers adopt different leadership styles depending on:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-497\">Project complexity<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-498\">Team experience<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-499\">Safety risks<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-500\">Technology<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-501\">Deadlines<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-100\">C. Quantitative (Management Science) Theory<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-502\">This theory applies mathematics and analytical models to managerial decision-making.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-101\">Techniques<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-503\">Operations research<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-504\">Linear programming<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-505\">Simulation<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-506\">Inventory models<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-507\">Forecasting<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-508\">Queuing theory<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-509\">Decision analysis<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-102\">Engineering Application<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-510\">Widely used in:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-511\">Production planning<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-512\">Logistics<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-513\">Transportation<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-514\">Manufacturing<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-515\">Project scheduling<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-516\">Supply chain optimization<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-103\">D. Total Quality Management (TQM)<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-517\">TQM focuses on continuous improvement and customer satisfaction through organization-wide participation.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-104\">Key Principles<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-518\">Customer focus<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-519\">Continuous improvement<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-520\">Employee involvement<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-521\">Process management<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-522\">Data-driven decision-making<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-523\">Engineering organizations use TQM to improve product quality and reduce defects.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-105\">E. Lean Management<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-524\">Lean Management seeks to eliminate waste while maximizing customer value.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-106\">Types of Waste<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-525\">Overproduction<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-526\">Waiting time<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-527\">Excess inventory<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-528\">Defects<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-529\">Unnecessary transportation<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-530\">Excess motion<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-531\">Overprocessing<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-532\">Engineering firms adopt Lean to improve productivity and reduce costs.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-107\">F. Agile Management<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-533\">Agile emphasizes flexibility, collaboration, iterative development, and rapid response to change.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-534\">It is widely used in:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-535\">Software engineering<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-536\">Product development<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-537\">Innovation projects<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-538\">Research and development<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-108\">Comparison of Major Management Theories<\/h3><table><tr><th>Theory<\/th><th>Main Focus<\/th><th>Strengths<\/th><th>Weaknesses<\/th><\/tr><tr><td>Scientific Management<\/td><td>Efficiencyand productivity<\/td><td>High productivity<\/td><td>Ignores human needs<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Administrative Theory<\/td><td>Organizational structure<\/td><td>Universal principles<\/td><td>Rigid in some situations<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Bureaucratic Theory<\/td><td>Rules and hierarchy<\/td><td>Accountability<\/td><td>Excessive formalization<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Human Relations<\/td><td>Employee satisfaction<\/td><td>Better morale<\/td><td>May underemphasize productivity<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Behavioural Science<\/td><td>Human behaviour<\/td><td>Improved leadership<\/td><td>Human behaviour is difficult to predict<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Systems Theory<\/td><td>Organizational interdependence<\/td><td>Holistic perspective<\/td><td>Can be complex to implement<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Contingency Theory<\/td><td>Situational management<\/td><td>Flexible<\/td><td>No universal guidelines<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Quantitative Theory<\/td><td>Data-driven decisions<\/td><td>Analytical accuracy<\/td><td>May overlook human factors<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>TQM<\/td><td>Continuous improvement<\/td><td>Quality enhancement<\/td><td>Requires organizational commitment<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Lean Management<\/td><td>Waste reduction<\/td><td>Cost efficiency<\/td><td>Difficult cultural change<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Agile Management<\/td><td>Adaptability<\/td><td>Innovation and speed<\/td><td>Less suitable for highly rigid environments<\/td><\/tr><\/table><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-109\">Relevance of Management Theories to Engineering<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-539\">Engineering organizations apply these theories in:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-540\">Project management<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-541\">Production management<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-542\">Quality assurance<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-543\">Supply chain management<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-544\">Construction management<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-545\">Manufacturing systems<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-546\">Research and development<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-547\">Innovation management<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-548\">Risk management<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-549\">Sustainability initiatives<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-550\">An effective engineering manager integrates ideas from multiple theories rather than relying on a single approach.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-110\">Contemporary Trends in Management Theory<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-551\">Management theory continues to evolve in response to global challenges. Emerging areas include:<\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-552\">Digital Transformation<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-553\">Artificial Intelligence (AI)-enabled Management<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-554\">Industry 4.0 and Smart Manufacturing<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-555\">Sustainability and Green Management<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-556\">Knowledge Management<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-557\">Strategic Management<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-558\">Innovation Management<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-559\">Remote and Hybrid Work Management<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-560\">Data Analytics for Decision-Making<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-561\">These developments are particularly relevant for engineering managers working in technologically advanced and globally connected industries.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-111\">Conclusion<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-562\">The evolution of management theories reflects the continuous search for better ways to manage organizations. From the efficiency-driven Classical School to the human-centred Neo-Classical School and the adaptive, technology-driven Modern School, each theory has contributed valuable insights. Contemporary engineering managers should understand the strengths and limitations of each perspective and apply them appropriately to improve organizational performance, innovation, and project success.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-112\">Key Takeaways<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-563\">Management theories evolved from <b>Classical<\/b> to <b>Neo-Classical<\/b> and <b>Modern<\/b> perspectives.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-564\">No single theory is universally applicable; effective managers combine ideas from multiple schools.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-565\">Engineering management requires balancing efficiency, organizational structure, human relations, and adaptability.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-566\">Modern theories emphasize systems thinking, contingency, data-driven decisions, quality, lean practices, and agility.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-2-p0-567\">Understanding the evolution of management thought enables engineering graduates to manage complex projects and organizations effectively.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-113\">TOPIC: MANAGEMENT AND ADMINISTRATION: Conceptual Clarification<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-568\"><b>Management<\/b> is the process of planning, organizing, staffing, directing, coordinating, and controlling organizational resources to achieve predetermined objectives efficiently and effectively.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-569\"><b>Administration<\/b> is the process of determining organizational objectives, formulating policies, and coordinating resources for achieving those objectives. Administration deals primarily with policy formulation, strategic planning, governance, and organizational direction.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-570\">Definitions<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-571\">Theo Haimann: Administration is the overall determination of policies, setting of major objectives, identification of general purposes and laying down broad programmes.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-572\">E.F.L. Brech: Administration is concerned with determining the objectives and major policies of an organization.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-114\">Characteristics of Administration<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-573\">Determines organizational goals.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-574\">Formulates policies.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-575\">Represents top-level authority.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-576\">Focuses on long-term direction.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-577\">Coordinates organizational activities.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-578\">Allocates strategic resources.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-2-115\">DIFFERENCE BETWEEN MANAGEMENT AND ADMINISTRATION<\/h3><table><tr><th>Management<\/th><th>Administration<\/th><\/tr><tr><td>Implements policies<\/td><td>Formulates policies<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Concerned with execution<\/td><td>Concerned with decision making<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Middle and lower levels<\/td><td>Top management level<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Operational in nature<\/td><td>Strategic in nature<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Focuses on efficiency<\/td><td>Focuses on effectiveness<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Supervises employees<\/td><td>Directs managers<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Concerned with &quot;how&quot; work is done<\/td><td>Concerned with &quot;what&quot; and &quot;why&quot;<\/td><\/tr><\/table><h2 id=\"bus-block-2-116\">5. RELATIONSHIP BETWEEN MANAGEMENT AND ADMINISTRATION<\/h2><p id=\"bus-block-2-p0-579\">Although management and administration have different emphases, they are complementary. Administration establishes organizational objectives and policies, while management implements these policies by coordinating people and resources. Successful engineering organizations require both sound administrative leadership and effective managerial execution.<\/p>", "toc": [{"id": "bus-block-2-0", "t": "Learning Objectives", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-1", "t": "1. Introduction", "lv": 2}, {"id": "bus-block-2-2", "t": "2. Definition of Management", "lv": 2}, {"id": "bus-block-2-3", "t": "Characteristics of Management", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-4", "t": "Importance of Management in Engineering", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-5", "t": "3. Evolution of Management", "lv": 2}, {"id": "bus-block-2-6", "t": "Stage One: Ancient Management (3000 BC–1800 AD)", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-7", "t": "Ancient Egypt", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-8", "t": "China", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-9", "t": "Roman Empire", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-10", "t": "Industrial Crafts", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-11", "t": "Stage Two: Industrial Revolution (1760–1840)", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-12", "t": "Stage Three: Scientific Management School", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-13", "t": "Founder", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-14", "t": "Principles of Scientific Management", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-15", "t": "Contributions", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-16", "t": "Applications in Engineering", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-17", "t": "Criticisms", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-18", "t": "Summary of the Evolution of Management", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-19", "t": "Functions of Management", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-20", "t": "Importance", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-21", "t": "Activities", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-22", "t": "Activities", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-23", "t": "Activities", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-24", "t": "Steps", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-25", "t": "Relationship Among the Functions of Management", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-26", "t": "Application of Management Functions in Engineering", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-27", "t": "Suggested References", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-28", "t": "Lecture II: Management as an Art, a Science, or Both?", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-29", "t": "Learning Objectives", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-30", "t": "Introduction", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-31", "t": "Characteristics of Art", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-32", "t": "3.Creativity: Managers frequently encounter situations without standard solutions. Creativity enables managers to:", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-33", "t": "6. Continuous Improvement: Artists continually improve their skills through practice.", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-34", "t": "Why Management is an Art", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-35", "t": "Meaning of Science", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-36", "t": "Characteristics of Science", "lv": 2}, {"id": "bus-block-2-37", "t": "1. Systematic Body of Knowledge: Science is organized into principles and theories. Management has developed systematic theories such as:", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-38", "t": "2. Universal Principles: Scientific principles apply in many situations.", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-39", "t": "4. Observation and Experimentation: Management knowledge has developed through research and observation.", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-40", "t": "Why Management is a Science", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-41", "t": "Differences Between Management as an Art and Management as a Science", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-42", "t": "Why Management is Both an Art and a Science", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-43", "t": "Engineering Perspective", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-44", "t": "Management as Science in Engineering", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-45", "t": "Management as Art in Engineering", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-46", "t": "Artistic Aspect", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-47", "t": "Advantages of Viewing Management as Both Art and Science", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-48", "t": "Criticisms", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-49", "t": "Contemporary View", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-50", "t": "Conclusion", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-51", "t": "Key Takeaways", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-52", "t": "Topic: Evolution of Management Theories", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-53", "t": "Learning Objectives", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-54", "t": "1. Introduction", "lv": 2}, {"id": "bus-block-2-55", "t": "2. Meaning of Management Theory", "lv": 2}, {"id": "bus-block-2-56", "t": "3. Evolution of Management Theories", "lv": 2}, {"id": "bus-block-2-57", "t": "I. Classical Management Theories (1880–1930)", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-58", "t": "A. Scientific Management Theory", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-59", "t": "Proponent", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-60", "t": "Background", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-61", "t": "Principles of Scientific Management", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-62", "t": "Contributions", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-63", "t": "Advantages", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-64", "t": "Criticisms", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-65", "t": "Engineering Application", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-66", "t": "B. Administrative Management Theory", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-67", "t": "Proponent", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-68", "t": "Fayol's 14 Principles of Management", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-69", "t": "Fayol's Functions of Management", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-70", "t": "Contributions", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-71", "t": "Criticisms", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-72", "t": "Engineering Application", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-73", "t": "C. Bureaucratic Management Theory", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-74", "t": "Proponent", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-75", "t": "Characteristics", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-76", "t": "Advantages", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-77", "t": "Criticisms", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-78", "t": "Engineering Application", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-79", "t": "II. Neo-Classical (Behavioural) Management Theories (1930–1950)", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-80", "t": "A. Human Relations Theory", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-81", "t": "Proponent", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-82", "t": "Hawthorne Studies (1924–1932)", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-83", "t": "Major Findings", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-84", "t": "Contributions", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-85", "t": "Engineering Application", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-86", "t": "B. Behavioural Science Theory", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-87", "t": "Main Ideas", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-88", "t": "Contributions", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-89", "t": "Engineering Application", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-90", "t": "III. Modern Management Theories (1950–Present)", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-91", "t": "A. Systems Theory", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-92", "t": "Proponent", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-93", "t": "Main Idea", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-94", "t": "Components of a System", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-95", "t": "Engineering Application", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-96", "t": "B. Contingency Theory", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-97", "t": "Major Contributors", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-98", "t": "Main Idea", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-99", "t": "Engineering Application", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-100", "t": "C. Quantitative (Management Science) Theory", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-101", "t": "Techniques", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-102", "t": "Engineering Application", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-103", "t": "D. Total Quality Management (TQM)", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-104", "t": "Key Principles", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-105", "t": "E. Lean Management", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-106", "t": "Types of Waste", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-107", "t": "F. Agile Management", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-108", "t": "Comparison of Major Management Theories", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-109", "t": "Relevance of Management Theories to Engineering", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-110", "t": "Contemporary Trends in Management Theory", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-111", "t": "Conclusion", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-112", "t": "Key Takeaways", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-113", "t": "TOPIC: MANAGEMENT AND ADMINISTRATION: Conceptual Clarification", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-114", "t": "Characteristics of Administration", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-115", "t": "DIFFERENCE BETWEEN MANAGEMENT AND ADMINISTRATION", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-2-116", "t": "5. RELATIONSHIP BETWEEN MANAGEMENT AND ADMINISTRATION", "lv": 2}]}]}, "bus-block-3": {"course": "BUS", "ref": "Block 3", "title": "Total Quality Management", "kicker": "Block 3 · Dr O. G. Oyenuga", "words": 10741, "parts": [{"label": "Introduction to Total Quality Management", "note": "OYENUGA, O. G. Ph.D.", "src": "3.1 INTRODUCTION TO TOTAL QUALITY MANAGEMENT.pptx", "html": "<h3 id=\"bus-block-3-0\">NATURE AND SCOPE<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-1\">Total Quality Management = Total + Quality + Management<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-2\">Total means complete amount or a whole, Quality talks about the degree of excellence while, Management means the act of getting things done through other people.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-3\">Thus total-quality-management stands for a management philosophy that emphasizes continuous improvement in all aspects of an organisation’s operations, with the ultimate goal of achieving customer satisfaction.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-4\"><b>Total Quality Management<\/b> is a technique of managing the organisation to achieve excellence with focus on <b>CUSTOMER…<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-5\"><b>Simply…<\/b> It means “Do the right things, right the first time, every time”<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-1\">CORE PRINCIPLES OF TQM<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-6\">Customer Focus<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-7\">Continuous Improvement<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-8\">Employee Involvement<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-9\">Process Approach<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-10\">Integrated System<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-11\">Strategic and Systematic Approach<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-12\">Decision-making based on Data<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-13\">Effective communication<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-2\">DIMENSIONS OF TQM<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-14\">Leadership Commitment: Commitment from top management.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-15\">Customer Satisfaction: Understanding and meeting customer needs not wants.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-16\">Employee Involvement: Training, empowerment, and participation.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-17\">Process Management: Systematic approach to managing processes.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-18\">Continuous Improvement (Kaizen): Ongoing effort to enhance firm’s performance.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-19\">Fact-Based Decision-making: Using data for decision making.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-3\">TQM TOOLS<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-20\"><b>Pareto Charts<\/b>: used for identifying most significant factors.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-21\"><b>Fishbone Diagram<\/b>: used for root cause analysis.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-22\"><b>Control Charts<\/b>: used for monitoring process variation.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-23\"><b>Flow Charts<\/b>: used for visualizing process steps.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-24\"><b>Check Sheets<\/b>: used for data collection.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-25\"><b>Scatter Diagrams<\/b>: used for identifying correlations.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-26\"><b>Histograms<\/b>: used for understanding data distribution.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-4\">OBSTACLES IN IMPLEMENTING TQM<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-27\">Lack of management commitment<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-28\">Poor employee engagement<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-29\">Insufficient training<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-30\">Resistance to change<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-31\">Inadequate resources<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-32\">Misunderstanding of TQM principles<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-33\">Short-term focus over long-term goals<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-5\">STRATEGIES TO OVERCOME TQM BARRIERS<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-34\">Secure top management support<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-35\">Provide continuous training and education<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-36\">Encourage open communication<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-37\">Align TQM with organisational goals<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-38\">Recognise and reward quality improvements.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-6\">ISO 9000<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-39\"><b>ISO 9000 series<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-40\">These are international standards for quality management systems that focus on process consistency and customer satisfaction.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-41\">It helps in documentation and continuous improvement of the production/service system.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-7\">Other Quality Systems<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-42\">ISO 14000 series<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-43\">ISO 14000: Environmental management<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-44\">ISO 14001: Occupational health and safety<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-45\">Six sigma<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-46\">Lean management<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-47\">Baldrige Performance Excellence Program<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-48\">NAFDAC<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-49\">NUC<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-8\">BENEFITS OF TQM<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-50\">Higher customer satisfaction<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-51\">Improved product and service quality<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-52\">Increased efficiency and productivity<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-53\">Better employee morale<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-54\">Enhanced competitive advantage<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p0-55\">Cost reduction and fewer errors<\/li><\/ul>", "toc": [{"id": "bus-block-3-0", "t": "NATURE AND SCOPE", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-1", "t": "CORE PRINCIPLES OF TQM", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-2", "t": "DIMENSIONS OF TQM", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-3", "t": "TQM TOOLS", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-4", "t": "OBSTACLES IN IMPLEMENTING TQM", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-5", "t": "STRATEGIES TO OVERCOME TQM BARRIERS", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-6", "t": "ISO 9000", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-7", "t": "Other Quality Systems", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-8", "t": "BENEFITS OF TQM", "lv": 3}]}, {"label": "Total Quality Management in full", "note": "The eight-topic note the deck condenses", "src": "3.2 total-quality-management-bus-314.pdf", "html": "<h2 id=\"bus-block-3-9\">Topic 1: DEFINITION, PRINCIPLES AND TECHNIQUES OF TOTAL QUALITY MANAGEMENT (TQM)<\/h2><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-10\">Definition<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-1\">Total Quality Management (TQM) is a management philosophy that emphasizes continuous improvement in all aspects of an organization's operations, with the ultimate goal of achieving customer satisfaction. It involves the participation of all employees, from top management to frontline workers, in the pursuit of quality excellence. TQM focuses on customer needs and expectations, process improvement, teamwork, and the use of data and analysis to make informed decisions. Key principles of TQM include customer focus, continuous improvement, employee involvement, process management, and fact-based decision making. TQM originated in manufacturing industries but has since been applied across various sectors, including services, healthcare, and education.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-11\">Principles of TQM<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-2\">The following principles provide a framework for organizations to implement and sustain Total Quality Management practices effectively.<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-3\">Customer Focus: Organizations should understand and meet the needs and expectations of their customers. Customer satisfaction is paramount to the success of TQM. E.g. A restaurant regularly gathers feedback from customers through surveys or comment cards to understand their preferences and improve the dining experience accordingly.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-4\">Continuous Improvement: Also known as Kaizen, this principle emphasizes the ongoing effort to improve products, processes, and services. It involves incremental changes and innovation to achieve higher levels of quality. E.g. A manufacturing company implements regular brainstorming sessions with employees to identify areas for improvement in production processes, resulting in increased efficiency and reduced defects over time. “Six Sigma” concept can also be employed to achieve quality.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-5\">Employee Involvement: TQM encourages the active participation and empowerment of employees at all levels of the organization. Employees are seen as valuable assets who contribute ideas, identify problems, and implement solutions to improve quality. E.g. An IT company encourages employees to participate in cross-functional quality improvement teams, where they collaborate to identify and solve software development challenges, leading to higher-quality products.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-6\">Process Approach: TQM views organizational activities as interconnected processes that contribute to the achievement of quality objectives. Managing and improving these processes systematically leads to better overall performance. E.g. A logistics company maps out its order fulfilment process, identifies bottlenecks, and implements streamlined procedures to reduce lead times and improve customer satisfaction. (lead time refers to the amount of time it takes for an order or shipment to be processed, fulfilled, and delivered to the customer i.e. order processing, preparation, transportation, and delivery.)<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-7\">Systematic Approach to Management: TQM advocates for a systematic and structured approach to managing quality. This involves setting clear goals, implementing standardized procedures, and using data-driven methods for decision making.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-8\">Factual Decision Making: Decisions should be based on data, analysis, and evidence rather than opinions or assumptions. TQM relies on accurate and reliable information to drive continuous improvement efforts. E.g. A healthcare organization analyzes patient outcome data and patient feedback to identify areas for improvement in service delivery, leading to more effective treatment protocols and higher patient satisfaction scores.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-9\">Supplier Relationship Management: TQM recognizes the importance of strong relationships with suppliers. Working closely with suppliers to ensure the quality of inputs is essential for achieving quality objectives. E.g. An automobile manufacturer works closely with its suppliers to establish quality standards and conduct regular audits to ensure that parts meet specifications, resulting in fewer defects in the final product.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-10\">Leadership Involvement and Commitment: Leadership plays a critical role in driving TQM initiatives. Leaders should be actively involved, set a clear vision for quality, and demonstrate commitment to TQM principles through their actions and decisions. E.g. The CEO of a retail company actively champions quality initiatives, allocates resources for training, and recognizes employees who contribute to quality improvement efforts, fostering a culture of excellence throughout the organization.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-11\">Strategic Approach: TQM aligns quality objectives with the organization's overall strategic goals and vision. Quality improvement efforts should support the organization's long-term success and competitiveness.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-12\">Continuous Training and Education: TQM emphasizes the importance of training and developing employees to enhance their skills, knowledge, and capabilities. Continuous learning is essential for maintaining and improving quality standards.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-12\">TQM Techniques/Tools<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-13\">Total Quality Management (TQM) encompasses a variety of techniques and tools aimed at achieving and maintaining high levels of quality throughout an organization. Here are some key techniques commonly associated with TQM:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-14\"><b>Quality Planning<\/b>: This technique involves establishing quality objectives and developing plans to achieve them. Quality planning encompasses activities such as defining quality standards, identifying customer requirements, and determining processes needed to meet quality goals.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-15\"><b>Process Management<\/b>: TQM emphasizes the importance of managing processes effectively to ensure consistent quality. Techniques such as process mapping, flowcharting, and process analysis help identify opportunities for improvement and optimize workflow.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-16\"><b>Continuous Improvement (Kaizen)<\/b>: Kaizen involves making incremental improvements to processes, products, and services over time. Techniques such as Plan- Do-Check-Act (PDCA) cycle, Lean Six Sigma, and quality circles are commonly used to facilitate continuous improvement efforts.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-17\"><b>Statistical Process Control (SPC)<\/b>: SPC involves using statistical methods to monitor and control processes to ensure they operate within acceptable limits. Control charts, Pareto analysis, and statistical sampling are some of the tools used in SPC to identify and address variations in processes.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-18\"><b>Quality Tools<\/b>: TQM utilizes various quality tools to analyze data, solve problems, and make informed decisions. Some common quality tools include:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-19\">Flowcharts: Flowcharts visually represent the steps in a process, helping to identify inefficiencies, bottlenecks, and opportunities for improvement.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-20\">Pareto Analysis: Identifying the most significant factors contributing to quality issues. (80% 20% rule), this is a principle that states that 805 of outcomes (or outputs) result from 20% of all causes (or inputs) for any given event. In business, a goal of the 80-20 rule is to identify inputs that are potentially the most productive and make them the priority.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-21\">Cause-and-Effect Diagrams (Fishbone Diagrams): A fishbone-like diagram that helps team members analyse a problem’s root causes by identifying potential causes and their relationships. Its help team focus on the root cause rather than symptoms.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-22\">Histograms: Visualizing data distribution to identify patterns and trends. Its similar to a bar chat.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-23\">Scatter Diagrams: Examining relationships between variables to identify correlations. A graph in which the values of two variables are plotted along two axes, the pattern of the resulting points revealing any correlation present. It provides insights into how two variables affect each other when they are plotted over a graph.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-24\">Six Sigma: Six Sigma is a data-driven methodology focused on reducing defects and variations in processes to achieve near-perfect quality. It involves a structured approach known as DMAIC (Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control) for problem-solving and process improvement.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-25\">Check Sheets: A check sheet is a simple document used for collecting and analysing data to facilitate decision making. This is a generic data collection and analysis tool that can be adapted for a wide variety of purposes and is considered one of the seven basic quality tools.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-26\"><b>Supplier Management<\/b>: Ensuring quality throughout the supply chain is essential for TQM. Techniques such as supplier audits, performance evaluations, and supplier partnerships help maintain high standards of quality in inputs and components.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-27\"><b>Employee Involvement and Training<\/b>: TQM emphasizes the importance of involving employees at all levels in quality improvement efforts. Techniques such as training programs, suggestion systems, and empowerment initiatives encourage employees to contribute ideas, identify problems, and actively participate in quality initiatives.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-28\"><b>Customer Feedback and Satisfaction<\/b>: Gathering and analyzing customer feedback is crucial for TQM. Techniques such as customer surveys, focus groups, and complaint handling systems help organizations understand customer needs and expectations, leading to improvements in products and services.<\/li><\/ol><h2 id=\"bus-block-3-13\">Topic 2: SCOPE AND PURPOSE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT<\/h2><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-14\">Process and Product Quality<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-29\">Total Quality Management (TQM) focuses on both process and product quality as integral parts of its philosophy. Here's how TQM addresses each aspect:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-30\">Process Quality:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-31\">TQM emphasizes the importance of effective processes in achieving high- quality outcomes. It involves continuous improvement of processes to enhance efficiency, reduce waste, and meet customer requirements consistently.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-32\">Process quality in TQM involves several key principles:<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-33\">Process Mapping: Understanding and documenting processes to identify areas for improvement and optimization.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-34\">Standardization: Establishing standardized procedures and protocols to ensure consistency and reliability in operations.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-35\">Continuous Improvement: Encouraging ongoing evaluation and refinement of processes through methods like Kaizen (continuous improvement) to drive incremental enhancements.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-36\">Employee Involvement: Engaging employees at all levels in process improvement initiatives to leverage their insights and expertise.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-37\">Error Prevention: Implementing measures to prevent errors and defects from occurring in processes, such as error-proofing techniques and thorough training.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-38\">TQM uses various tools and techniques such as statistical process control (SPC), Six Sigma, and Lean methodologies to monitor, analyze, and optimize processes continually.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-39\">Product Quality:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-40\">Product quality is a core focus of TQM, aiming to deliver goods and services that consistently meet or exceed customer expectations.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-41\">TQM principles applied to product quality include:<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-42\">Customer Focus: Understanding and prioritizing customer needs and expectations to design products that provide value and satisfaction.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-43\">Design Quality: Integrating quality considerations into the product design phase to ensure robustness, reliability, and functionality.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-44\">Supplier Partnerships: Collaborating closely with suppliers to maintain high standards of raw materials and components, as well as to facilitate continuous improvement.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-45\">Quality Control: Implementing rigorous quality control measures throughout the production process to detect and rectify defects before products reach customers.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-46\">Feedback Loops: Establishing mechanisms for gathering customer feedback and using it to drive product improvements and innovations.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-47\">TQM emphasizes the concept of &quot;zero defects,&quot; aiming for error-free production through proactive quality management practices.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-15\">Ways to Improve Process and Product Quality<\/h3><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-16\">1. Define quality goals<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-48\">The first step to improve quality is to define what quality means for your product and process. You need to identify the customer requirements, specifications, standards, and expectations that your product and process must meet or exceed. You also need to establish measurable and realistic quality goals that align with your business objectives and strategy. You can use tools like SMART (specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound) criteria, quality function deployment (QFD), or voice of the customer (VOC) to define your quality goals.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-17\">2. Analyze Current Performance<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-49\">The next step is to analyze how well your product and process are performing against your quality goals. You need to collect and analyze data on the quality characteristics, defects, variations, and root causes of your product and process. Tools like control charts, Pareto charts, fishbone diagrams can be used to monitor and analyze the current performance.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-18\">3. Implement Improvement Actions<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-50\">The third step is to implement improvement actions that will address the gaps and problems identified in the previous step. You need to prioritize the most critical and feasible improvement opportunities and design solutions that will eliminate or reduce the root causes of poor quality. You can use tools like brainstorming, benchmarking, design of experiments (DOE), or failure mode and effects analysis (FMEA) to generate and test improvement ideas.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-19\">4. Evaluate Results and Feedback<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-51\">The final step is to evaluate the results and feedback of your improvement actions. You need to measure and compare the before and after performance of your product and process and determine if your improvement actions have achieved your quality goals. You also need to solicit feedback from your customers, employees, and stakeholders on the effectiveness and satisfaction of your improvement actions. You can use tools like surveys, audits, or reviews to evaluate results and feedback.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-20\">5. Sustain and Standardize Improvements<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-52\">Improving quality is not a one-off project, but a continuous process that requires ongoing monitoring and adjustment. You need to sustain and standardize the improvements you have made by documenting and communicating the best practices, training and empowering your employees, and rewarding and recognizing your achievements. You can use tools like standard operating procedures (SOPs), checklists, or visual management to sustain and standardize improvements.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-21\">6. Learn and Innovate for Excellence<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-53\">Improving quality is also a learning and innovation opportunity that can help you achieve excellence in your product and process. You need to learn from your successes and failures, identify new challenges and opportunities, and seek new ways to enhance your quality performance. You can use tools like Kaizen, Six Sigma, or Lean to learn and innovate for excellence.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-22\">Quality Assurance and Standards<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-54\">Total Quality Management (TQM) is a comprehensive approach to quality management that emphasizes the involvement of all employees in continuous improvement to meet or exceed customer expectations. Central to the TQM philosophy are quality assurance (QA) practices and adherence to established standards, which collectively contribute to the delivery of high- quality products and services. Quality Assurance (QA) in TQM: Quality assurance in TQM involves systematic processes and procedures to ensure that products or services consistently meet specified quality requirements. It encompasses activities aimed at preventing defects, rather than merely detecting and correcting them. Key aspects of QA in TQM include:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-55\">Process Adherence: Ensuring that standardized processes and procedures are followed rigorously at every stage of production or service delivery.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-56\">Continuous Improvement: Encouraging employees to identify opportunities for improvement and innovation in processes and products.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-57\">Employee Training: Providing comprehensive training and development programs to equip employees with the skills and knowledge necessary to maintain quality standards.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-58\">Customer Focus: Placing a strong emphasis on understanding and fulfilling customer needs and expectations through proactive quality management practices.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-23\">Standards in TQM<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-59\">Standards serve as benchmarks or criteria against which the quality, safety, and performance of products and processes are evaluated in TQM. These standards can be internal, industry- specific, or internationally recognized. Key roles of standards in TQM include:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-60\">Guidance: Providing guidelines and best practices for quality management processes, ensuring consistency and reliability.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-61\">Benchmarking: Facilitating comparison with industry peers and competitors to identify areas for improvement and set performance goals.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-62\">Compliance: Ensuring conformity with regulatory requirements and industry standards, enhancing trust and credibility.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-63\">Continuous Improvement: Serving as a basis for evaluating and refining existing processes and implementing new quality initiatives. Integration of QA and Standards in TQM: In TQM, quality assurance practices and adherence to standards are integrated into every aspect of organizational operations. By fostering a culture of quality and continuous improvement, TQM emphasizes the importance of proactive quality management rather than reactive defect correction. Through robust QA processes and adherence to recognized standards, organizations can enhance efficiency, reduce waste, and consistently deliver products and services that meet or exceed customer expectations.<\/li><\/ol><h2 id=\"bus-block-3-24\">Topic 3: Developing a Strategic Plan for Total Quality Management<\/h2><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-64\">Developing a strategic plan for Total Quality Management (TQM) is essential for several reasons:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-65\"><b>Alignment with Organizational Goals<\/b>: A strategic plan for TQM ensures that quality management efforts are aligned with the broader strategic objectives of the organization. By identifying quality goals and objectives that support the organization's mission and vision, the strategic plan ensures that TQM initiatives contribute directly to overall business success.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-66\"><b>Clear Direction and Focus<\/b>: TQM involves a wide range of activities and initiatives aimed at improving processes, products, and services. A strategic plan provides clarity on where to focus efforts and resources to achieve the greatest impact. It outlines specific priorities, timelines, and responsibilities, helping to prevent fragmentation and ensure a coordinated approach to quality improvement.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-67\"><b>Resource Allocation<\/b>: Developing a strategic plan for TQM allows organizations to allocate resources effectively. By identifying the necessary resources—such as financial, human, and technological—and prioritizing their allocation based on strategic objectives, the organization can optimize its investments in quality management initiatives.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-68\"><b>Risk Management<\/b>: TQM strategic planning involves assessing potential risks and challenges that may impact quality performance. By conducting a thorough analysis of internal and external factors that could affect quality outcomes, organizations can proactively identify and mitigate risks, ensuring the success and sustainability of TQM initiatives.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-69\"><b>Continuous Improvement<\/b>: TQM is rooted in the philosophy of continuous improvement. A strategic plan provides a framework for systematically identifying opportunities for improvement, setting targets for performance enhancement, and monitoring progress over time. It allows organizations to adapt to changing market conditions, customer preferences, and technological advancements, ensuring that quality management practices remain relevant and effective.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-70\"><b>Enhanced Customer Satisfaction<\/b>: Quality management is ultimately about meeting or exceeding customer expectations. A strategic plan for TQM helps organizations focus on understanding customer needs, preferences, and feedback. By incorporating customer-centric goals and metrics into the strategic plan, organizations can drive improvements in product and service quality, leading to higher levels of customer satisfaction and loyalty.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-71\"><b>Competitive Advantage<\/b>: In today's competitive business environment, quality has become a critical differentiator. A strategic plan for TQM enables organizations to develop unique capabilities and competencies that set them apart from competitors. By consistently delivering high-quality products and services, organizations can build a reputation for excellence, attract and retain customers, and sustain long-term competitive advantage.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-25\">Benefits of Total Quality Management<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-72\">Total Quality Management (TQM) offers several benefits for an organization, including:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-73\">Increased Productivity: Consistent process improvement, enhanced worker engagement, and decreased inefficiencies can yield increased productivity in businesses' goods and/or services.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-74\">Removal of Defects and Waste: Continuous improvement and a strategic approach eliminate inefficiencies, waste, and defects that degrade products. This approach helps in the efficient manufacturing of high-quality products.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-75\">Strengthen Competitive Position: Businesses rise if they generate high-quality goods and/or services in the market. This consistency in providing quality products increases competencies with rivals by attracting more customers and strengthening the position in the market.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-76\">Enhanced Profitability: The higher the quality of the products, the higher their value in the market. Consistent satisfaction with the quality of products builds trust among consumers, leading them to stay loyal to the products. This leads to an increase in profitability and higher revenues.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-77\">Greater Market Share: The more satisfied the customers are with the higher quality of the goods and/or services, the higher the reliability, revenue growth, and market share through word-of-mouth marketing or upselling by the consumers.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-78\">Adaptability to Change: Total Quality Management promotes agility and adaptability through consistent improvement in the process. Continuous learning makes organizations susceptible to changes in technology, market, and customer demands.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-79\">Higher Job Security: One of the benefits of TQM is increasing job security through efficiently establishing the organization in the business market. This stability of the organization with improved work culture and increased opportunities ensures higher job security for employees.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-80\">Improved Customer Satisfaction: Consumer satisfaction motivates organizations to keep delivering high-quality products in the market. One of the key benefits of TQM is that it prioritizes the customer’s requirements and expectations and encourages innovation, leading to enhanced satisfaction and loyalty of the consumers for a longer duration.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-81\">Upgraded Processes: Monitoring processes and keeping them updated according to the regulatory bodies, market trends, and patterns contributes to the success of the products. Once the goods and/or services meet the business goals, the business remains competitive.<\/li><\/ol><h2 id=\"bus-block-3-26\">Topic 4: TQM BEST PRACTICES<\/h2><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-82\">Total Quality Management (TQM) encompasses a range of practices aimed at improving quality and efficiency throughout an organization. Here are some best practices commonly associated with TQM:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-83\">Leadership Commitment: Top management commitment is crucial for the successful implementation of TQM. Leaders need to actively support and promote quality initiatives throughout the organization.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-84\">Customer Focus: Understanding and meeting customer needs and expectations is fundamental to TQM. Organizations should gather feedback from customers regularly and use it to drive continuous improvement.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-85\">Employee Involvement: TQM emphasizes the involvement of all employees in quality improvement efforts. Employees should be empowered to identify problems, suggest solutions, and participate in decision-making processes.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-86\">Continuous Improvement (Kaizen): TQM is based on the principle of continuous improvement. Organizations should constantly strive to improve processes, products, and services to enhance quality and efficiency.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-87\">Process Approach: TQM encourages a systematic approach to managing processes. This involves identifying, understanding, and optimizing key processes to achieve desired outcomes.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-88\">Data-Driven Decision Making: TQM relies on data and evidence to drive decision- making. Organizations should collect and analyze relevant data to identify trends, root causes of problems, and areas for improvement.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-89\">Supplier Relationships: TQM extends beyond the organization to include suppliers and partners in the supply chain. Building strong relationships with suppliers and working collaboratively with them can improve overall quality and efficiency.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-90\">Training and Development: Providing training and development opportunities for employees is essential for building the skills and knowledge required to support TQM initiatives.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-91\">Benchmarking: Comparing performance against industry benchmarks and best practices can provide valuable insights for improvement.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-92\">Recognition and Reward: Recognizing and rewarding individuals and teams for their contributions to quality improvement can help sustain momentum and motivation.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-93\">Quality Assurance: Implementing robust quality assurance processes ensures that products and services meet established standards and specifications.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-94\">Quality Circles: Quality circles involve small groups of employees who meet regularly to identify, analyze, and solve work-related problems. This participatory approach fosters collaboration and innovation.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-95\">Documented Processes: Documenting processes, procedures, and standards helps ensure consistency and clarity, making it easier for employees to follow best practices.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-96\">Culture of Quality: Creating a culture that values quality, continuous improvement, and excellence is fundamental to the success of TQM. This involves promoting open communication, trust, and accountability throughout the organization.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-27\">Problems Associated with TQM<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-97\">Improving quality within an organization can be a complex endeavour, and several challenges may arise along the way. Here are some common problems associated with improving quality:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-98\">Resistance to Change: Employees may resist changes to established processes or methods, especially if they perceive them as threatening or disruptive to their roles or routines.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-99\">Lack of Leadership Support: Without visible and committed leadership support, quality improvement initiatives may lack direction, resources, and momentum.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-100\">Insufficient Resources: Limited budgets, time constraints, and competing priorities can hinder efforts to invest in quality improvement activities, such as training, technology, or process redesign.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-101\">Inadequate Communication: Poor communication can lead to misunderstandings, confusion, and resistance among employees. Clear, consistent communication is essential to ensure alignment and engagement in quality improvement efforts.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-102\">Short-Term Focus: Organizations may prioritize short-term gains over long-term sustainability, leading to superficial improvements or neglect of underlying systemic issues.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-103\">Silos and Fragmentation: Lack of collaboration and coordination between departments or functional areas can result in fragmented approaches to quality improvement, inhibiting holistic solutions.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-104\">Overemphasis on Metrics: While metrics are important for measuring progress and performance, an overemphasis on quantitative measures can lead to a narrow focus on numbers at the expense of qualitative aspects of quality.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-105\">Inadequate Training and Skills: Employees may lack the necessary skills, knowledge, or training to effectively participate in quality improvement initiatives or implement new processes and technologies.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-106\">Complexity and Red Tape: Bureaucratic obstacles, complex procedures, and excessive red tape can impede the agility and responsiveness needed to implement quality improvements quickly and efficiently.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-107\">Resistance from Suppliers or Partners: Suppliers or external partners may be resistant to changes in processes or quality standards, which can affect the organization's ability to maintain consistency and reliability across the supply chain.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-108\">Cultural Barriers: Organizational culture, norms, and values may not align with quality improvement principles, making it challenging to foster a culture of continuous improvement and accountability.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-109\">Inadequate Feedback Loops: Without mechanisms for gathering and acting on feedback from customers, employees, and other stakeholders, organizations may struggle to identify areas for improvement or measure the impact of quality initiatives.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-28\">Quality Management Process<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-110\">Quality management is a crucial aspect of ensuring that products or services consistently meet or exceed customer expectations. The process of quality management typically involves several steps:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-111\">Planning: This involves setting quality objectives and determining the processes needed to deliver a product or service that meets customer requirements.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-112\">Quality Assurance (QA): QA involves the systematic monitoring and evaluation of the various processes used to produce a product or deliver a service. It aims to ensure that the processes are adequate and that products or services meet quality standards.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-113\">Quality Control (QC): QC involves the inspection and testing of products or services to ensure that they meet predefined quality standards. This can include both physical testing and evaluation of processes.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-114\">Continuous Improvement: Also known as continuous quality improvement (CQI) or continuous improvement process (CIP), this step involves regularly reviewing processes and procedures to identify areas for improvement. This can include implementing changes to enhance efficiency, reduce defects, and improve overall quality.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-115\">Feedback Loop: Establishing mechanisms for collecting feedback from customers, stakeholders, and employees is essential for identifying issues and opportunities for improvement. This feedback loop feeds into the continuous improvement process.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-116\">Training and Education: Ensuring that employees are adequately trained and educated on quality standards, processes, and techniques is crucial for maintaining consistent quality levels.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-117\">Documentation: Keeping detailed records of quality-related activities, including processes, inspections, tests, and improvements, is important for traceability and accountability.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-118\">Risk Management: Identifying potential risks to quality and implementing measures to mitigate or eliminate them is essential for ensuring consistent quality.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-29\">Concepts of Customer Value in TQM<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-119\">Total Quality Management (TQM) emphasizes meeting customer needs and expectations as a fundamental principle. Customer value in TQM refers to the perception of worth or benefit that a customer receives from a product or service. It's about delivering quality that aligns with what the customer desires and values. Here's how the concept of customer value fits into TQM:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-120\">Customer Focus: TQM places a strong emphasis on understanding customer requirements and preferences. Organizations actively engage with customers to gather feedback, understand their needs, and incorporate these insights into product or service development processes.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-121\">Quality from the Customer's Perspective: TQM views quality not just as meeting internal standards but as meeting or exceeding customer expectations. Therefore, the definition of quality is based on how well a product or service satisfies the customer's needs and delivers value to them.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-122\">Continuous Improvement: TQM advocates for continuous improvement in processes, products, and services to enhance customer value. This involves identifying areas for improvement based on customer feedback, analyzing processes to eliminate defects or inefficiencies, and implementing changes to enhance the overall value delivered to customers.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-123\">Customer Satisfaction and Loyalty: TQM recognizes that satisfied customers are more likely to be loyal customers and advocates for the organization. By consistently delivering high-quality products or services that meet or exceed customer expectations, organizations can build strong relationships with customers and enhance their loyalty over time.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-124\">Value-Added Activities: TQM encourages organizations to focus on value-added activities that directly contribute to meeting customer needs and delivering value. This involves streamlining processes, eliminating waste, and optimizing resources to ensure that every activity adds value from the customer's perspective.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-125\">Employee Involvement: TQM recognizes that employees play a crucial role in delivering value to customers. By involving employees in quality improvement initiatives and empowering them to identify and address customer needs, organizations can better align their efforts with customer expectations and enhance overall customer value. The concept of customer value in TQM underscores the importance of understanding, meeting, and exceeding customer needs and expectations through a relentless focus on quality, continuous improvement, and employee involvement. By prioritizing customer value, organizations can build stronger customer relationships, enhance satisfaction and loyalty, and drive long-term success.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-30\">TOPIC 5: EMERGING TENDENCIES TOWARD GLOBAL COMPETITIVENESS IN TQM<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-126\">Total Quality Management (TQM) is a management approach aimed at continuously improving the quality of products and services, involving all employees in the pursuit of customer satisfaction. In the context of global competitiveness, several emerging tendencies in TQM have been observed:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-127\">Customer-Centric Approach: Emphasis on understanding and meeting the diverse needs and preferences of customers worldwide. This involves gathering feedback, analyzing data, and tailoring products and services to different markets while maintaining high quality standards.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-128\">Data-Driven Decision Making: Utilizing big data analytics, machine learning, and other advanced technologies to collect, analyze, and interpret vast amounts of data related to quality processes. This allows for more informed decision-making, predictive maintenance, and identification of areas for improvement.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-129\">Supply Chain Integration: Recognizing the interconnectedness of global supply chains, TQM practices now extend beyond organizational boundaries to include suppliers and partners. Collaborative efforts ensure the quality of inputs and outputs throughout the supply chain, minimizing defects and enhancing overall competitiveness.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-130\">Agility and Flexibility: In a rapidly changing global marketplace, organizations need to be agile and adaptable. TQM frameworks are evolving to incorporate principles of flexibility, responsiveness, and innovation, allowing companies to quickly adjust to market demands and stay ahead of competitors.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-131\">Continuous Improvement Culture: Beyond traditional quality control measures, there's a growing emphasis on fostering a culture of continuous improvement at all levels of the organization. This involves empowering employees to identify problems, suggest solutions, and participate in initiatives aimed at enhancing quality and efficiency.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-132\">Global Standards and Certifications: Increasingly, companies are seeking internationally recognized quality standards and certifications to demonstrate their commitment to quality excellence on a global scale. Adherence to standards such as ISO 9001 ensures consistency, reliability, and compatibility with global supply chain partners.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-133\">Risk Management and Resilience: TQM practices now include proactive risk management strategies to anticipate and mitigate potential disruptions to quality and operations. This resilience is essential for maintaining competitiveness in the face of unforeseen events such as natural disasters, geopolitical instability, or supply chain disruptions.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-134\">Ethical and Sustainable Practices: Quality management is expanding to encompass broader ethical and sustainability considerations. This includes ensuring compliance with environmental regulations, promoting fair labor practices, and fostering transparency and accountability throughout the value chain.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-31\">Six-Sigma Management in TQM<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-135\">The six sigma concept was developed by Motorola in the 1980s and popularized by General Electric, Six Sigma focuses on reducing defects and variation in processes to achieve near- perfect quality levels (3.4 defects per million opportunities). Six Sigma uses a structured approach called DMAIC (Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control) to tackle process improvement projects. Six Sigma quality&quot; means that a process operates with minimal variation, resulting in consistently high-quality outputs.<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-136\"><b>Define<\/b>: Clearly define the problem or opportunity for improvement, along with project goals and customer requirements.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-137\"><b>Measure<\/b>: Collect and analyze data to understand the current performance of the process and establish a baseline.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-138\"><b>Analyze<\/b>: Identify root causes of defects or variations within the process using statistical analysis and problem-solving techniques.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-139\"><b>Improve<\/b>: Develop and implement solutions to address identified root causes and improve process performance.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-140\"><b>Control<\/b>: Establish controls and monitoring systems to sustain improvements over time and prevent regression.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-32\">Some of the key tools used in Six Sigma include:<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-141\">Process Mapping: Visualizing and understanding the current state of processes.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-142\">Statistical Analysis: Using statistical tools like regression analysis, hypothesis testing, and design of experiments to identify root causes and make data-driven decisions.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-143\">Control Charts: Monitoring process performance over time to detect any deviations from the mean.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-144\">Failure Mode and Effects Analysis (FMEA): Identifying potential failure modes in a process and prioritizing them based on severity, occurrence, and detection.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-145\">Lean Principles: Integration of lean tools and techniques to eliminate waste and streamline processes. The five lean principles include value, value stream, flow, pull, and perfection. These five principles rely on the two pillars of lean: continuous improvement and respect for people.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-33\">The Lean Principle in TQM<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-146\">In Total Quality Management (TQM), the lean principle represents a key approach to enhancing efficiency, eliminating waste, and optimizing processes to deliver higher value to customers. While lean principles originated in the Toyota Production System (TPS), they have been integrated into TQM practices to achieve better overall quality management. Here's an explanation of the lean principle within the context of TQM:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-147\"><b>Elimination of Waste<\/b>: One of the fundamental concepts of lean thinking is the identification and elimination of waste, or &quot;muda&quot; in Japanese. Waste can manifest in various forms, including overproduction, excess inventory, unnecessary motion, waiting time, defects, and underutilized talent. In TQM, the focus on waste elimination aligns with the broader goal of delivering quality products or services efficiently.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-148\"><b>Continuous Improvement (Kaizen)<\/b>: Lean principles emphasize continuous improvement, or &quot;Kaizen,&quot; which involves making small, incremental changes to processes over time. This philosophy encourages employees at all levels of the organization to actively participate in identifying opportunities for improvement and implementing solutions. In TQM, the integration of Kaizen fosters a culture of continuous learning and adaptation, leading to sustained improvements in quality and efficiency.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-149\"><b>Value Stream Mapping<\/b>: Value stream mapping is a lean tool used to visualize and analyze the flow of materials and information through a process from the perspective of adding value to the customer. By identifying value-added and non-value-added activities, organizations can streamline processes, reduce lead times, and eliminate bottlenecks. In TQM, value stream mapping helps identify areas for improvement and prioritize initiatives that contribute to overall quality enhancement.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-150\"><b>Just-in-Time (JIT) Production<\/b>: JIT is a lean manufacturing strategy aimed at producing goods or delivering services at the exact moment they are needed, thereby minimizing inventory holding costs and reducing lead times. JIT principles emphasize the synchronization of production with customer demand, smooth flow of materials, and pull-based replenishment systems. In TQM, the adoption of JIT practices contributes to improved responsiveness to customer needs and reduced waste in the form of excess inventory and overproduction.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-151\"><b>Respect for People<\/b>: Lean principles emphasize the importance of respecting and empowering employees by involving them in decision-making processes, providing opportunities for skill development, and fostering a supportive work environment. In TQM, the integration of this principle reinforces the notion that quality improvement efforts are driven by the collective expertise and commitment of employees throughout the organization.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-34\">Benchmarking in TQM<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-152\">Benchmarking is a strategic tool commonly used in Total Quality Management (TQM) to improve processes, products, and services by comparing performance metrics with those of industry leaders, competitors, or best practices. Here's how benchmarking is applied within the framework of TQM:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-153\">Identifying Benchmarking Targets: In TQM, organizations first identify key performance indicators (KPIs) relevant to quality, such as defect rates, cycle times, customer satisfaction scores, or process efficiency metrics. These KPIs serve as benchmarks against which the organization's performance will be measured.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-154\">Selecting Benchmarking Partners: Organizations choose benchmarking partners or targets—other companies or organizations that are known for their excellence in the areas being assessed. These partners could be industry leaders, competitors, or organizations with best practices that align with the organization's goals.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-155\">Gathering Data: Once benchmarking partners are identified, data related to the selected performance metrics is collected from both the organization and the benchmarking partners. This data may include process documentation, performance reports, customer feedback, and other relevant information.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-156\">Analyzing Performance Gaps: The collected data is then analyzed to identify performance gaps between the organization and the benchmarking partners. This analysis helps pinpoint areas where the organization falls short of best-in-class performance and where improvement efforts should be focused.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-157\">Understanding Best Practices: Through benchmarking, organizations gain insights into the best practices, techniques, and strategies employed by benchmarking partners to achieve superior performance. This understanding allows organizations to learn from others' successes and identify opportunities for improvement.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-158\">Developing Improvement Plans: Based on the analysis of performance gaps and best practices, organizations develop improvement plans or action strategies aimed at closing the identified gaps and achieving performance levels comparable to or better than the benchmarking partners.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-159\">Implementing Changes: Improvement plans are implemented through systematic changes to processes, systems, or organizational practices. This may involve redesigning workflows, implementing new technologies, providing training to employees, or making other organizational changes to enhance quality and performance.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-160\">Monitoring Progress: Throughout the implementation phase, organizations continuously monitor progress against the established benchmarks and performance targets. Regular performance reviews allow organizations to track the effectiveness of improvement initiatives and make adjustments as needed.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-161\">Sustaining Improvement: Benchmarking is not a one-time activity but an ongoing process. To sustain improvement in quality and performance, organizations must continually reassess their performance against benchmarks, identify new opportunities for improvement, and adapt to changing market conditions and customer needs.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-35\">Importance of “Benchmarking” in TQM<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-162\">Benchmarking plays a crucial role in Total Quality Management (TQM) for several reasons:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-163\">Identifying Best Practices: Benchmarking allows organizations to compare their processes, practices, and performance metrics against those of industry leaders or competitors. By studying organizations known for their excellence in quality management, TQM practitioners can identify best practices and adopt them to improve their own processes.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-164\">Setting Performance Standards: Benchmarking helps organizations establish performance standards and benchmarks for quality-related metrics such as defect rates, cycle times, customer satisfaction scores, and product/service features. By benchmarking against industry leaders or recognized standards, organizations can set ambitious yet achievable targets for quality improvement.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-165\">Gap Analysis: Benchmarking facilitates a thorough gap analysis, enabling organizations to identify areas where they fall short of best-in-class performance. By comparing their current performance against benchmark data, organizations can pinpoint specific weaknesses or inefficiencies in their processes and prioritize improvement efforts accordingly.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-166\">Learning from Others: Benchmarking provides valuable learning opportunities for organizations seeking to enhance their quality management practices. By studying the strategies, techniques, and innovations implemented by top performers in their industry or related sectors, organizations can gain insights into new approaches and methodologies for achieving excellence in quality.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-167\">Driving Continuous Improvement: TQM is inherently focused on continuous improvement, and benchmarking serves as a catalyst for ongoing enhancement efforts. By regularly benchmarking their performance against industry standards or competitors, organizations can identify opportunities for further optimization and innovation, ensuring that they remain competitive in the long term.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-168\">Enhancing Customer Satisfaction: Benchmarking helps organizations understand customer expectations and industry norms, allowing them to align their quality management efforts more closely with customer needs and preferences. By benchmarking customer satisfaction scores and feedback against industry benchmarks, organizations can identify areas for improvement and implement changes to enhance the overall customer experience.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-169\">Stimulating Innovation: Benchmarking encourages organizations to explore new ideas and approaches to quality management. By studying innovative practices implemented by industry leaders or emerging competitors, organizations can identify opportunities for innovation and differentiation, driving continuous improvement and fostering a culture of innovation within the organization.<\/li><\/ol><h2 id=\"bus-block-3-36\">Topic 6: QUALITY CONTROL Quality Control Planning<\/h2><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-170\">Quality planning is a critical component of Total Quality Management (TQM) and involves the systematic development of processes, procedures, and strategies to ensure that products or services meet or exceed customer expectations. Here's a breakdown of what quality planning entails:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-171\">Identification of Customer Requirements: • Quality planning begins with a thorough understanding of customer needs, preferences, and expectations. This involves gathering feedback, conducting market research, and analyzing customer data to identify key requirements.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-172\">Establishment of Quality Objectives: • Based on customer requirements and organizational goals, quality objectives are defined. These objectives outline specific, measurable targets for quality improvement, such as defect reduction, enhanced customer satisfaction, or increased efficiency.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-173\">Development of Quality Plans: • Quality plans outline the actions, processes, and resources needed to achieve quality objectives. They specify who is responsible for each task, the timeline for implementation, and the methods for measuring progress and success.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-174\">Process Design and Documentation: • Quality planning involves designing processes and workflows that support the achievement of quality objectives. This includes identifying critical process steps, defining quality control measures, and documenting procedures to ensure consistency and repeatability.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-175\">Resource Allocation: • Quality planning requires the allocation of resources, including personnel, equipment, and budget, to support quality improvement initiatives. Resources are allocated based on the priority of quality objectives and the expected impact on customer satisfaction.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-176\">Risk Assessment and Mitigation: • Quality planning involves identifying potential risks and uncertainties that could impact product or service quality. Risk assessment techniques such as Failure Mode and Effects Analysis (FMEA) are used to prioritize risks and develop mitigation strategies.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-177\">Training and Development: • Employees play a crucial role in quality planning and implementation. Training and development programs are provided to ensure that employees have the necessary skills and knowledge to meet quality objectives and adhere to established procedures.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-178\">Continuous Improvement: • Quality planning is an iterative process that emphasizes continuous improvement. Feedback mechanisms are established to monitor performance, gather customer input, and identify opportunities for refinement and enhancement.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-37\">Software Quality Control<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-179\">Software quality control is a subset of software quality management focused on verifying that software products meet specified quality standards and requirements. It involves the systematic examination of software components, processes, and outputs to ensure they conform to predefined quality criteria. Key aspects of software quality control include:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-180\">Defect Identification: The process of identifying defects or deviations from quality standards in software artifacts such as code, documentation, or user interfaces. This may involve various activities such as code reviews, inspections, and testing.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-181\">Verification and Validation (V&amp;V): Verification involves ensuring that the software product conforms to its specifications and requirements, while validation involves ensuring that the software meets the needs and expectations of its users. Both verification and validation are essential components of software quality control.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-182\">Testing: Testing is a fundamental aspect of software quality control, involving the execution of software components or systems to identify defects and ensure they function correctly. This includes various types of testing such as unit testing, integration testing, system testing, regression testing, and acceptance testing.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-183\">Quality Assurance Activities: Quality control is often complemented by quality assurance activities aimed at preventing defects from occurring in the first place. This may include defining and implementing processes, standards, and best practices to promote quality throughout the software development lifecycle.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-184\">Documentation and Reporting: Quality control involves documenting and reporting on the results of quality assurance activities, including defects identified, test outcomes, and compliance with quality standards. This documentation provides transparency and accountability regarding the quality of the software product.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-185\">Continuous Improvement: Software quality control is an iterative process that involves continuously monitoring and improving the quality of software products and processes. Feedback from quality control activities is used to identify areas for improvement and implement corrective actions to prevent similar issues in the future.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-38\">Implementing Software Quality Control<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-186\">Software quality control involves ensuring that software products meet specified quality standards and requirements. Here's a structured approach to implementing software quality control:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-187\">Requirements Analysis: Start by clearly defining and documenting the requirements for the software product. This includes functional requirements (what the software should do) and non-functional requirements (performance, security, usability, etc.).<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-188\">Quality Planning: Develop a quality plan outlining the quality objectives, standards, processes, and tools that will be used throughout the software development lifecycle (SDLC). Identify key quality attributes that need to be maintained, such as reliability, efficiency, maintainability, and usability.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-189\">Code Reviews and Inspections: Implement code reviews and inspections to ensure that the code meets coding standards, design guidelines, and best practices. This helps identify defects early in the development process and ensures that the codebase remains clean and maintainable.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-190\">Testing Strategies: Develop comprehensive testing strategies that include unit testing, integration testing, system testing, regression testing, and acceptance testing. Each type of testing focuses on different aspects of the software's functionality and helps uncover defects at various stages of development.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-191\">Automated Testing: Implement automated testing frameworks and tools to streamline the testing process and improve test coverage. Automated tests can be run frequently and consistently, allowing for early detection of regressions and ensuring the stability of the software product.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-192\">Continuous Integration and Deployment (CI/CD): Implement CI/CD pipelines to automate the build, testing, and deployment processes. This ensures that changes to the codebase are continuously integrated, tested, and deployed, reducing the risk of introducing defects into the production environment.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-193\">Defect Tracking and Management: Use a defect tracking system to capture, prioritize, and manage defects identified during testing and reviews. Ensure that defects are promptly addressed, and appropriate corrective actions are taken to prevent recurrence.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-194\">Performance Monitoring: Implement tools for monitoring the performance of the software application in real-time. This helps identify performance bottlenecks, resource constraints, and scalability issues, allowing for timely optimization and tuning.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-195\">Security Testing: Conduct security testing to identify and address potential vulnerabilities in the software application. This includes penetration testing, vulnerability scanning, code analysis, and compliance checks to ensure that the software is secure against potential threats.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-196\">Documentation and Training: Document the software quality control processes, procedures, and best practices to ensure consistency and clarity. Provide training to team members on quality control methodologies, tools, and techniques to empower them to contribute effectively to the quality of the software product.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-197\">Feedback and Continuous Improvement: Gather feedback from stakeholders, end- users, and quality assurance teams to identify areas for improvement in the software quality control process. Continuously refine and adapt the process based on lessons learned and emerging industry trends.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-39\">Compromise in Quality Control<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-198\">Compromise in quality control refers to situations where standards or procedures are relaxed, ignored, or bypassed, leading to a decline in the quality of products or services. This compromise can occur for various reasons, and while it may seem expedient in the short term, it often leads to negative consequences in the long run. Here are some common scenarios where compromise in quality control might occur:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-199\">Cost-cutting measures: In an effort to reduce expenses, companies may cut corners in quality control processes, such as using cheaper materials or reducing inspection frequency. While this may lower immediate costs, it can result in inferior products that fail to meet customer expectations and damage the company's reputation.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-200\">Time constraints: Pressures to meet tight deadlines or production quotas may lead to shortcuts in quality control procedures. Rushing through inspections or skipping certain steps can result in overlooked defects or errors that compromise product quality.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-201\">Resource limitations: Limited resources, such as skilled personnel or advanced equipment, can hinder effective quality control. In such cases, organizations may prioritize other aspects of production over quality control, leading to compromises in product quality.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-202\">Supplier issues: Reliance on external suppliers introduces risks to quality control. If suppliers deliver substandard materials or components, companies may be tempted to overlook quality issues to avoid production delays or additional costs.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-203\">Competitive pressures: In highly competitive markets, companies may feel pressured to release products quickly or undercut competitors on price, leading to compromises in quality control to maintain competitiveness.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-204\">Regulatory compliance: Some organizations may compromise on quality control to meet regulatory requirements while minimizing costs and effort. This can result in compliance failures, fines, and reputational damage if regulators discover substandard practices.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-205\">Lack of oversight or accountability: Inadequate monitoring or accountability mechanisms within an organization can allow compromises in quality control to go unnoticed or unchecked.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-40\">Total Quality Management versus Total Quality Control<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-206\">TQM is all about continuous improvement in processes – while Total Quality Control – or TQC is all about maintaining quality standards throughout the process.<\/p><h2 id=\"bus-block-3-41\">Topic 7: QUALITY ASSURANCE AND STANDARDS<\/h2><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-207\">Quality Assurance (QA) and Standards are closely related concepts but serve different purposes:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-208\">Quality Assurance (QA):<\/li><\/ol><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-209\">QA is a proactive approach implemented within an organization to ensure that processes, procedures, and practices are designed and executed to meet predefined quality criteria.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-210\">It involves systematic planning, implementation, evaluation, and improvement of processes to ensure that products or services consistently meet or exceed customer expectations.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-211\">QA focuses on preventing defects or errors before they occur by establishing robust quality management systems, conducting audits, and implementing corrective and preventive actions.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-212\">The goal of QA is to instil confidence in customers by delivering high-quality products or services that are reliable, safe, and meet specified requirements.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-213\">Standards:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-214\">Standards are established guidelines, specifications, or criteria that define the characteristics, properties, or performance requirements of products, processes, services, or systems.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-215\">They serve as benchmarks or reference points against which quality, safety, and compliance are measured.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-216\">Standards are developed through consensus by relevant stakeholders, including industry organizations, government agencies, and international standards bodies.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-217\">Compliance with standards helps ensure consistency, reliability, interoperability, and regulatory compliance across industries and markets.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-42\">Process Based Quality and Practical Based Quality Approaches in Quality Assurance<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-218\">In quality assurance, two main approaches are often discussed: process-based quality and practical-based quality.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-43\">1. Process-based Quality:<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-219\">This approach focuses on ensuring that the processes used to create a product or service are well-defined, documented, and followed consistently.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-220\">The emphasis is on establishing and maintaining efficient and effective processes that lead to high-quality outcomes.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-221\">Process-based quality assurance involves activities such as defining procedures, conducting audits, and implementing improvements to streamline processes and reduce errors.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-222\">The goal is to prevent defects from occurring by addressing potential issues in the processes themselves.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-44\">2. Practical-based Quality:<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-223\">Practical-based quality focuses on evaluating the actual output or outcome of a product or service to determine its quality.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-224\">This approach emphasizes real-world performance and user satisfaction rather than just adherence to predefined processes.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-225\">Practical-based quality assurance involves techniques such as user testing, customer feedback analysis, and usability studies to assess how well a product or service meets the needs and expectations of its users.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-226\">The goal is to identify and address any deficiencies in the product or service that may impact its usability, functionality, or overall quality. While process-based quality focuses on establishing robust procedures and controls, practical- based quality centers around validating the end result against user requirements and expectations. Ideally, both approaches work in tandem to ensure that high-quality products or services are consistently delivered to customers.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-45\">Importance of Process and Product Standards<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-227\">Process and product standards play crucial roles in ensuring quality, consistency, and reliability in manufacturing and service industries. Here's why they are important:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-228\"><b>Quality Assurance:<\/b> Standards provide a framework for quality assurance by defining the criteria and specifications that products and processes must meet. They help ensure that products and services consistently meet customer expectations and regulatory requirements.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-229\"><b>Consistency:<\/b> Standards establish uniformity in processes and products, promoting consistency across different batches, production lines, and locations. Consistent quality enhances customer satisfaction and builds trust in the brand.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-230\"><b>Risk Reduction:<\/b> Standards help identify and mitigate risks associated with product defects, safety hazards, and non-compliance with regulations. Adhering to standards reduces the likelihood of costly recalls, liability claims, and regulatory penalties.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-231\"><b>Interoperability:<\/b> Standards facilitate interoperability between different systems, components, and products. They enable seamless integration and compatibility, allowing products from different manufacturers to work together effectively.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-232\"><b>Efficiency:<\/b> By defining best practices and optimal processes, standards promote efficiency and productivity. They streamline operations, reduce waste, and minimize errors, leading to cost savings and improved competitiveness.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-233\"><b>Market Access:<\/b> Compliance with recognized standards is often a prerequisite for accessing domestic and international markets. Adhering to standards helps companies demonstrate their commitment to quality and regulatory compliance, enhancing their marketability and competitiveness.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-234\"><b>Innovation:<\/b> Standards provide a foundation for innovation by establishing common benchmarks and requirements. They encourage continuous improvement and the adoption of new technologies, driving innovation within industries.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-235\"><b>Customer Confidence:<\/b> Standards reassure customers about the quality, safety, and reliability of products and services. Compliance with standards signals a commitment to meeting customer needs and expectations, fostering loyalty and repeat business.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-236\"><b>Legal Compliance:<\/b> Standards help companies comply with relevant laws, regulations, and industry-specific requirements. Non-compliance can lead to legal repercussions, fines, and reputational damage.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-237\"><b>Sustainability:<\/b> Standards address environmental and social concerns by promoting sustainable practices, resource efficiency, and responsible sourcing. Compliance with sustainability standards enhances corporate social responsibility and stakeholder trust.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-46\">Problems of standard<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-238\">While standards offer numerous benefits, they also come with certain challenges and limitations. Here are some common problems associated with standards:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-239\"><b>Complexity:<\/b> Standards can be complex and difficult to understand, especially for small businesses or organizations with limited resources. Complex standards may require specialized expertise to interpret and implement effectively.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-240\"><b>Cost:<\/b> Achieving compliance with standards can be costly, particularly for small or resource-constrained businesses. Costs may include training, certification, equipment upgrades, and process modifications.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-241\"><b>Rapid Technological Change:<\/b> Standards may struggle to keep pace with rapid advancements in technology. Outdated standards may become irrelevant or insufficient to address emerging risks or opportunities, leading to gaps in regulation or guidance.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-242\"><b>Inflexibility:<\/b> Standards may be perceived as rigid and inflexible, particularly if they do not accommodate variations in products, processes, or cultural contexts. This lack of flexibility can stifle innovation and hinder adaptation to changing market conditions.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-243\"><b>Overlapping or Conflicting Standards:<\/b> In some industries, multiple standards bodies may develop overlapping or conflicting standards, causing confusion and inefficiency. Harmonizing standards across different organizations and jurisdictions can be challenging.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-244\"><b>Complacency:<\/b> Strict adherence to standards may breed complacency, with organizations focusing solely on meeting minimum requirements rather than striving for excellence or continuous improvement.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-245\"><b>Resource Intensiveness:<\/b> Implementing and maintaining compliance with standards can be resource-intensive, requiring dedicated personnel, time, and financial investments. This burden may disproportionately affect smaller organizations or those operating in resource-constrained environments.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-246\"><b>Cultural Resistance:<\/b> Organizations may encounter resistance to adopting or implementing standards due to cultural or organizational barriers. Resistance may stem from ingrained practices, skepticism about the benefits of standards, or fear of change.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-247\"><b>Global Variability:<\/b> Standards may vary significantly between countries or regions, posing challenges for organizations operating in international markets. Harmonizing global standards and addressing regional differences can be complex and time- consuming.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-248\"><b>Enforcement and Monitoring:<\/b> Ensuring widespread compliance with standards can be difficult, particularly in industries with diverse stakeholders and regulatory environments. Effective enforcement mechanisms and monitoring systems are essential to uphold the integrity of standards.<\/li><\/ol><h2 id=\"bus-block-3-47\">Topic 8: QUALITY ATTRIBUTES, QUALITY MEASURES AND QUALITY REVIEWS<\/h2><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-48\">Quality Attributes<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-249\">Quality attributes, also known as quality characteristics or quality factors, refer to the measurable or perceptible characteristics of a product or service that contribute to its overall quality. These attributes are used to evaluate and assess the performance, functionality, reliability, and usability of a product or service. Here are some common quality attributes:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-250\"><b>Performance:<\/b> Performance refers to the speed, throughput, responsiveness, and efficiency of a product or service. It measures how well the product or service performs its intended functions under specific conditions.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-251\"><b>Reliability:<\/b> Reliability refers to the ability of a product or service to perform consistently and predictably over time without failure or downtime. It measures the likelihood of a product or service to function as expected under normal operating conditions.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-252\"><b>Usability:<\/b> Usability refers to the ease of use, learnability, and user satisfaction of a product or service. It measures how well users can interact with and accomplish tasks using the product or service with minimal effort or confusion.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-253\"><b>Maintainability:<\/b> Maintainability refers to the ease with which a product or service can be maintained, repaired, upgraded, or modified. It measures the ability to quickly and cost-effectively address issues, make changes, or adapt to evolving requirements.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-254\"><b>Scalability:<\/b> Scalability refers to the ability of a product or service to accommodate increasing workloads, users, or data volumes without sacrificing performance or functionality. It measures how well the system can grow or expand to meet changing demands.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-255\"><b>Security:<\/b> Security refers to the protection of a product or service against unauthorized access, data breaches, cyber threats, and other security risks. It measures the effectiveness of security controls, encryption, authentication, and authorization mechanisms.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-256\"><b>Compatibility:<\/b> Compatibility refers to the ability of a product or service to interact and function correctly with other systems, platforms, devices, or software components. It measures interoperability and adherence to industry standards and protocols.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-257\"><b>Robustness:<\/b> Robustness refers to the ability of a product or service to maintain stable and reliable performance even in the presence of errors, invalid inputs, or adverse conditions. It measures resilience and fault tolerance.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-258\"><b>Portability:<\/b> Portability refers to the ease with which a product or service can be transferred, deployed, or adapted to different environments, platforms, or hardware configurations. It measures flexibility and adaptability.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-259\"><b>Compliance:<\/b> Compliance refers to adherence to legal, regulatory, industry, or organizational standards, requirements, and specifications. It measures conformity and accountability.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-49\">Quality Measures<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-260\">Quality measures are quantitative indicators used to assess and monitor the performance, effectiveness, and adherence to quality standards within an organization or across various processes, products, or services. These measures provide tangible data that can be analyzed to identify areas for improvement, track progress over time, and make data-driven decisions to enhance quality. Here are some common quality measures used in different industries:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-261\"><b>Defect Rate:<\/b> Defect rate measures the number of defects or errors identified in a product or service relative to the total units produced or delivered. It helps identify areas where quality issues are occurring and guides efforts to reduce defects and improve overall quality.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-262\"><b>Customer Satisfaction:<\/b> Customer satisfaction measures the level of satisfaction or dissatisfaction of customers with a product, service, or experience. It can be assessed through surveys, feedback mechanisms, and customer reviews to identify areas for improvement and ensure that customer expectations are met or exceeded.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-263\"><b>First-Time Yield (FTY):<\/b> First-Time Yield measures the percentage of products or services that meet quality standards without requiring rework, repair, or additional processing. It reflects the efficiency of production or service delivery processes and identifies opportunities to reduce waste and improve productivity.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-264\"><b>On-Time Delivery:<\/b> On-time delivery measures the percentage of products or services delivered to customers within the agreed-upon timeframe or schedule. It assesses the reliability and responsiveness of delivery processes and helps maintain customer satisfaction and loyalty.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-265\"><b>Process Cycle Time:<\/b> Process cycle time measures the time taken to complete a specific process or task from start to finish. It identifies bottlenecks, inefficiencies, and opportunities for streamlining processes to improve productivity and reduce lead times.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-266\"><b>Cost of Quality (COQ):<\/b> Cost of Quality measures the total costs incurred to ensure product or service quality, including prevention costs (e.g., training, quality planning), appraisal costs (e.g., inspection, testing), and failure costs (e.g., rework, warranty claims). It helps organizations understand the financial impact of quality-related activities and make cost-effective decisions to improve quality.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-267\"><b>Employee Satisfaction and Engagement:<\/b> Employee satisfaction and engagement measures the level of satisfaction, morale, and commitment of employees within an organization. It correlates with quality performance, productivity, and customer satisfaction and helps identify areas for improvement in organizational culture and leadership.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-268\"><b>Compliance and Regulatory Adherence:<\/b> Compliance measures the extent to which an organization adheres to legal, regulatory, industry, or organizational standards and requirements. It ensures accountability, mitigates risks, and avoids potential fines, penalties, or reputational damage.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-269\"><b>Supplier Performance:<\/b> Supplier performance measures the quality, reliability, and consistency of products or services provided by suppliers. It assesses supplier capabilities, delivery performance, and adherence to quality standards to ensure a reliable supply chain and minimize disruptions.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-270\"><b>Overall Equipment Effectiveness (OEE):<\/b> OEE measures the efficiency and effectiveness of manufacturing equipment or assets by considering factors such as availability, performance, and quality. It helps optimize asset utilization, reduce downtime, and improve overall equipment performance.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-50\">Quality Reviews<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-271\">Quality reviews, also known as quality audits or quality inspections, are systematic evaluations conducted within an organization to assess and ensure compliance with quality standards, procedures, and requirements. These reviews aim to identify areas for improvement, address non-conformances, and enhance overall quality across processes, products, or services. Here's an overview of quality reviews:<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-51\">1. Purpose:<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-272\">The primary purpose of quality reviews is to evaluate the effectiveness and efficiency of processes, procedures, and practices in meeting predefined quality objectives and standards.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-273\">Quality reviews help verify that products or services meet customer requirements, regulatory compliance, and industry best practices.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-274\"><b>Scope:<\/b><\/li><\/ol><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-275\">Quality reviews can cover various aspects of an organization's operations, including production processes, service delivery, project management, documentation, and quality management systems.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-276\">They may focus on specific areas or departments within the organization or encompass a broader assessment of the entire quality management framework.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-52\">3. Participants:<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-277\">Quality reviews typically involve participation from cross-functional teams, quality assurance professionals, subject matter experts, and relevant stakeholders.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-278\">Participants may include individuals responsible for executing processes, quality managers, auditors, and representatives from customer or regulatory agencies.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-53\">4. Process:<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-279\">The process of conducting a quality review typically involves planning, preparation, execution, and follow-up activities.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-280\">Planning includes defining review objectives, selecting review criteria, identifying review participants, and scheduling review activities.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-281\">During the review, auditors or review teams collect data, evidence, and observations through interviews, documentation review, observations, and testing.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-282\">After the review, findings are analyzed, documented, and communicated to relevant stakeholders. Corrective and preventive actions may be proposed and implemented to address identified issues and improve quality.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-54\">5. Types of Reviews:<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-283\">There are various types of quality reviews, including internal audits, supplier audits, process reviews, product inspections, and management reviews.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-284\">Internal audits assess the organization's adherence to its own quality management system and procedures.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-285\">Supplier audits evaluate the quality performance of external suppliers and vendors.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-286\">Process reviews focus on specific processes or workflows to identify opportunities for improvement.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-287\">Product inspections involve evaluating the quality of finished products or services against predefined criteria.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-55\">6. Benefits:<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-288\">Quality reviews provide valuable insights into the strengths, weaknesses, and opportunities for improvement within an organization.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-289\">They help ensure compliance with quality standards, regulations, and customer requirements.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-290\">Quality reviews facilitate continuous improvement initiatives and contribute to enhancing overall organizational performance and customer satisfaction.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-3-56\">Continuous Quality Improvement<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-3-p9-291\">Continuous Quality Improvement (CQI) is an ongoing process within an organization focused on enhancing products, services, processes, and systems to consistently meet or exceed customer expectations and organizational objectives. CQI emphasizes the principles of continuous learning, adaptation, and incremental improvement to drive innovation, efficiency, and effectiveness. Here's an overview of Continuous Quality Improvement:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-292\">Key Principles:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-293\">Customer Focus: CQI begins with understanding customer needs, expectations, and feedback. Customer satisfaction is a primary driver for improvement efforts.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-294\">Data-Driven Decision Making: CQI relies on data and evidence to identify areas for improvement, measure performance, and monitor progress towards quality goals.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-295\">Employee Involvement: Employees at all levels are actively involved in CQI initiatives, contributing ideas, expertise, and feedback to drive improvement.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-296\">Process Orientation: CQI focuses on optimizing processes and workflows to eliminate waste, reduce variation, and enhance efficiency and effectiveness.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-297\">Continuous Learning: CQI encourages a culture of continuous learning, experimentation, and adaptation to foster innovation and resilience.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-298\">Key Components:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-299\">Plan-Do-Study-Act (PDSA) Cycle: The PDSA cycle is a fundamental framework for CQI. It involves planning a change, implementing it on a small scale, studying the results, and acting on what was learned to make further improvements.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-300\">Root Cause Analysis (RCA): RCA is a systematic process for identifying underlying causes of problems or issues to prevent recurrence. It helps organizations address the root causes of quality deficiencies.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-301\">Quality Metrics and Measurement: CQI relies on establishing key quality metrics and performance indicators to monitor progress, track trends, and evaluate the effectiveness of improvement initiatives.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-302\">Benchmarking: Benchmarking involves comparing organizational performance and practices against industry leaders or best-in-class organizations to identify opportunities for improvement and set targets for excellence.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-303\">Implementation Process:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-304\">Identify Opportunities for Improvement: Organizations identify areas for improvement based on customer feedback, data analysis, performance metrics, and stakeholder input.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-305\">Plan Improvement Initiatives: Improvement initiatives are planned, prioritized, and scoped to define objectives, timelines, resources, and success criteria.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-306\">Implement Changes: Changes are implemented gradually, often through pilot projects or small-scale experiments to test effectiveness and minimize risks.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-307\">Monitor and Evaluate Results: Performance metrics are monitored to assess the impact of changes and determine whether objectives are being met.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-308\">Adjust and Iterate: Based on evaluation results, adjustments are made to improvement strategies, and the cycle of planning, implementation, evaluation, and adjustment continues iteratively.<\/li><\/ul><ol><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-309\">Benefits:<\/li><\/ol><ul><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-310\">CQI helps organizations achieve higher levels of customer satisfaction, loyalty, and retention by consistently delivering high-quality products and services.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-311\">It drives operational efficiency, cost savings, and waste reduction through process optimization and innovation.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-312\">CQI fosters a culture of employee engagement, empowerment, and accountability, leading to higher morale, productivity, and retention.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-3-p9-313\">It enhances organizational agility and adaptability by promoting continuous learning, innovation, and responsiveness to changing market conditions.<\/li><\/ul>", "toc": [{"id": "bus-block-3-9", "t": "Topic 1: DEFINITION, PRINCIPLES AND TECHNIQUES OF TOTAL QUALITY MANAGEMENT (TQM)", "lv": 2}, {"id": "bus-block-3-10", "t": "Definition", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-11", "t": "Principles of TQM", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-12", "t": "TQM Techniques/Tools", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-13", "t": "Topic 2: SCOPE AND PURPOSE OF QUALITY MANAGEMENT", "lv": 2}, {"id": "bus-block-3-14", "t": "Process and Product Quality", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-15", "t": "Ways to Improve Process and Product Quality", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-16", "t": "1. Define quality goals", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-17", "t": "2. Analyze Current Performance", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-18", "t": "3. Implement Improvement Actions", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-19", "t": "4. Evaluate Results and Feedback", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-20", "t": "5. Sustain and Standardize Improvements", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-21", "t": "6. Learn and Innovate for Excellence", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-22", "t": "Quality Assurance and Standards", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-23", "t": "Standards in TQM", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-24", "t": "Topic 3: Developing a Strategic Plan for Total Quality Management", "lv": 2}, {"id": "bus-block-3-25", "t": "Benefits of Total Quality Management", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-26", "t": "Topic 4: TQM BEST PRACTICES", "lv": 2}, {"id": "bus-block-3-27", "t": "Problems Associated with TQM", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-28", "t": "Quality Management Process", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-29", "t": "Concepts of Customer Value in TQM", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-30", "t": "TOPIC 5: EMERGING TENDENCIES TOWARD GLOBAL COMPETITIVENESS IN TQM", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-31", "t": "Six-Sigma Management in TQM", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-32", "t": "Some of the key tools used in Six Sigma include:", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-33", "t": "The Lean Principle in TQM", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-34", "t": "Benchmarking in TQM", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-35", "t": "Importance of “Benchmarking” in TQM", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-36", "t": "Topic 6: QUALITY CONTROL Quality Control Planning", "lv": 2}, {"id": "bus-block-3-37", "t": "Software Quality Control", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-38", "t": "Implementing Software Quality Control", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-39", "t": "Compromise in Quality Control", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-40", "t": "Total Quality Management versus Total Quality Control", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-41", "t": "Topic 7: QUALITY ASSURANCE AND STANDARDS", "lv": 2}, {"id": "bus-block-3-42", "t": "Process Based Quality and Practical Based Quality Approaches in Quality Assurance", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-43", "t": "1. Process-based Quality:", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-44", "t": "2. Practical-based Quality:", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-45", "t": "Importance of Process and Product Standards", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-46", "t": "Problems of standard", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-47", "t": "Topic 8: QUALITY ATTRIBUTES, QUALITY MEASURES AND QUALITY REVIEWS", "lv": 2}, {"id": "bus-block-3-48", "t": "Quality Attributes", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-49", "t": "Quality Measures", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-50", "t": "Quality Reviews", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-51", "t": "1. Purpose:", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-52", "t": "3. Participants:", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-53", "t": "4. Process:", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-54", "t": "5. Types of Reviews:", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-55", "t": "6. Benefits:", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-3-56", "t": "Continuous Quality Improvement", "lv": 3}]}]}, "bus-block-4": {"course": "BUS", "ref": "Block 4", "title": "Leadership, Managerial Skills and Organisations", "kicker": "Block 4 · Dr Abraham Osa Ehiorobo", "words": 3909, "parts": [{"label": "Managerial Roles, Skills and Types of Organizations", "note": "ABRAHAM OSA EHIOROBO, PH.D.", "src": "4.2 MANAGERIAL ROLES, SKILLS AND TYPES OF ORGANIZATIONS.pptx", "html": "<h3 id=\"bus-block-4-0\">MANAGERIAL ROLES BY HENRY MINTZBERG<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-1\"><b>Managerial Roles<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-2\">Henry Mintzberg, an internationally renowned author in management books, in his<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-3\">research, identified and summarized these expected roles of managers as follows:<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-4\">Interpersonal Role<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-5\">Informational Role and<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-6\">Decision Making Role<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-7\"><b>(1) Interpersonal Role:<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-8\">A manager has a relationship with his subordinates, his organisation and people outside<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-9\">the organisation some of which are ceremonial and symbolic in nature, so he is the<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-10\">following:<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-11\">a figurehead,<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-12\">a leader and<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-13\">a liaison officer<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-14\">When a manager performs social obligations, such as receiving visitors and speaking at functions,<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-15\">presenting his company’s products and services at functions, attending weddings of his<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-16\">subordinates, or signing legal documents on behalf of his company, he is described <b>as a<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-17\"><b>figurehead<\/b>.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-18\">When he motivates his subordinates to go the extra mile and shows by example how dedicated he<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-19\">is to his job so that they can follow his steps, he is described <b>as a leader<\/b>.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-20\">By maintaining a good network with the outside world, to know, and act promptly to changes in<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-21\">policies, rules, and regulations from the government. All these he does by attending seminars and conferences he is described as <b>a liaison officer<\/b>.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-22\"><b>(2) Informational Role<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-23\">A manager collects, receives, and disseminates information within the organisation, so he is the following:<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-24\">a monitor,<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-25\">a disseminator, and<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-26\">a spokesman.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-27\">He is <b>a monitor<\/b> when he constantly checks what is happening internally among his subordinates and top management. You might ask why? Because he collects and studies information from outside that could affect his company, he does all these by reading newspapers, reports, periodicals or by personal contacts.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-28\">He is <b>a disseminator<\/b> because he passes information about changes in policies and other issues to subordinates and other members of the organisation, You might also ask how he does this. He does this through telephone calls, memos, individual or group meetings, and messages. He is <b>a spokesman<\/b> when he represents his unit in either sending information or making the demand on their behalf to management through meetings or phone calls.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-29\"><b>(3) Decision Making Role<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-30\">A manager is a decision-maker because he had to decide from many alternative choices<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-31\">facing the organisation, so he is the following:<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-32\">as an entrepreneur,<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-33\">disturbance handler,<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-34\">a resource allocator and<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-35\">a negotiator<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-36\">as <b>an entrepreneur<\/b> -He must always search for new ideas to either improve or develop new products using suggestion boxes or meetings with research and development personnel<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-37\"><b>Disturbance handler<\/b> - He is always involved in solving differences among the employees and settling conflicts between employees and top management such as making demands on behalf of his subordinates for higher pay and an increase in benefits<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-38\"><b>He is a resource allocator<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-39\">He prepares budgets for various requirements of his unit and makes sure that available funds are utilized properly.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-40\"><b>A negotiator<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-41\">He plays this role by negotiating contracts with unions, negotiating prices with customers and vendors or suppliers to get better deals for his company<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-42\"><b>He is a resource allocator<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-43\">He prepares budgets for various requirements of his unit and makes sure that available funds are utilized properly.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-44\"><b>A negotiator<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-45\">He plays this role by negotiating contracts with unions, negotiating prices with customers and vendors or suppliers to get better deals for his company.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-4-1\">MANAGERIAL LEVELS<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-46\"><b>Management Levels<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-47\">For you to perform the management functions effectively you will require an understanding of the various management levels as well as roles expected at the various levels of management and their relative importance in your organization. However, irrespective of your managerial levels you will also need to possess variety of skills that may come from your abilities, knowledge, practices and aptitude Organizations, particularly large organizations have many levels with different managers.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-48\">Basically, three levels of Management had been clearly identified, and they are:<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-49\">Low level management<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-50\">Middle level management<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-51\">Top level management.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-52\"><b>Low level management –<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-53\">This consists of frontline managers, otherwise known as operational managers who supervise the organization's operations. They are directly involved with non-management employees, implementing specific plans. They are the link between management and nonmanagement personnel, for example, supervisors, and sales Officers and so on<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-54\"><b>Middle level management –<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-55\">This consists of Managers located in the middle layers of the organizational hierarchy below the top-level management and above the low-level management. They are otherwise known as tactical managers that are responsible for translating the general goals and plans of organizations in to specific objectives and activities. They are the linchpins of internal communication, interpreting and disseminating top management priorities downward and equally channeling and translating information from the low-level management upward. They provide creative ideas, operating skills and practical problem-solving skills that keep the organization working.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-56\"><b>Top level management<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-57\">This consists of senior executives of an organization who are responsible for its overall management. They are otherwise known as strategic managers. They provide leadership for the organization and focus on long-term issues, policy and strategy formulation, emphasize organization survival, growth, and organization’s overall effectiveness. They are concerned with the organization as well as its external environment, example of managers at this level are the chief executive officers (CEO) chief operating officers (COO), executive directors and members of the management team.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-4-2\">MANAGERIAL SKILLS<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-58\">Managers at all levels of an organization need to plan, organize, lead and control. However, there are differences among managers based on the amount of time they devote to each of the activities. It may also be due to the kind of organization or type of job to be done.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-59\">The differences could also be dependent on the level of the manager in the organization’s hierarchy.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-60\">Managers need a variety of skills to carry-out their activities. Skills are specific abilities that result from knowledge, information, practice, and aptitude.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-61\">Robert. L. Katz (1974) identified three basic types of skills needed by all managers: Technical, Human, and Conceptual skills.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-62\"><b>Technical Skills:<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-63\">The ability to use tools, procedures, and techniques of a specialized field. The manager needs enough technical skills to accomplish the mechanics of a particular job. It is the ability to perform a specialized task involving particular method or process.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-64\"><b>Human Relation Skills:<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-65\">The ability to work with, understand, and motivate other people either as individuals or groups. It is the people skills that enable managers to communicate effectively with others because they spend most of their time interacting with people. Managers need more of human relations skills to work with other members of the organization or to head a group.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-66\"><b>Conceptual Skills:<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-67\">These are skills that relate to the ability to identify and resolve problems for the benefit of the organization and its members. It is the mental ability to coordinate and integrate all of the organization’s interests/ activities i.e. the ability to have an overview of the entire organization, how it works, and relationship between the parts, the various factors in a given situation and their interrelationship etc. This will enable the manager to take the best actions in the overall interest of the organization.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-4-3\">MANAGEMENT LEVELS, SKILLS AND THEIR RELATIONSHIPS<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-68\">The importance of skills varies by managerial levels and roles in any organization.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-69\">All the above-mentioned skills are essential to effective management and their relative importance to a specific manager depends on his/her rank in the organization. For instance, technical skill is most important at lower levels of management but becomes less important as we move up the chain of command. An example could be a production supervisor whose technical skill is more important than that of a managing director/chief executive officer.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-70\">Conceptual skill increases as managers move up the rank. The higher a manager moves in the hierarchy, the more he/she will be involved in the broad, long–term decisions that affect the organization. For top management, conceptual skill is very important. Moreover, human skill is very important at every level of the organization, why? Because management is simply put ‘getting things done through other people’, managers interact more with people.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-4-4\">FORMS OF ORGANIZATIONS<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-71\"><b>Organisation<\/b> is the arrangement as to who should do what management and administration have decided upon.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-72\"><b>Types/Forms of Organisation<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-73\">There are three forms of organisation, namely:<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-74\">line organisation,<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-75\">functional organization,<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-76\">line and staff organisation.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-77\"><b>Line Organisation<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-78\">This is also known as scalar organisation or military type of organisation. It is characterised<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-79\">by direct lines of authority flowing from the top to the bottom of the organisational<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-80\">hierarchy and lines of responsibility flowing in an opposite but equally direct manner. It is<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-81\">characterised by superior subordinate relationship. A superior delegates authority to<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-82\">another subordinate and so on, forming a line from the very top to the bottom of the<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-83\">organisational structure. The line of authority so established is referred to as line authority.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-84\">Suitability of line organization structure is possible where:<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-85\">Scale of operations is limited or for a small-scale business.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-86\">Work is simple and routine in nature.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-87\">Continuous production firms e.g., spinning, weaving etc.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-88\">Flexible labour management problems.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-89\"><b>Advantages<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-90\">Simple to establish – understood easily by employees.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-91\">Fixed responsibility – clearly defined duties and responsibility for each individual, thus eliminating confusion.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-92\">Discipline as a result of the unity of command.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-93\">Coordination – control of activities by one person.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-94\">Quick decision – a decision is taken by one person – the head.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-95\"><b>Disadvantages<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-96\">Overload – too many responsibilities for the executive<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-97\">Lack of Specialisation – on account of many functions and complexities.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-98\">The executive may not be an expert in all aspects of managerial activities.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-99\">Lack of initiative – ultimate authority lies in the hands of top management.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-100\">This affects the initiatives of departmental heads and enthusiasm to motivate the subordinates.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-101\">Lack of communication from lower ranks since authority flows from top to down<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-102\"><b>Functional Organisation –<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-103\">This means that the organisation should be based on various functions. This is based on the principle of specialisation and tries to bring about organisational balance.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-104\">Each worker, instead of encountering one superior, would receive his orders from a group of specialised supervisors each of whom performs a particular function.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-105\">Functional organisation also operates at higher levels of management. The whole work in the organisation is divided into various departments – Similar types of work and transactions are put in one department under the control of a departmental manager. The various departments include purchases, sales, finance, production, personnel etc.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-106\">The head of each department will be responsible for carrying out various activities of their departments in the organisation. At the top level of management is that a subordinate anywhere in the organisation will be controlled and commanded directly by numbers of managers operating in different departments.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-107\"><b>Merits<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-108\">Specialisation – As every head is an expert in his area.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-109\">Increased efficiency – As workers operate under the expert and competent personnel and perform limited operations.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-110\">Flexibility – Function as a whole can be cut by eliminating positions at the lower levels without seriously affecting its total performances.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-111\"><b>Demerits<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-112\">Conflict of authority – A violation of unity of command more than one boss over subordinates<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-113\">Expensive – Multiplicity of experts makes it expensive.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-114\">Problems of coordination – There is delay in decision making as it requires involvement of more than one specialist.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-115\"><b>The line and staff organization<\/b>.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-116\">It is an improvement on the above-mentioned two systems viz.; line organization and functional organization. The line organization concentrates too much on control, whereas the functional system decentralizes it to a great extent. The line- staff balances the two.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-117\">In line-staff organization, line officers get the advice of the staff which is very helpful in carrying out the task in an efficient manner. It is advisory in nature where certain functions requiring skill and objectivity are required. The staff has the power to recommend and not to enforce them. Thus, the staff members are thinkers while the line officers are ‘doers. The staff members prepare plans while the line officers execute them.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-118\"><b>Merits<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-119\">Specialisation – It brings about utilisation of expert knowledge for the benefit of management.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-120\">Better Decisions – Staff specialists; help the line managers in taking better decisions by providing them with adequate information of the right type at the right time.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-121\">Lesser burden on line officers – The work of the line officers is considerably reduced with the help of staff officers.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-122\">Advancement of Research – Staff officers constantly undertake research and experimentation for improvement.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-123\"><b>Demerits<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-124\">Conflict between line and staff officers – where the advice of staff officers is not taken to the letter, and where line officers feel that correct advice is not given.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-125\">Problem of line and Staff Authority – who is superior in terms of relationship?<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-126\">Lack of Responsibility – Staff specialists may not be held accountable for the outcome of wrong advice.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-127\">Expensive System – as experts are appointed.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-4-5\">INFORMAL ORGANIZATIONS<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-128\">The various methods of structuring identified are formally established by management, hence they are formal organisations.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-129\">However, the social desires of persons find their expression in the informal structure.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-130\">Informal structuring can be defined as the grouping of people emerging unofficially and without being designated as parts of the organization. They are often found as cliques or sub-group within the formal organization.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-131\">The existence of an informal organization is to satisfy human needs in a way that formal organizations do not.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-132\">The functions of the informal organization include:<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-133\">i. <i><b>Communication<\/b><\/i> – The need for information, both need for news and the need for prompt news. Enterprise in practice often circulates news slowly through the formal line, transmits it poorly, or even withholds certain information. The informal organization provides the channel or grape-vine for speedy communication with their members.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-134\">ii. <i><b>To perpetuate the culture of a group<\/b><\/i> – The establishment of norms or standard ways of doing things may differ from the formal way. For instance, a group may be unified in its desire to maintain a certain standard of education, discipline, or work outcome. The authority as an example may review tea time to 15 minutes but informal groups may agree for 20 minutes. If a given department employs engineers exclusively, an informal group may oppose the use of technicians for some of the jobs.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-135\">iii. <i><b>To acquire status<\/b><\/i> – The formal structuring may underrate an employee but through the informal group, he is recognized as a leader in a particular social event.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-136\">Since informal organisation can either aid or interfere with enterprise interest, the<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-137\">Management cannot afford to disregard informal organisation because:<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-138\">It brings cohesiveness to formal organisation members. A feeling of status and self-respect and satisfaction is attained.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-139\">It fills the gap in the formal structure – those things that the official structure cannot accomplish are met.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-140\">It is a breeding ground for future leaders.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-141\">It is a useful channel of employee communication.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-142\">Force managers to be better managers.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-143\">Improves productivity of workers since they are more satisfied.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-144\">As a result of all these the activities of informal organisation should be directed into<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-145\">constructive channels, rather than discouraging or killing it.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-146\">The lifespan of an informal organisation can be as short as minutes and can be last for years. A leader just emerged without any formal election and the leadership is not permanent depending on the acceptability of the leadership.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-147\">Once leaders of informal groups are identified and their cooperation gained. organising and<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-148\">directing become easier. Managers use the informal organisation as channels of<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-149\">communication, and molders of employee morale.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-150\"><b>Limitations of Informal Organisation<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-151\">The following limitations are identified with the informal organisation:<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-152\">It may lead to a violation of official rules.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-153\">Lack of uniformity in the application of official relationships.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-154\">It may lead to multiple leadership.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p0-155\">Information could be negatively filtered.<\/li><\/ul>", "toc": [{"id": "bus-block-4-0", "t": "MANAGERIAL ROLES BY HENRY MINTZBERG", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-4-1", "t": "MANAGERIAL LEVELS", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-4-2", "t": "MANAGERIAL SKILLS", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-4-3", "t": "MANAGEMENT LEVELS, SKILLS AND THEIR RELATIONSHIPS", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-4-4", "t": "FORMS OF ORGANIZATIONS", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-4-5", "t": "INFORMAL ORGANIZATIONS", "lv": 3}]}, {"label": "Leadership, Trust and Managing Communication", "note": "ABRAHAM OSA EHIOROBO, PH.D. 09098760201.", "src": "4.1 LEADERSHIP, TRUST AND MANAGING COMMUNICATION.pptx", "html": "<h3 id=\"bus-block-4-6\">LEADERSHIP<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-1\">Leadership in management is the ability to influence, inspire, and guide individuals or teams toward achieving organizational goals. It involves setting a strategic vision, motivating employees, making critical decisions, and fostering a collaborative culture. Unlike management, which focuses on planning and control, leadership centers on empowering people to achieve, adapt, and succeed.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-2\"><b>Leadership vs. Management<\/b>While often used interchangeably, these terms have distinct nuances within an organization:<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-3\">Management is generally associated with planning, organizing, budgeting and controlling processes.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-4\">Leadership is more about setting direction, motivating and driving change.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-5\">Effective managers must combine both leadership and managerial skills to succeed.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-4-7\">Here are five essential leadership qualities:<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-6\"><b>Integrity &amp; Accountability:<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-7\">Being honest, ethical, and taking responsibility for actions builds trust and respect, setting a strong moral compass for the team.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-8\"><b>Effective Communication:<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-9\">Leaders must clearly articulate vision, provide feedback, listen actively, and create an open environment where team members feel comfortable sharing ideas.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-10\"><b>Vision &amp; Purpose:<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-11\">A good leader inspires by having a clear sense of direction, a compelling future vision, and the ability to motivate others towards achieving it.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-12\"><b>Empathy &amp; Emotional Intelligence:<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-13\">Understanding and connecting with team members' emotions, showing compassion, and managing their own emotions helps build strong relationships and a supportive culture.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-14\"><b>Adaptability &amp; Resilience:<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-15\">In dynamic environments, leaders need to be flexible, embrace change, learn from setbacks, and remain positive and encouraging through challenges.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-4-8\">Here are seven functions a leader needs to perform<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-16\">Setting goals. ...<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-17\">Organising. ...<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-18\">Take initiatives. ...<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-19\">Cooperation among employees. ...<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-20\">Motivation and direction. ...<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-21\">Liaison between workers and management. ...<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-22\">Policy making.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-4-9\">LEADERSHIP AND TRUST<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-4-p6-23\">Leadership and trust in management are foundational to organizational success, driving employee engagement, collaboration, and performance. Trust is built through integrity, competence, consistency, and empathy, where leaders act ethically, demonstrate capability, deliver on promises, and show genuine care for employees. A high-trust environment encourages open communication and better results.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-4-10\">Key Pillars of Trust in Leadership<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-24\"><b>Integrity:<\/b> Acting with honesty, fairness, and ethical behavior.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-25\"><b>Competence:<\/b> Demonstrating the knowledge, skills, and ability to lead effectively.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-26\"><b>Consistency:<\/b> Ensuring actions align with words and maintaining reliability.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-27\"><b>Connection/Empathy:<\/b> Showing care for employees and building strong relationships.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-28\"><b>Openness:<\/b> Communicating transparently and encouraging two-way dialogue.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-4-11\">Building and Maintaining Trust<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-29\"><b>Be Transparent:<\/b> Share the reasoning behind decisions and admit when information is lacking.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-30\"><b>Empowerment:<\/b> Delegate, trust team members, and support them when errors occur.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-31\"><b>Visibility:<\/b> Engage in face-to-face meetings to foster connection.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-32\"><b>Action-Oriented:<\/b> Consistently follow through on commitments.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-33\"><b>Impact of Trust<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-34\"><b>Increased Performance:<\/b> High-trust environments foster better results, speed, and innovation.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-35\"><b>Loyalty and Retention:<\/b> Employees are more likely to stay with leaders who are honest and supportive.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-36\"><b>Reduced Risk:<\/b> Trust allows teams to take risks, support each other, and adapt to change.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-4-12\">MANAGING COMMUNICATION<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-37\">Communication management is the structured planning, execution, monitoring, and control of all communication activities within an organization, team, or project. It ensures that the right messages reach the right audience through the right channels at the right time, keeping everyone aligned and informed.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-38\">The 7Cs of communication represent clarity, conciseness, correctness, completeness, courtesy, concreteness, and consideration. Together, these principles of effective communication form a foundation for expressing ideas efficiently and professionally<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-4-13\">MANAGING COMMUNICATION, KNOWLEDGE AND INFORMATION<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-4-p6-39\">Managing communication, knowledge, and information involves systematically capturing, storing, sharing, and utilizing data to improve decision-making, foster collaboration, and enhance organizational efficiency. It combines structured knowledge management (databases, repositories) with strategic communication (tailored messages, active feedback) to ensure the right information reaches the right audience at the right time.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-4-14\">Key aspects of communication and information management<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-40\"><b>Information Flow:<\/b> Using, creating, distributing, and manipulating information via ICT tools such as phones, internet, and software<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-41\"><b>Knowledge Management (KM):<\/b> Identifying and organizing internal expertise, such as lessons learned and data repositories to ensure it is accessible.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-42\"><b>Communication Strategy:<\/b> Adapting styles, using visual aids (charts, infographics), and utilizing storytelling to make complex data engaging.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-43\"><b>Tools &amp; Techniques:<\/b> Employing knowledge management systems (KMS) for data retrieval, project management information systems, and active listening for feedback<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-44\"><b>Sources of Information:<\/b> Utilizing primary sources like customer feedback and secondary sources like industry news and social media.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-4-15\">BENEFITS OF COMMUNICATION AND INFORMATION MANAGEMENT<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-45\"><b>Improved Decision-Making:<\/b> Facilitates better, faster decisions by providing relevant information to stakeholders.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-46\"><b>Enhanced Performance:<\/b> Promotes learning and accountability within organizations.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p6-47\"><b>Efficiency:<\/b> Reduces downtime and boosts productivity through better documentation and knowledge sharing.<\/li><\/ul>", "toc": [{"id": "bus-block-4-6", "t": "LEADERSHIP", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-4-7", "t": "Here are five essential leadership qualities:", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-4-8", "t": "Here are seven functions a leader needs to perform", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-4-9", "t": "LEADERSHIP AND TRUST", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-4-10", "t": "Key Pillars of Trust in Leadership", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-4-11", "t": "Building and Maintaining Trust", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-4-12", "t": "MANAGING COMMUNICATION", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-4-13", "t": "MANAGING COMMUNICATION, KNOWLEDGE AND INFORMATION", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-4-14", "t": "Key aspects of communication and information management", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-4-15", "t": "BENEFITS OF COMMUNICATION AND INFORMATION MANAGEMENT", "lv": 3}]}, {"label": "Managing Communication and Information", "note": "ABRAHAM OSA EHIOROBO, PH.D.", "src": "4.3 MANAGING COMMUNICATION AND INFORMATION.pptx", "html": "<h3 id=\"bus-block-4-16\">LEADERSHIP AND TRUST<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-1\">Leadership is an influence process that involves motivating and inspiring people to work together to achieve a common goal.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-2\">OBLIGATIONS OF LEADERSHIP<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-3\">Developing and Communicating a clear vision<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-4\">Motivating and inspiring team members<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-5\">Setting clear expectations<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-6\">Making informed decisions<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-7\">Fosters commitment and accountability<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-8\">Fosters a positive work environment<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-9\">Delegates tasks<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-10\">Coaching team members<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-11\">Conflict resolution<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-4-17\">TRUST IN LEADERSHIP<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-12\">Trust in leadership is important because it helps people feel secure and confident in their organization. It can lead to better collaboration, engagement and success.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-13\">HOW TRUST IS BUILT<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-14\">COMPETENCE: You must possess necessary skills and expertise<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-15\">COMMUNICATION: Communicate clearly for mutual understanding<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-16\">COMMITMENT: Being committed to the goals and overall success of the organization<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-17\">CARING: A leader must show that he truly cares about others<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-18\">CHARACTER: The leader must reflect ethical and honest behaviour.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-4-18\">INTRODUCTION TO COMMUNICATION<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-19\">Everybody communicates in one way or the other, but few people deliver their messages as well as they can.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-20\">Good communication is the lifeblood of organizations. It takes many forms, such as speaking, writing, and listening, though, its purpose is always to convey a message to recipients. Thus, it can be used to handle information and improve relationships.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-21\">EFFECTIVE COMMUNICATION: Effective communication hinges on people understanding your meaning, and replying in terms that move the exchange forward.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-22\">Communication is a two-way process between a sender and a receiver.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-4-19\">COMMUNICATION<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-23\">ACHIEVING CLARITY: The three rules that govern good communication include:<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-24\">Be clear in your own mind about what you want to communicate.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-25\">Deliver the message succinctly<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-26\">Ensure that the message has been clearly and correctly understood.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-27\">Good communication means saying what you mean and fully comprehending any feedback.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-4-20\">TYPES OF COMMUNICATION<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-28\">WRITTEN WORDS: Written words are basic to literate societies. The words could be in any language or media. They include letters, memos, reports, notes, proposals, notices, contracts and documents.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-29\">SPOKEN WORDS: This form of communication is only effective only when it is heard by the right people. Examples include conversations, interviews, meetings, phone calls, debates, speeches and announcements.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-30\">SYMBOLIC GESTURES: Any positive or negative behaviour that can be seen or heard by the intended audience. They include gestures, facial expressions, actions, deeds, tone of voice, silence, posture, movement, absence, presence.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-31\">VISUAL IMAGES: Images that can be perceived by a target group. They include photographs, paintings, illustrations, graphics, drawings, slides, prints, cartoons, charts, graphs, videos, logos, films, and color schemes.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-32\">MULTIMEDIA: A combination of the various methods described in visual images. They include television, newspapers, radio, videos, flyers, posters, internet CD-ROMs.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-4-21\">MANAGING INFORMATION<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-33\">Human beings spend most of their time delivering and receiving messages in person. Honesty and feedback are both essential for good information management.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-34\">FINDING INFORMATION: The employees of an organization need to obtain information and the ability of managers to provide all types of information in the right way are crucial elements in any organization.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-35\">Start by finding out which areas people most want to know about. Job security, working conditions, reward, location and other benefits are all important to employees.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-4-22\">GIVING FEEDBACK<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-4-p16-36\">Feedback is essential to communication –to check that you have understood the other person’s message, and to react to what they have said and done.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-4-p16-37\">It can be difficult to give negative feedback but it has to be given when it is the only thing to do<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-4-p16-38\">When giving negative feedback, follow these simple rules to avoid antagonism.<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-39\">Show an understanding of exactly what went wrong, and why.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-40\">Draw out ways in which poor performance or behaviour can improve<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-41\">Use questions rather than assertions to let the person know what you mean.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-42\">Aim to express your negative opinions honestly, but in a positive manner.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-4-p16-43\">Be objective and avoid being emotional or personal.<\/li><\/ol>", "toc": [{"id": "bus-block-4-16", "t": "LEADERSHIP AND TRUST", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-4-17", "t": "TRUST IN LEADERSHIP", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-4-18", "t": "INTRODUCTION TO COMMUNICATION", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-4-19", "t": "COMMUNICATION", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-4-20", "t": "TYPES OF COMMUNICATION", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-4-21", "t": "MANAGING INFORMATION", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-4-22", "t": "GIVING FEEDBACK", "lv": 3}]}]}, "bus-block-5": {"course": "BUS", "ref": "Block 5", "title": "Quality Control and Statistical Process Control", "kicker": "Block 5 · Dr O. P. Olonade", "words": 1525, "parts": [{"label": "Lecture 2: Quality Control", "note": "L2 — June 22, 2026 · Dr. Olonade", "src": "5.1 Lecture 2 - 22nd June (transcript).md", "html": "<h4 id=\"bus-block-5-0\">Page 1<\/h4><p id=\"bus-block-5-p0-1\">ISO 9000 — Fundamentals ISO 9001 — Requirements<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-5-p0-2\"><b>Quality Control<\/b> is the set of procedures organisations use to assess the suitability, effectiveness and conditions of their products and services. It involves techniques, activities and actions designed to guide processes and eliminate potential deviations.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-5-p0-3\">Effective quality control is essential to a business's overall performance. It increases customer satisfaction, operational efficiency and continuous improvements while reducing long-term cost.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-5-p0-4\">The goal of quality control is to prevent, identify and control errors or defects — ideally during production, before the product reaches the end customer or enters another manufacturing stage. To achieve this, companies conduct a series of checks: from design stage, lab testing and simulations to pre-production material inspection. Also, …<\/p><h4 id=\"bus-block-5-1\">Page 2<\/h4><p id=\"bus-block-5-p0-5\">… QC may be defined as a system that is used to maintain a desired level of quality in a product or service. It is a systematic control of various factors that affect the quality of the products. It depends on materials, tools, machines, type of labour, working condition, etc.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-5-2\">Objectives of Quality Control<\/h3><ol><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-6\">To improve the company's income by making the production more acceptable to the customers.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-7\">To reduce company's cost through reduction of losses due to defects.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-8\">To achieve interchangeability and manufacture of large-scale production.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-9\">To produce optimal quality at reduced price.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-10\">To ensure satisfaction of customers with production of services or high quality level, to meet customer goodwill, confidence and reputation of the manufacturer.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-11\">To make inspection prompt to ensure quality control.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-12\">To check the variation during manufacturing.<\/li><\/ol><h4 id=\"bus-block-5-3\">Page 3<\/h4><h3 id=\"bus-block-5-4\">Steps in Quality Control<\/h3><ol><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-13\">Formulate quality policy.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-14\">Set the standards or specification on the basis of customer preference, cost and profit.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-15\">Select inspection plan and set up procedure for checking.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-16\">Detect deviations from set standard of specification.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-17\">Take corrective actions or necessary changes to achieve the standards.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-18\">Decide on salvage method, i.e. to decide how the defective parts are disposed of — entirely scrapped or reworked.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-19\">Coordination of quality problems.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-20\">Developing quality consciousness both within and outside the organisation.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-21\">Developing procedures for good vendor–vendee relations.<\/li><\/ol><h4 id=\"bus-block-5-5\">Page 4<\/h4><h3 id=\"bus-block-5-6\">Benefits of Quality Control<\/h3><ol><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-22\">Improving the quality of products and services.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-23\">Increasing the productivity of manufacturing processes, commercial business and corporations.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-24\">Reducing manufacturing and corporate costs.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-25\">Determining and improving the marketability of products and services.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-26\">Reducing customer prices of products and services.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-27\">Improving and/or assuring on-time deliveries and availability.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-28\">Assisting in the management of the enterprise.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-5-7\">Quality Control Tools<\/h3><ol><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-29\"><b>Check Sheets:<\/b> These are simple forms with certain formats that can aid the user to record data in a firm and systematic way. Data are collected and interpreted on the check sheet to record the frequency of specific events during a data collection period.<\/li><\/ol><h4 id=\"bus-block-5-8\">Page 5<\/h4><ol start=\"2\"><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-30\"><b>Histogram:<\/b> This is very useful to describe a series of the frequency distribution of observed values of a variable. It is a type of bar chart that visualises both attribute and variable data of a product or process; also assists users to show the distribution of data and the amount of variation within a process.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-31\"><b>Pareto Analysis:<\/b> This is a special type of histogram that can easily be applied to find and prioritise quality problems, conditions, or their cause.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-32\"><b>Fishbone:<\/b> It is also known as the cause and effect diagram, developed by Ishikawa in 1943. This is a problem-solving tool that investigates and analyses systematically all the potential or real causes that result in a single effect. On the other hand, it is an effective tool that equips the organisation's management to explore the possible causes of a problem.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-33\"><b>Scatter Diagram:<\/b> This is a powerful tool to draw the distribution of information in 2D, which helps to detect and analyse the pattern relationship between two quality and …<\/li><\/ol><h4 id=\"bus-block-5-9\">Page 6<\/h4><p id=\"bus-block-5-p0-34\">… compliance variables.<\/p><ol start=\"6\"><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-35\"><b>Flowchart:<\/b> Presents a diagrammatic picture that indicates a series of symbols to describe the sequence of steps existing in an operation or processes. On the other hand, a flowchart visualises a picture between the inputs, activities, decision points and output for using and understanding easily concerning the overall objective [of the] true process.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p0-36\"><b>Control Chart:<\/b> Can be utilised for estimating the parameters and the variability in a process. The main aim in a control chart is to prevent the defects in the process.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-5-10\">Illustration / Example<\/h3><table><tr><th>Batch no.<\/th><th>1<\/th><th>2<\/th><th>3<\/th><th>4<\/th><th>5<\/th><th>6<\/th><th>7<\/th><\/tr><tr><td>Sample size<\/td><td>10<\/td><td>8<\/td><td>12<\/td><td>9<\/td><td>15<\/td><td>11<\/td><td>10<\/td><\/tr><\/table><p id=\"bus-block-5-p0-37\">i) Construct an X̄-Chart ii) Is the process of production under control? iii) What is our advice?<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-5-p0-38\">X̄-chart:<\/p><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-5-p0-39\"><span class=\"tex\">\\bar{X} \\pm 3\\sigma<\/span><\/div><h4 id=\"bus-block-5-11\">Page 7<\/h4><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-5-p0-40\"><span class=\"tex\">\\bar{X} = \\frac{10 + 8 + 12 + 9 + 15 + 11 + 10}{7} = \\frac{75}{7} = 10.71<\/span><\/div><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-5-p0-41\"><span class=\"tex\">\\sigma = \\sqrt{\\frac{\\Sigma(x − \\bar{X})^2}{n}}<\/span><\/div><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-5-p0-42\"><span class=\"tex\">\\Sigma(x − \\bar{X})^2 = (10 − 10.71)^2 + (8 − 10.71)^2 + (12 − 10.71)^2 + (9 − 10.71)^2<\/span><\/div><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-5-p0-43\"><span class=\"tex\">\\qquad + (15 − 10.71)^2 + (11 − 10.71)^2 + (10 − 10.71)^2 = 31.43<\/span><\/div><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-5-p0-44\"><span class=\"tex\">\\sigma = \\sqrt{\\frac{31.43}{7}} = 2.12<\/span><\/div><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-5-p0-45\"><span class=\"tex\">\\bar{X} + 3\\sigma = 10.71 + 3(2.12) = 17.07<\/span><\/div><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-5-p0-46\"><span class=\"tex\">\\bar{X} − 3\\sigma = 10.71 − 3(2.12) = 4.35<\/span><\/div><pre id=\"bus-block-5-p0-47\">UCL ├────────────────────── 17.07 (\\\\\\\\ = poor quality)\nCL ├────────────────────── 10.71 (◯ = good quality)\nLCL ├────────────────────── 4.35\n └──────────────────────<\/pre><p id=\"bus-block-5-p0-48\">Legend: <code>\\\\\\\\<\/code> — Poor Quality; <code>◯<\/code> — Good Quality<\/p><h4 id=\"bus-block-5-12\">Page 8<\/h4><p id=\"bus-block-5-p0-49\">R̄-chart, with the mean:<\/p><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-5-p0-50\"><span class=\"tex\">\\bar{X} \\pm A_2\\bar{R}<\/span><\/div><p id=\"bus-block-5-p0-51\">R̄-chart, without the mean:<\/p><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-5-p0-52\"><span class=\"tex\">\\mathrm{UCL} = D_4\\bar{R} \\qquad \\mathrm{LCL} = D_3\\bar{R}<\/span><\/div><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-5-p0-53\"><span class=\"tex\">A_2 = 0.58 \\quad D_3 = 0 \\quad D_4 = 2.11<\/span><\/div><table><tr><th>Sample<\/th><th>X̄<\/th><th>Range<\/th><\/tr><tr><td>1<\/td><td>7.0<\/td><td>2<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>2<\/td><td>7.5<\/td><td>3<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>3<\/td><td>8.0<\/td><td>2<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>4<\/td><td>10<\/td><td>2<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>5<\/td><td>9.5<\/td><td>3<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>6<\/td><td>11<\/td><td>4<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>7<\/td><td>11.5<\/td><td>3<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>8<\/td><td>4<\/td><td>2<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>9<\/td><td>3.5<\/td><td>3<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>10<\/td><td>4<\/td><td>2<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><b>Σ<\/b><\/td><td><b>ΣX̄ = 76<\/b><\/td><td><b>ΣRange = 26<\/b><\/td><\/tr><\/table><p id=\"bus-block-5-p0-54\">i) Construct a range chart with mean ii) Construct a range chart without mean<\/p><h4 id=\"bus-block-5-13\">Page 9<\/h4><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-5-p0-55\"><span class=\"tex\">\\bar{\\bar{X}} = \\frac{76}{10} = 7.6 \\qquad \\bar{R} = \\frac{26}{10} = 2.6<\/span><\/div><p id=\"bus-block-5-p0-56\">i) With the mean:<\/p><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-5-p0-57\"><span class=\"tex\">\\bar{\\bar{X}} \\pm A_2\\bar{R}<\/span><\/div><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-5-p0-58\"><span class=\"tex\">7.6 + 0.58(2.6) = 9.108 \\qquad 7.6 − 0.58(2.6) = 6.092<\/span><\/div><pre id=\"bus-block-5-p0-59\">UCL ├────────────────────── 9.108\nCL ├────────────────────── 7.6\nLCL ├────────────────────── 6.092\n └──────────────────────<\/pre><p id=\"bus-block-5-p0-60\">ii) Without the mean:<\/p><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-5-p0-61\"><span class=\"tex\">\\mathrm{UCL} = D_4\\bar{R} = 2.11(2.6) = 5.486<\/span><\/div><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-5-p0-62\"><span class=\"tex\">\\mathrm{LCL} = D_3\\bar{R} = 0(2.6) = 0<\/span><\/div><pre id=\"bus-block-5-p0-63\">UCL ├────────────────────── 5.486\nLCL ├────────────────────── 0\n └──────────────────────<\/pre>", "toc": [{"id": "bus-block-5-0", "t": "Page 1", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-5-1", "t": "Page 2", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-5-2", "t": "Objectives of Quality Control", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-5-3", "t": "Page 3", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-5-4", "t": "Steps in Quality Control", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-5-5", "t": "Page 4", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-5-6", "t": "Benefits of Quality Control", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-5-7", "t": "Quality Control Tools", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-5-8", "t": "Page 5", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-5-9", "t": "Page 6", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-5-10", "t": "Illustration / Example", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-5-11", "t": "Page 7", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-5-12", "t": "Page 8", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-5-13", "t": "Page 9", "lv": 4}]}, {"label": "Lecture 3: Project Evaluation & Statistical Process Control", "note": "", "src": "5.2 Lecture 3 (transcript).md", "html": "<h4 id=\"bus-block-5-14\">Page 1<\/h4><h3 id=\"bus-block-5-15\">Project Evaluation<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-5-p14-1\"><b>Traditional Method of Project Evaluation<\/b><\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-5-p14-2\">Payback Period<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p14-3\">Accounting Rate of Return<\/li><\/ol><p id=\"bus-block-5-p14-4\"><b>Scientific Method of Project Evaluation<\/b><\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-5-p14-5\">Net Present Value (NPV)<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p14-6\">Internal Rate of Return (IRR)<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-5-p14-7\">Profitability Index (PI)<\/li><\/ol><p id=\"bus-block-5-p14-8\"><b>Example 1 — Payback Period<\/b><\/p><p id=\"bus-block-5-p14-9\">A project needs ₦40,000 as initial investment, and it will generate an annual cash flow of ₦10,000 for 10 years.<\/p><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-5-p14-10\"><span class=\"tex\">\\mathrm{PBP} = \\frac{\\text{initial investment}}{\\text{annual cash flow}} = \\frac{₦40{,}000}{₦10{,}000} = 4\\;\\text{years}<\/span><\/div><p id=\"bus-block-5-p14-11\">Is it feasible? Yes.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-5-p14-12\"><b>Example 2<\/b><\/p><p id=\"bus-block-5-p14-13\">Project A has an initial investment of ₦700,000 and a projected cash flow of ₦225,000 for 5 years.<\/p><h4 id=\"bus-block-5-16\">Page 2<\/h4><h3 id=\"bus-block-5-17\">Statistical Process Control<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-5-p14-14\">Statistical Process Control is defined as the use of statistical techniques to control the process or production method. Statistical process control tools are procedures that help monitor process behaviour, discover issues in internal systems and find solutions for production issues. <b>Statistical Quality Control<\/b> is often used interchangeably. Statistical Quality Control uses a data-driven approach to improve quality by eliminating unusual variations in the process being analysed.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-5-p14-15\">These variations can be caused by many factors including human error, machine error or environmental factors. SQC can be used to monitor manufacturing processes, assembly lines, service operations and even office work.<\/p><h3 id=\"bus-block-5-18\">Types of Variations<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-5-p14-16\">There are two types of variation that can occur in any process:<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-5-p14-17\">a) Common Cause Variation b) Special Cause Variation<\/p><h4 id=\"bus-block-5-19\">Page 3<\/h4><p id=\"bus-block-5-p14-18\"><b>a) Common Cause Variation<\/b> is caused by factors that are inherent to the process being monitored. These factors can include the type of materials being used, the equipment used, the environment or the natural variations that occur when humans are involved in the process.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-5-p14-19\">It is important to note that any process is bound to have a certain level of inherent variability. This is due to the fact that variation is a natural phenomenon. Thus, it is impossible to eliminate all sources of variability. Common cause variation takes this inherent variability into account and aims to keep it within acceptable limits.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-5-p14-20\">For example, if the overall design of a product is causing various quality control issues that are not due to any specific individual or machine.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-5-p14-21\">Another example would be if the workers assembling a product are not working at their full potential due to the design of their workstation.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-5-p14-22\">In both cases, the root cause of the variation is not due to any specific event or individual; rather it is due to the inherent design of the process.<\/p><h4 id=\"bus-block-5-20\">Page 4<\/h4><p id=\"bus-block-5-p14-23\"><b>b) Special Cause Variation:<\/b> This is caused by factors that are not inherent to the process being monitored, or a specific event or issue. These factors can include human error, machine error or outside influences, such as a series of extreme weather events. Special cause variation is usually easier to identify and eliminate.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-5-p14-24\">For example, if the specific machine is not functioning properly and is causing specific defects in a product.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-5-p14-25\">Another example is if a single worker is not following the proper procedures, therefore causing extremely significant difference in large numbers of products.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-5-p14-26\">In both of these cases, the root cause of the variation is due to a specific event or issue that can be identified and addressed.<\/p>", "toc": [{"id": "bus-block-5-14", "t": "Page 1", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-5-15", "t": "Project Evaluation", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-5-16", "t": "Page 2", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-5-17", "t": "Statistical Process Control", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-5-18", "t": "Types of Variations", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-5-19", "t": "Page 3", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-5-20", "t": "Page 4", "lv": 4}]}]}, "bus-block-6": {"course": "BUS", "ref": "Block 6", "title": "Project Management", "kicker": "Block 6 · Miss Mary Isaac", "words": 3915, "parts": [{"label": "Project Management", "note": "Stages, components, evaluation, appraisal and control", "src": "6.1 BUS440(1).pptx", "html": "<h3 id=\"bus-block-6-0\">What Project entails<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-1\">A project is an undertaking that involves sequence of tasks that must be completed at a specific period of time to attain a certain outcome. Examples are construction of road, building an aircraft, developing new medicine for cancer treatment etc.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-2\">Project differs from other organizational routine operations<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-3\">A lot of activities takes place whenever a project is being undertaken like in building project, there are activities like block making, building, plumbing work, roofing, plastering, painting etc.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-4\">These activities are interrelated with some of them being done simultaneously while others are done one after another.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-5\">Whatever the nature of a project is, all projects have certain characteristics<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-6-1\">Attributes/Characteristics of a project<\/h3><ol><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-6\">all projects have a definite beginning and end point, that is start and finish time<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-7\">they must have clearly defined aims, objectives or goals that should be SMART<\/li><\/ol><ul><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-8\">it involves a set of activities that are related<\/li><\/ul><ol start=\"4\"><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-9\">it is unique and different from other routine activities in an organization<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-10\">resources are allocated specifically to project in the form of project budget. The budget caters for payment of workers, equipment used, materials and other resources<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-11\">it involves manpower (a team of people)<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-12\">it involves risk and uncertainty<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-13\">it is time consuming and has specific completion date. Large and complex projects have financial penalties when jobs are completed late.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-14\">it involves non-repetitive tasks<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-6-2\">Classification of a project<\/h3><ol><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-15\">Compliance / emergency project: this project is needed to meet regulatory regulations required to operate in a region. it is called ‘’must-do’’ project. It attract penalties if they are nor implemented. Example is rebuilding a factory destroyed by fire<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-16\">Operational project: this is a project that is needed to support current operations. It is designed to improve efficiency of delivery systems, reduce product costs and improve performance. Example is total quality management project<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-17\">Strategic project: this is a project that directly support the organization’s long-run mission. The project is direct towards increasing revenue or market shares. Example are new products, research and development.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-6-3\">What is management?<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-18\">Management is defined as the process of planning, organizing, directing and controlling of organizational resources (man, material, machine, money, information, idea) in order to effectively and efficiently achieve organizational goals and objectives.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-19\">It is also the act of getting things done through people (Mary Parker Follett)<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-6-4\">Project Management<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-20\">Project management is defined as the process of planning, organizing, directing and controlling of resources to meet technical, cost and time constraint of a project.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-21\">The goal of project management is to ensure that the objectives of project are achieved on time and as budgeted for<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-6-5\">Objective of Project Management<\/h3><ol><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-22\">to determine the cost and duration of the project<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-23\">to determine the level of resources which will be needed<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-24\">to assist in allocating work and monitor progress (for accountability)<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-25\">to help reduce or eliminate the associated risk or uncertainty<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-26\">to effectively and efficiently utilize the available resources<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-6-6\">Project Life Cycle<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-6-p0-27\">The life cycle of a project is in five phases:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-28\">Initiation phase: is a phase that comprises activities associated with starting the project such as kick-off meeting, identifying the project team, developing resources, getting infrastructure. This phase is crucial in determining if the project is viable. The project scope, objectives and deliverables are defined here.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-29\">Planning phase: is a phase for organizing and making preparation on how the project will be executed. A detailed resource guideline is developed at this phase<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-30\">Execution phase: this is the stage where all work is being carried out for successful completion of the project as major activities and tasks are taken up<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-31\">Monitoring and control: this phase is executed to ensure the project is on track and meets its objectives. Where issues arise, corrective actions are taken<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-32\">Close-out phase: this is the phase where the project is closed by archiving documents, turning over project results to clients, closing offices.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-6-7\">Stages of Project Management<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-6-p0-33\">The stages of project management are<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-34\">project planning<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-35\">project scheduling<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-36\">project controlling<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-6-8\">Components of Project Management<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-37\">1. Objective of the project: This is a detailed statement of what is to be accomplished in the project, how it may achieve the company’s goal and meet its strategic plans and also provide estimate of <b>time<\/b> that it should be completed, the <b>cost<\/b> as well as the expected <b>returns<\/b>. An objective is achieved when there is effective planning, organizing and controlling of various activities<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-38\">Project Scope: This specifies all work that must be done to satisfy the customer. It defines the boundary of a project, outlines goals, deliverables, tasks, cost and deadlines. It can also include <b>justification<\/b> for the project and what constitutes project success<\/li><\/ul><ol start=\"3\"><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-39\">Contract requirement: it is a general structure of managerial, reporting and performance responsibility which includes detailed list of staff, suppliers, sub-contractors, managerial requirement and agreement, reporting requirement and a projected organization structure<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-40\">Schedules: it is a list of all <b>major event<\/b> and <b>activities<\/b> that must be performed from start to finish from which a master schedule is developed<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-41\">Resources: this is the overall project budget for all resource requirement and the procedure for budget rate control<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-42\">Personnel: comprises the project team; labour, managers, supervisors etc. that execute the project<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-43\">Control: This is a procedure for monitoring and evaluating progress and performance of a project<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-44\">Deliverables: these are tangible or intangible product or items to be provided or delivered at a specified time during a project or at the end of the project<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-45\">Risk and problem analysis: this is the anticipated assessment of uncertainties, problems and potential difficulties that might increase the risk of the project’s completion, delay or failure of project thus threatening project success<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-6-9\">Project Evaluation<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-46\">Project evaluation is defined as the systematic assessment of the performance, effectiveness and outcome of a project against predetermined objective.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-47\">It helps determine whether a project is achieving its objectives. Evaluation spans through the life span of a project.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-48\">The first thing to consider is the baseline data (a benchmark that measures a project’s progress against its original plan stated in the objective).<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-49\">Both the baseline data and endline data are compared and evaluated to know the extent to which the objectives were achieved<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-50\">it helps to know if a project should be continued, adjusted or discontinued<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-6-10\">Project evaluation methods<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-6-p0-51\">There are two methods of evaluating a project<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-52\">The traditional method: this is evaluated using the following approaches<\/li><\/ol><ul><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-53\">A. payback period (PBP)<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-54\">B. accounting rate of return (ARR)<\/li><\/ul><ol start=\"2\"><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-55\">The scientific method: this is evaluated using the following approaches<\/li><\/ol><ul><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-56\">A. net present value (NPV)<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-57\">B. internal rate of return (IRR)<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-58\">Any method chosen or used can serve its purpose depending on the objective<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-59\">Payback period: this measures the time it will take to recover the project investment or initial outlay.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-60\">It is the simplest and widely used model.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-61\">It is used to evaluate project that involve high uncertainty.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-62\">It can be used to eliminate unusually risky projects (those with lengthy payback periods). Shorter paybacks are desirable.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-63\">Though, it strongly emphasises cash flows, a major limitation is that it ignores the time value of money and profitability<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-64\">Accounting rate of return (ARR): this measures the profitability of an investment by comparing the average annual profit to the average investment.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-65\">where one project is involved, accept the project whose calculated ARR is higher than the company target, otherwise reject.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-66\">For two projects, it is recommended that the project with the highest ARR is accepted provided it is higher than the company or industry targeted ARR<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-67\">Net present value (NPV): This is a measure used to determine the financial viability of a project by using the desired rate of return (discount rate) to compute the present value of all net cash inflows.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-68\">For single project, if the NPV is positive, the project is accepted and if the NPV is negative, the project is rejected.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-69\">For mutually exclusive projects, accept project with the highest positive NPV.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-70\">NPV results are more realistic than PBP because it considers time value of<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-71\">money, cash flows and profitability<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-72\">Internal rate of return (IRR): It measures the rate of discount at which the project total present value of return equals the total present value of investment. That is, the discounted rate at which the NPV of the project is zero.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-73\">For single project, accept project if the calculated IRR is higher than the company cost of capital, otherwise reject.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-74\">For mutually exclusive projects, accept project with the highest IRR provided it is higher than the company cost of capital.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-6-11\">Project Appraisal<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-75\">Project appraisal is the process of analyzing the technical feasibility, economic viability, and potential impact of a proposed project<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-76\">Project appraisal is defined as the process of critical examination and analysis of the project proposal in totality.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-77\">Appraisal is used to determine how feasible or viable a project is. Where several projects are being considered, appraisal helps determine which one to consider and which not to<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-6-12\">Project Appraisal Process<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-6-p0-78\">Project appraisal include<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-79\">Market appraisal: It focuses on demand projections, adequacy of marketing infrastructure and competence of the key marketing personnel. Factors to consider are – product specifications, pricing, channels of distribution, trade practices, threat of substitutes, domestic and international competition, opportunities for exports etc.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-80\">Technical appraisal: Technical feasibility analysis is the systematic gathering and analysis of the data pertaining to the technical inputs required. The availability of the raw materials, equipment, hard/software, power, sewerage services, transportation facility, skilled man power, engineering facilities, maintenance, local people etc., depending on the type of project are coming under technical analysis. It determines whether the technical parameters are soundly conceived, realistic and technically feasible.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-81\">Economic appraisal: this entails how far the project contributes to the development of the sector, industrial development, social development, maximizing the growth of employment, etc.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-82\">Financial appraisal: it examines the viability of the project from financial considerations and indicates the return on the investments. used to determine whether the financial costs and returns are properly estimated and whether the project is financially viable. Some of the commonly used techniques for financial analysis are as follows; profitability index, cost-benefit analysis, cash flow, NPV and IRR<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-83\">Managerial appraisal: it focuses on promoters, organization structure, managerial personnel, and HR management.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-84\">Environmental appraisal: evaluates impact on land use and micro-environment, commitment of natural resources, and government policy. It is used to assess any detrimental environmental impacts and how to minimise the impacts. Environmental appraisal is concerned with the impact of environment on the project. It takes into consideration water, air, land, sound, geographical location etc.<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-6-13\">Project Control<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-6-p0-85\">Project Control is the process of comparing actual project performance against planned objective to identify possible deviations, evaluate possible alternative courses of actions, and take appropriate corrective action.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-6-p0-86\">Controlling is a responsibility to ensure the actions of the project team contribute toward the project goals; the project manager must establish standards for performance, measure performance and compare it with the established standards; detect variations and make the necessary corrections. This responsibility ensures that the project is on track.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-6-p0-87\">The project control steps for measuring and evaluating project performance are:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-88\">Setting a baseline plan.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-89\">Measuring progress and performance.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-90\">Comparing result against actual plan.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-91\">Taking action (where deviation exist).<\/li><\/ol><ul><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-92\"><i><b>Setting a Baseline Plan<\/b><\/i><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-93\">The baseline plan provides us with the elements for measuring performance. The baseline is derived from the cost and duration information found in the work breakdown structure (WBS) database and time-sequence data from the network and resource scheduling decisions. From the WBS the project resource schedule is used to time-phase all work, resources, and budgets into a baseline plan.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-94\"><i><b>Measuring Progress and Performance<\/b><\/i><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-95\">Time and budgets are quantitative measures of performance that readily fit into the integrated information system. Qualitative measures such as meeting customer technical specifications and product function are most frequently determined by on-site inspection or actual use.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-96\">Measurement of time performance is done to reflect the critical path early, on schedule, or late; the slack<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-97\">Measuring performance against budget (e.g., money, units in place, labor hours) Earned value is necessary to provide a realistic estimate of performance against a time-phased budget. <b>Earned value<\/b> (EV) is defined as the budgeted cost of the work performed.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-98\"><i><b>Comparing Result against Actual Plan<\/b><\/i><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-99\">Because plans seldom materialize as expected, it becomes imperative to measure deviations from plan to determine if action is necessary.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-100\">Periodic monitoring and measuring the status of the project allow for comparisons of actual versus expected plans. It is crucial that the timing of status reports be frequent enough to allow for early detection of variations from plan and early correction of causes.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-101\">Usually status reports should take place every <b>one to four weeks<\/b> to be useful and allow for <b>proactive<\/b> correction.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-102\"><i><b>Taking Action<\/b><\/i><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-103\">If deviations from plans are significant, corrective action will be needed to bring the project back in line with the original or revised plan. In some cases, conditions or scope can change, which, in turn, will require a change in the baseline plan to recognize new information.<\/li><\/ul><h3 id=\"bus-block-6-14\">Project Control Process<\/h3><ul><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-104\">Project control process involves regularly gathering data on project performance, comparing actual performance to planned performance and taking corrective actions if actual performance is behind planned performance.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-105\">This process must occur regularly throughout the project. The shorter the reporting period, the better the chances of identifying problems early and taking effective corrective action<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-106\">Steps in control process begins with establishing a baseline plan that shows how the project scope(tasks) will be accomplished on time (schedule) and within budget (resources, costs). Once the baseline plan is agreed upon by the customer and the contractor or project team, the project can start.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-107\">A regular reporting period should be established for comparing actual progress with planned progress. Reporting may be daily, weekly, biweekly or monthly depending on the complexity or overall duration of the project.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-108\">If a project is expected to have an overall duration of a month, the reporting period might be as short as a day. On the other hand, if a project is expected to run five years, the reporting period might be a month<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-109\">During each reporting period, two kinds of data or information need to be collected<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-110\">1. Data on actual performance: this includes:<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-111\">A. the actual time that activities were started and finished<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-112\">B. the actual costs expended and committed<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-113\">2. Information on any changes to the project scope, schedule and budget: changes could be initiated by the customer or project team or could be as a result of an unanticipated occurrence such as natural disaster, labor strike or resignation of a key project team member<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-114\">Once changes are incorporated into the plan and agreed on by the customer, a new baseline plan has to be established. The scope, schedule, and budget of the new baseline plan may be different from those of the original baseline plan<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p0-115\">It is crucial that the data and information be collected in a timely manner and used to calculate an updated project schedule and budget.<\/li><\/ul>", "toc": [{"id": "bus-block-6-0", "t": "What Project entails", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-6-1", "t": "Attributes/Characteristics of a project", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-6-2", "t": "Classification of a project", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-6-3", "t": "What is management?", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-6-4", "t": "Project Management", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-6-5", "t": "Objective of Project Management", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-6-6", "t": "Project Life Cycle", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-6-7", "t": "Stages of Project Management", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-6-8", "t": "Components of Project Management", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-6-9", "t": "Project Evaluation", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-6-10", "t": "Project evaluation methods", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-6-11", "t": "Project Appraisal", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-6-12", "t": "Project Appraisal Process", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-6-13", "t": "Project Control", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-6-14", "t": "Project Control Process", "lv": 3}]}, {"label": "Lecture 1: Project Management", "note": "22nd May 2026", "src": "6.2 Lecture 1 - 22nd May (transcript).md", "html": "<h4 id=\"bus-block-6-15\">Page 1<\/h4><h3 id=\"bus-block-6-16\">Project Management<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-6-p15-1\">A project is an undertaking that involves a sequence of tasks which must be completed at a specific period of time to ascertain a certain outcome.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-6-p15-2\"><b>Characteristics of a project<\/b><\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-3\">All projects have beginning and end points.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-4\">The project must be clearly defined and have all the goals and aims.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-5\">It must involve sets of activities that are related.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-6\">The project has to [be] unique and different from other activities in an organization.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-7\">It involves manpower.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-8\">It involves risk and uncertainty.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-9\">It is time-consuming.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-10\">It involves non-repetitive tasks.<\/li><\/ol><p id=\"bus-block-6-p15-11\"><b>Classification of a project<\/b><\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-12\"><b>Compliance or emergency projects:<\/b> They are called must-do projects because they are projects that must be taken up.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-13\"><b>Operational projects:<\/b> These projects are needed to support current operations and are as well designed to improve the …<\/li><\/ol><h4 id=\"bus-block-6-17\">Page 2<\/h4><p id=\"bus-block-6-p15-14\">… efficiency of delivery systems, reduce product cost and improve performance, e.g. total quality management project.<\/p><ol start=\"3\"><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-15\"><b>Strategic projects:<\/b> These projects directly support the organization's long run and are often directed towards improving the revenue or market shares of the organization.<\/li><\/ol><p id=\"bus-block-6-p15-16\"><b>Management<\/b> can be defined as the process of planning, organizing, directing, and controlling of organizational resources. These resources could be in [the] form of man, material, information, machine, money, idea, etc., in order to effectively and efficiently achieve organizational goals and objectives.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-6-p15-17\"><b>Project management<\/b> can be defined as the process of planning, organizing, directing, and controlling of resources to meet technical, cost and time constraints of a project.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-6-p15-18\"><b>Objectives of project management<\/b><\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-19\">To determine the cost and duration of a project.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-20\">To determine the level of resources that would be needed.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-21\">It assists in allocating work and monitoring progress.<\/li><\/ol><h4 id=\"bus-block-6-18\">Page 3<\/h4><ol start=\"4\"><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-22\">It helps to reduce or eliminate associated risks or uncertainties.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-23\">To effectively and efficiently utilize the available resources.<\/li><\/ol><p id=\"bus-block-6-p15-24\"><b>Project Lifecycle<\/b><\/p><p id=\"bus-block-6-p15-25\">There are five stages:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-26\"><b>Initiation stage:<\/b> This is the starting point of the project.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-27\">Planning stage<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-28\">Execution stage<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-29\">Monitoring and control<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-30\">Close out (closure)<\/li><\/ol><p id=\"bus-block-6-p15-31\"><b>Stages of project management<\/b><\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-32\">Project planning<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-33\">Project scheduling<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-34\">Project controlling<\/li><\/ol><ul><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-35\"><b>Project planning<\/b> provides details of work, resources and time needed to complete a project, as well as cost involved [and] the budget allocated to complete a project.<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-6-p15-36\">Steps involved in project planning:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-37\">Set up the objectives of the project.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-38\">List the various tasks or jobs that must be performed, which …<\/li><\/ol><h4 id=\"bus-block-6-19\">Page 4<\/h4><p id=\"bus-block-6-p15-39\">… will require developing a work breakdown structure.<\/p><ol start=\"3\"><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-40\">Estimate the cost and the weeks associated with the project.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-41\">Develop a budget for the resources.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-42\">Determine the duration of the project.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-43\">Develop work-performance criteria.<\/li><\/ol><ul><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-44\"><b>Project scheduling<\/b> is the preparation of [a] timetable for implementing project activities and computing of resources required at different stages of the project. It also includes identifying critical tasks and resources so that the entire project proceeds in a logical manner according to precedence.<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-6-p15-45\">Steps involved in project scheduling:<\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-46\">Identify all the people involved in the tasks.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-47\">Estimate the duration of the project.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-48\">Identify the critical parts of the project and give attention to them.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-49\">Specify the interrelationships of each activity.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-50\">Develop a network diagram.<\/li><\/ol><ul><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-51\"><b>Project controlling<\/b> is the process of monitoring a project to ensure it progresses towards successful completion.<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-6-p15-52\">Steps involved in project controlling:<\/p><h4 id=\"bus-block-6-20\">Page 5<\/h4><ol><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-53\">Give periodical reports.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-54\">Review the report in relation to the project schedule.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-55\">Analyze the status of the project.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-56\">Take up managerial decisions.<\/li><\/ol><p id=\"bus-block-6-p15-57\"><b>Components of project management<\/b><\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-58\"><b>Objectives of the project:<\/b> It is a detailed statement of what would be accomplished in a project and how it may achieve the goals and meet strategic plans. It also involves the estimation of time, cost, and returns.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-59\"><b>Project scope:<\/b> A project scope defines the boundaries of a project. It outlines the tasks, goals, deliverables, costs. It specifies the work that needs to be done to complete a project.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-60\"><b>Contract requirement:<\/b> It is a general structure of reporting and performance responsibility which involves detailed lists of staff, suppliers, subcontractors, managerial and reporting requirements, as well as a projected organizational structure.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-61\"><b>Project schedule:<\/b> It is a timetable that specifies the list of all major events and activities which must be performed from the start to the finish.<\/li><\/ol><h4 id=\"bus-block-6-21\">Page 6<\/h4><ol start=\"5\"><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-62\"><b>Resources:<\/b> It is the overall project budget for all resource requirements and procedures for budget rate control.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-63\"><b>Personnel:<\/b><\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-64\"><b>Control:<\/b> It is the procedure for monitoring and evaluating the progress and performance of a project.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-65\"><b>Deliverables:<\/b> They are tangible or intangible products that are provided/delivered at a specified time during a project or at the end of a project.<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p15-66\"><b>Risk and problem analysis:<\/b> It is the anticipated assessment of uncertainties, problems and potential difficulties that might increase the risk of a project, its completion, cause delay or failure of a project.<\/li><\/ol><blockquote id=\"bus-block-6-p15-67\">★ Work breakdown structure may not contain the timelines for specific activities but Gantt chart does.<\/blockquote><h4 id=\"bus-block-6-22\">Page 7 — Diagram (red ink)<\/h4><p id=\"bus-block-6-p15-68\"><b>Project planning / Project scheduling / Project control flow<\/b><\/p><pre id=\"bus-block-6-p15-69\"> Project planning\n |\n +-------+\n | WBS |\n +-------+\n Project |\n scheduling v\n +-------------------+ +-----------------+\n | Network of |&lt;----| Deviations |\n | activities | | from plan |\n +-------------------+ +-----------------+\n | ^\n v | Project\n +-------------------+ +-----------------+ control\n | Gantt chart | | Actual tasks / |\n +-------------------+ | performance |\n | | data |\n v +-----------------+\n +-------------------+ ^\n | Scheduling / | |\n | Application of | +-----------------+\n | resources with | | Periodic Cost |\n | respect to time | | Reports |\n | and cost | +-----------------+\n +-------------------+ ^\n | |\n +--&gt; Implementation ----+\n of Plan<\/pre><h4 id=\"bus-block-6-23\">Page 8 — Whiteboard photo<\/h4><p id=\"bus-block-6-p15-70\">Photograph of the lecture whiteboard reproducing the same diagram, titled <b>&quot;PROJECT MANAGEMENT — BUS 440&quot;<\/b>, with the same boxes: Project planning → WBS → Project scheduling → Network of Activities → Gantt chart → Scheduling/Application; and on the right, Project Control → Actual tasks/Performance data → Deviation from plan, Periodic Cost Reports → Implementation of Plan.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-6-p15-71\">Partially visible at the left edge (from an earlier board): &quot;(ARR)&quot;, &quot;(IRR)&quot;, &quot;Initial Investment&quot;, &quot;Cash flow&quot;, &quot;…0,000&quot;, &quot;…0,000&quot;.<\/p>", "toc": [{"id": "bus-block-6-15", "t": "Page 1", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-6-16", "t": "Project Management", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-6-17", "t": "Page 2", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-6-18", "t": "Page 3", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-6-19", "t": "Page 4", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-6-20", "t": "Page 5", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-6-21", "t": "Page 6", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-6-22", "t": "Page 7 — Diagram (red ink)", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-6-23", "t": "Page 8 — Whiteboard photo", "lv": 4}]}, {"label": "Lecture 3: Control Charts, Project Evaluation, Appraisal & Control", "note": "L3 — July 24", "src": "6.3 Lecture 3 - July 24 (transcript).md", "html": "<h4 id=\"bus-block-6-24\">Page 1<\/h4><p id=\"bus-block-6-p24-1\"><b>Illustration<\/b><\/p><p id=\"bus-block-6-p24-2\">25 engine mounts are sampled each day and found to have an average width of 2 inches with a standard measure of 0.1 inch. What are the control limits?<\/p><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-6-p24-3\"><span class=\"tex\">\\bar{X} \\pm 3\\left(\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right)<\/span><\/div><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-6-p24-4\"><span class=\"tex\">n = 2.5 \\quad \\bar{X} = 2 \\quad \\sigma = 0.1<\/span><\/div><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-6-p24-5\"><span class=\"tex\">\\mathrm{UCL} = \\bar{X} + 3\\left(\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right) = 2 + 3\\left(\\frac{0.1}{\\sqrt{2.5}}\\right) = 2.06<\/span><\/div><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-6-p24-6\"><span class=\"tex\">\\mathrm{LCL} = \\bar{X} − 3\\left(\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right) = 2 - 3\\left(\\frac{0.1}{\\sqrt{2.5}}\\right) = 1.94<\/span><\/div><pre id=\"bus-block-6-p24-7\">UCL ├────────────────────── 2.06\nCL ├────────────────────── 2\nLCL ├────────────────────── 1.94\n └──────────────────────<\/pre><blockquote id=\"bus-block-6-p24-8\">Note (as written): n is recorded as 2.5 rather than 25, and √2.5 is used in the working — the arithmetic in the notes follows from that.<\/blockquote><h4 id=\"bus-block-6-25\">Page 2<\/h4><p id=\"bus-block-6-p24-9\">Project B has an initial investment of ₦400,000 and a projected cash flow of ₦110,000 for 5 years. What is the PBP and can both projects be accepted?<\/p><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-6-p24-10\"><span class=\"tex\">\\text{Project A: } \\mathrm{PBP} = \\frac{₦700{,}000}{₦225{,}000} = 3.18\\;\\text{years}<\/span><\/div><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-6-p24-11\"><span class=\"tex\">\\text{Project B: } \\mathrm{PBP} = \\frac{₦400{,}000}{₦110{,}000} = 3.6\\;\\text{years}<\/span><\/div><h3 id=\"bus-block-6-26\">Accounting Rate of Return<\/h3><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-6-p24-12\"><span class=\"tex\">\\mathrm{ARR} = \\frac{\\text{annual cash flow or savings}}{\\text{initial investment or cost}}<\/span><\/div><p id=\"bus-block-6-p24-13\"><b>Example 1<\/b><\/p><p id=\"bus-block-6-p24-14\">A project needs ₦40,000 and it will generate equal cash flows of ₦10,000 for 10 years. What is the ARR?<\/p><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-6-p24-15\"><span class=\"tex\">\\mathrm{ARR} = \\frac{10{,}000}{40{,}000} = 0.25 \\times 100\\% = 25\\%<\/span><\/div><h4 id=\"bus-block-6-27\">Page 3<\/h4><p id=\"bus-block-6-p24-16\"><b>Example 2<\/b><\/p><p id=\"bus-block-6-p24-17\">Project A has ₦20,000 as initial investment, and an annual cash flow of ₦10,000, while Project B has ₦30,000 as initial investment and an annual cash flow of ₦10,000. What is the ARR of each project, and which project will you recommend and why?<\/p><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-6-p24-18\"><span class=\"tex\">\\text{Project A: } \\mathrm{ARR} = \\frac{10{,}000}{20{,}000} = 0.5 \\times 100\\% = 50\\%<\/span><\/div><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-6-p24-19\"><span class=\"tex\">\\text{Project B: } \\mathrm{ARR} = \\frac{10{,}000}{30{,}000} = 0.33 \\times 100\\% = 33\\%<\/span><\/div><p id=\"bus-block-6-p24-20\">Project A should be recommended because it has a higher ARR, which makes it more feasible.<\/p><h4 id=\"bus-block-6-28\">Page 4<\/h4><h3 id=\"bus-block-6-29\">Net Present Value<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-6-p24-21\"><b>Decision rule<\/b><\/p><ul><li id=\"bus-block-6-p24-22\">NPV &gt; 0 → Accept project<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p24-23\">NPV &lt; 0 → Reject project<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p24-24\">NPV = 0 → Accept/Reject depending on other factors<\/li><\/ul><p id=\"bus-block-6-p24-25\"><b>Example 1<\/b><\/p><p id=\"bus-block-6-p24-26\">The sum of ₦40,000 is invested in an IT project. Given a cost of capital at 8% and series of projected cash inflows, what is NPV?<\/p><table><tr><th>(40,000)<\/th><th>Cash inflow<\/th><th>Discounted Cash Flow<\/th><th>PV<\/th><\/tr><tr><td>Yr 1<\/td><td>70,000<\/td><td>0.926<\/td><td>64,820<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Yr 2<\/td><td>120,000<\/td><td>0.857<\/td><td>102,840<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Yr 3<\/td><td>140,000<\/td><td>0.794<\/td><td>111,160<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Yr 4<\/td><td>140,000<\/td><td>0.735<\/td><td>102,900<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Yr 5<\/td><td>40,000<\/td><td>0.681<\/td><td>27,240<\/td><\/tr><\/table><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-6-p24-27\"><span class=\"tex\">\\mathrm{NPV} = 408{,}960 − 40{,}000 = ₦368{,}960<\/span><\/div><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-6-p24-28\"><span class=\"tex\">\\mathrm{FV} = \\mathrm{PV}(1 + r)^n<\/span><\/div><div class=\"eqn\" id=\"bus-block-6-p24-29\"><span class=\"tex\">\\mathrm{PV} = \\frac{\\mathrm{FV}}{(1 + r)^n} = \\mathrm{FV}(1 + r)^{−n}<\/span><\/div><h4 id=\"bus-block-6-30\">Page 5<\/h4><h3 id=\"bus-block-6-31\">Project Appraisal<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-6-p24-30\">It is the process of analysing technical feasibility, economic viability and potential impact of a project.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-6-p24-31\"><b>Methods of Appraisal (or areas?)<\/b><\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-6-p24-32\">Market Appraisal<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p24-33\">Technical Appraisal<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p24-34\">Industrial Appraisal<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p24-35\">Managerial Appraisal<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p24-36\">Economic Appraisal<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p24-37\">Environmental Appraisal<\/li><\/ol><h3 id=\"bus-block-6-32\">Project Control<\/h3><p id=\"bus-block-6-p24-38\">Project Control is a process of comparing actual performance against plan to identify deviations, evaluate alternative courses of action and take appropriate corrective actions.<\/p><p id=\"bus-block-6-p24-39\"><b>Steps:<\/b><\/p><ol><li id=\"bus-block-6-p24-40\">Set up baseline plan<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p24-41\">Measure performance and progress<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p24-42\">Compare results against actual plans<\/li><li id=\"bus-block-6-p24-43\">Take action<\/li><\/ol>", "toc": [{"id": "bus-block-6-24", "t": "Page 1", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-6-25", "t": "Page 2", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-6-26", "t": "Accounting Rate of Return", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-6-27", "t": "Page 3", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-6-28", "t": "Page 4", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-6-29", "t": "Net Present Value", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-6-30", "t": "Page 5", "lv": 4}, {"id": "bus-block-6-31", "t": "Project Appraisal", "lv": 3}, {"id": "bus-block-6-32", "t": "Project Control", "lv": 3}]}]}};
